export default {"format":"cadcraft-help","version":1,"app":"modelcraft","name":"ModelCraft","common":"basics","sections":{"start":"Getting started","tasks":"Walkthroughs","switching":"Coming from other software","reference":"Reference","trouble":"Troubleshooting and limits"},"variants":{"os":{"mac":"Mac","windows":"Windows","linux":"Linux"},"browser":{"chromium":"Chrome or Edge","firefox":"Firefox","safari":"Safari"},"shell":{"web":"In the browser","desktop":"Desktop app"},"net":{"online":"Online","offline":"Offline"},"input":{"mouse":"Mouse","touch":"Touch"}},"topics":[{"slug":"whats-new","scope":"modelcraft","title":"What's new in 0.4","summary":"Arcs, curves, offset, follow me, paint, tags, section planes, solid tools, DWG and DXF export, images and PDFs of the view, touch, and a Spanish draft.","section":"start","order":5,"keywords":["whats new","what's new","release notes","changelog","changes","0.4","version","new features","arc","curve","offset","follow me","paint","tags","sections","solids","dwg export","dxf export","image","pdf","touch","spanish","language"],"headings":[{"id":"new","text":"New","level":2},{"id":"drawing","text":"Drawing","level":3},{"id":"editing","text":"Editing","level":3},{"id":"organizing-and-viewing","text":"Organizing and viewing","level":3},{"id":"components","text":"Components","level":3},{"id":"files","text":"Files","level":3},{"id":"everywhere","text":"Everywhere","level":3},{"id":"improved","text":"Improved","level":2},{"id":"fixed","text":"Fixed","level":2},{"id":"files-and-compatibility","text":"Files and compatibility","level":2},{"id":"not-in-this-version-yet","text":"Not in this version yet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThis is what changed in ModelCraft 0.4, compared with 0.3, the version Try ran before.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"new\"\u003ENew\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"drawing\"\u003EDrawing\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EArcs.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArc\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"A\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) draws an arc from two ends and a bulge. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArc center\u003C/span\u003E draws one from a center, a radius and an angle. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/draw-faces#draw-an-arc\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/draw-faces\" data-hash=\"draw-an-arc\"\u003EDraw an arc\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EArcs, circles and polygons are curves.\u003C/strong\u003E One click selects the whole curve, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E shows its radius. Type a new radius to resize a flat one. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/draw-faces#arcs-circles-and-polygons-are-curves\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/draw-faces\" data-hash=\"arcs-circles-and-polygons-are-curves\"\u003EArcs, circles and polygons are curves\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESmooth curves.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECurve\u003C/span\u003E draws a smooth curve through the points you click, in 3D. Select it and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit points\u003C/span\u003E to reshape it. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/draw-faces#draw-a-smooth-curve\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/draw-faces\" data-hash=\"draw-a-smooth-curve\"\u003EDraw a smooth curve\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOffset\u003C/strong\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E). Copy a face&#039;s outline inside or outside it, or offset a chain of edges. Double-click a face to repeat the last distance. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/draw-faces#offset-a-border-inside-or-outside-a-face\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/draw-faces\" data-hash=\"offset-a-border-inside-or-outside-a-face\"\u003EOffset\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFollow me.\u003C/strong\u003E Sweep a face along a path of lines, arcs or a curve: pipes, cables, handrails, moldings. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/follow-me\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/follow-me\"\u003EFollow me\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E3D Text\u003C/strong\u003E in the Draw group. Lettering as flat faces or solid letters, placed on the face you click. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/text3d\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/text3d\"\u003E3D text\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETerrain.\u003C/strong\u003E Make ground from contour lines or as a grid, and raise or lower it with a soft brush. It is in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/terrain\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/terrain\"\u003ETerrain\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EProtractor.\u003C/strong\u003E Place a guide line at a typed or snapped angle. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/guides-measure#a-guide-at-an-angle-protractor\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/guides-measure\" data-hash=\"a-guide-at-an-angle-protractor\"\u003EA guide at an angle\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENew snap: the center of a face.\u003C/strong\u003E See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/inference#drawing-on-a-face-from-a-point\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/inference\" data-hash=\"drawing-on-a-face-from-a-point\"\u003EInference and snapping\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"editing\"\u003EEditing\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPaint\u003C/strong\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"B\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) and a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EColors\u003C/span\u003E panel. Give faces, groups and components solid colors. Colors are saved in the file and kept in GLB, Collada, OBJ, DWG and DXF exports. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/paint\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/paint\"\u003EPaint with colors\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESolid tools.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESolids\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOrganize\u003C/span\u003E group has \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnion\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESubtract\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIntersect\u003C/span\u003E for closed groups and components. When a group is not a solid, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E says why, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow problems\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFix\u003C/span\u003E help. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/solids\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/solids\"\u003ESolid tools\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIntersect faces.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIntersect with model\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIntersect with selection\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E draw the lines where shapes cut through each other. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/intersect\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/intersect\"\u003EIntersect faces\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFlip\u003C/strong\u003E along red, green or blue, or make a flipped copy beside the original, from \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/rotate-scale#flip\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/rotate-scale\" data-hash=\"flip\"\u003EFlip\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EScale grips.\u003C/strong\u003E With something selected, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale\u003C/span\u003E shows grips on its box. Stretch along one or two directions, or type one factor per axis, such as \u003Ccode\u003E2,1,0.5\u003C/code\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/rotate-scale#scale\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/rotate-scale\" data-hash=\"scale\"\u003ERotate and scale\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESoften edges by angle\u003C/strong\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E. Curved shapes then look like one smooth surface, in ModelCraft and in GLB and OBJ exports. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/soften\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/soften\"\u003ESoften and smooth edges\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMove and turn the drawing axes\u003C/strong\u003E with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlace axes\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAlign axes to face\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset axes\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/axes\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/axes\"\u003EMove and turn the drawing axes\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDimensions on arcs, circles and angles.\u003C/strong\u003E Click an arc for its radius, a circle for its diameter, or two edges that meet for the angle. Type a new radius or diameter to resize the curve. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/dimensions#radius-diameter-and-angle\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/dimensions\" data-hash=\"radius-diameter-and-angle\"\u003ERadius, diameter and angle\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"organizing-and-viewing\"\u003EOrganizing and viewing\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETags.\u003C/strong\u003E Give groups and components a tag in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E, then hide or show everything with that tag in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETags\u003C/span\u003E panel. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/tags\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/tags\"\u003ETags\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EHide, Isolate and Unhide all\u003C/strong\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E. Clicks and snaps go past hidden things. With Blender-style keys: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"H\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift H\"\u003E⇧H\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+H\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option H\"\u003E⌥H\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt+H\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/hide-isolate\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/hide-isolate\"\u003EHide, isolate and unhide\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESection planes.\u003C/strong\u003E Click a face with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESection\u003C/span\u003E to cut the model open. Slide, reverse or turn off the cut in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E. Cut solids are filled. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/sections\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/sections\"\u003ESection planes\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOutliner.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFind by name\u003C/span\u003E, an eye on every row, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F2\"\u003EF2\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF2\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to rename, and drag a group onto another to move it inside. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/workspace\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/workspace\"\u003EThe workspace\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPresentation view\u003C/strong\u003E (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EViews\u003C/span\u003E menu, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift P\"\u003E⇧P\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+P\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E). Only the model shows: no axes, guides, snapping marks or selection. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/styles-shadows#presentation-view\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/styles-shadows\" data-hash=\"presentation-view\"\u003EPresentation view\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EScenes remember what is hidden:\u003C/strong\u003E groups, tags and the section cut. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport animation\u003C/span\u003E saves the scenes as a WebM video. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/scenes#export-an-animation\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/scenes\" data-hash=\"export-an-animation\"\u003ESave views as scenes\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"components\"\u003EComponents\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EStarter library.\u003C/strong\u003E In the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EComponents\u003C/span\u003E panel, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStarter library\u003C/span\u003E has 34 ready-made things: doors and windows, furniture, kitchen and bath, stairs, trees, a person and a car. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/starter\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/starter\"\u003EStarter library\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EComponents that stretch.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStretch this copy…\u003C/span\u003E makes one copy longer, wider or taller while its parts keep their sizes: legs stay put, shelves spread out, railing posts repeat. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/stretch\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/stretch\"\u003EComponents that stretch\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"files\"\u003EFiles\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDWG and DXF export.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDWG for CAD (.dwg, 2004)\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDXF for CAD (.dxf, R12)\u003C/span\u003E. Faces become 3D faces, and groups and components become blocks. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/formats#dwg-and-dxf-export-dwg-dxf\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/formats\" data-hash=\"dwg-and-dxf-export-dwg-dxf\"\u003EDWG and DXF export\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESave the 3D view as an image.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImage of the view (.png)\u003C/span\u003E, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImage at twice the size (.png)\u003C/span\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/export-model#save-the-view-as-an-image\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/export-model\" data-hash=\"save-the-view-as-an-image\"\u003ESave the view as an image\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPrint the view, or save it as a PDF.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPDF of the view (.pdf)\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPrint the view…\u003C/span\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/export-model#print-the-view-or-save-it-as-a-pdf\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/export-model\" data-hash=\"print-the-view-or-save-it-as-a-pdf\"\u003EPrint the view\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIFC storeys.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIFC storeys…\u003C/span\u003E puts each group on its floor in the IFC file. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/formats#ifc-ifc\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/formats\" data-hash=\"ifc-ifc\"\u003EIFC\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERecover your work.\u003C/strong\u003E If the tab or browser closes before you save, ModelCraft asks &quot;Recover your work?&quot; the next time. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/files#if-the-browser-closes-before-you-save\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/files\" data-hash=\"if-the-browser-closes-before-you-save\"\u003EIf the browser closes before you save\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOpen in ModelCraft from CadCraft.\u003C/strong\u003E In the CadCraft drafting app, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen in ModelCraft\u003C/span\u003E hands the drawing over in a new tab. It arrives as a site plan. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/files#from-cadcraft-open-in-modelcraft\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/files\" data-hash=\"from-cadcraft-open-in-modelcraft\"\u003EFrom CadCraft\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERecent files\u003C/strong\u003E on the start card, in browsers that can reopen a file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"everywhere\"\u003EEverywhere\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETouch screens.\u003C/strong\u003E Tap to click, hold still to finish, two fingers to orbit and pinch to zoom, three fingers to pan. Buttons are bigger, and a bar offers \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDone\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECancel\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/orbit-pan-zoom#on-a-touch-screen\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/orbit-pan-zoom\" data-hash=\"on-a-touch-screen\"\u003EOn a touch screen\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELanguage.\u003C/strong\u003E Every word on screen now comes from a language file. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELanguage\u003C/span\u003E offers Español (México). It is a machine-made draft that no one has reviewed yet, so some words may be wrong. The help stays in English.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFind a tool or setting.\u003C/strong\u003E Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command K\"\u003E⌘K\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+K\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFind\u003C/span\u003E in the app bar, and type a few letters. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/find-tools\" data-topic=\"basics/find-tools\"\u003EFind a tool or setting\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"improved\"\u003EImproved\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDWG and DXF site plans bring in more.\u003C/strong\u003E Text (as single-stroke lettering), splines, ellipses, dimensions, hatches (as faces with their holes) and 3D faces now come in. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/site-plan\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/site-plan\"\u003EModel on a site plan\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBig DWG files are read in the background,\u003C/strong\u003E so the page stays responsive.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMore panel windows.\u003C/strong\u003E Panels can go to more than one window, and you can drag them between windows. A floating panel can be resized by touch. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/arrange-panels#more-than-one-panels-window\" data-topic=\"basics/arrange-panels\" data-hash=\"more-than-one-panels-window\"\u003EMore than one panels window\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe view cube follows the app&#039;s language.\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"fixed\"\u003EFixed\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECircles and rectangles on a face, started from a point.\u003C/strong\u003E A circle started on the midpoint of a pushed box&#039;s end face used to fall flat on the ground. Now a rectangle, circle or polygon started on a corner, a midpoint or an edge is drawn on the face under the pointer. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/inference#drawing-on-a-face-from-a-point\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/inference\" data-hash=\"drawing-on-a-face-from-a-point\"\u003EDrawing on a face from a point\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"files-and-compatibility\"\u003EFiles and compatibility\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENative files:\u003C/strong\u003E a 0.4 \u003Ccode\u003E.modelcraft\u003C/code\u003E file keeps curves, colors, hidden things, tags, section planes, axes, storeys and stretch rules. ModelCraft 0.3 can open it but leaves those out. If 0.3 saves it again, they are gone.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDWG export\u003C/strong\u003E writes the 2004 format. \u003Cstrong\u003EDXF export\u003C/strong\u003E writes R12. Dimensions and guides are not exported.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"not-in-this-version-yet\"\u003ENot in this version yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETextures and image materials. Paint uses solid colors.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETrim, Split and Outer shell.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMore than one section cut at a time.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDWG and DXF layers as tags.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003EWhat is not built yet\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"This is what changed in ModelCraft 0.4, compared with 0.3, the version Try ran before. New Drawing Arcs. Arc (A) draws an arc from two ends and a bulge. Arc center draws one from a center, a radius and an angle. See Draw an arc. Arcs, circles and polygons are curves. One click selects the whole curve, and Entity info shows its radius. Type a new radius to resize a flat one. See Arcs, circles and polygons are curves. Smooth curves. Curve draws a smooth curve through the points you click, in 3D. Select it and click Edit points to reshape it. See Draw a smooth curve. Offset (F). Copy a face's outline inside or outside it, or offset a chain of edges. Double-click a face to repeat the last distance. See Offset. Follow me. Sweep a face along a path of lines, arcs or a curve: pipes, cables, handrails, moldings. See Follow me. 3D Text in the Draw group. Lettering as flat faces or solid letters, placed on the face you click. See 3D text. Terrain. Make ground from contour lines or as a grid, and raise or lower it with a soft brush. It is in Edit › More. See Terrain. Protractor. Place a guide line at a typed or snapped angle. See A guide at an angle. New snap: the center of a face. See Inference and snapping. Editing Paint (B) and a Colors panel. Give faces, groups and components solid colors. Colors are saved in the file and kept in GLB, Collada, OBJ, DWG and DXF exports. See Paint with colors. Solid tools. Solids in the Organize group has Union, Subtract and Intersect for closed groups and components. When a group is not a solid, Entity info says why, and Show problems and Fix help. See Solid tools. Intersect faces. Intersect with model and Intersect with selection in Edit › More draw the lines where shapes cut through each other. See Intersect faces. Flip along red, green or blue, or make a flipped copy beside the original, from Edit › More. See Flip. Scale grips. With something selected, Scale shows grips on its box. Stretch along one or two directions, or type one factor per axis, such as 2,1,0.5. See Rotate and scale. Soften edges by angle in Entity info. Curved shapes then look like one smooth surface, in ModelCraft and in GLB and OBJ exports. See Soften and smooth edges. Move and turn the drawing axes with Place axes, Align axes to face and Reset axes in Edit › More. See Move and turn the drawing axes. Dimensions on arcs, circles and angles. Click an arc for its radius, a circle for its diameter, or two edges that meet for the angle. Type a new radius or diameter to resize the curve. See Radius, diameter and angle. Organizing and viewing Tags. Give groups and components a tag in Entity info, then hide or show everything with that tag in the Tags panel. See Tags. Hide, Isolate and Unhide all in Edit › More. Clicks and snaps go past hidden things. With Blender-style keys: H, Shift+H and Alt+H. See Hide, isolate and unhide. Section planes. Click a face with Section to cut the model open. Slide, reverse or turn off the cut in Entity info. Cut solids are filled. See Section planes. Outliner. Find by name, an eye on every row, F2 to rename, and drag a group onto another to move it inside. See The workspace. Presentation view (Views menu, or Shift+P). Only the model shows: no axes, guides, snapping marks or selection. See Presentation view. Scenes remember what is hidden: groups, tags and the section cut. Export animation saves the scenes as a WebM video. See Save views as scenes. Components Starter library. In the Components panel, Starter library has 34 ready-made things: doors and windows, furniture, kitchen and bath, stairs, trees, a person and a car. See Starter library. Components that stretch. Stretch this copy… makes one copy longer, wider or taller while its parts keep their sizes: legs stay put, shelves spread out, railing posts repeat. See Components that stretch. Files DWG and DXF export. Export › DWG for CAD (.dwg, 2004) or DXF for CAD (.dxf, R12). Faces become 3D faces, and groups and components become blocks. See DWG and DXF export. Save the 3D view as an image. Export › Image of the view (.png), or Image at twice the size (.png). See Save the view as an image. Print the view, or save it as a PDF. Export › PDF of the view (.pdf) or Print the view…. See Print the view. IFC storeys. Export › IFC storeys… puts each group on its floor in the IFC file. See IFC. Recover your work. If the tab or browser closes before you save, ModelCraft asks \"Recover your work?\" the next time. See If the browser closes before you save. Open in ModelCraft from CadCraft. In the CadCraft drafting app, View › More › Open in ModelCraft hands the drawing over in a new tab. It arrives as a site plan. See From CadCraft. Recent files on the start card, in browsers that can reopen a file. Everywhere Touch screens. Tap to click, hold still to finish, two fingers to orbit and pinch to zoom, three fingers to pan. Buttons are bigger, and a bar offers Done, Cancel and Undo. See On a touch screen. Language. Every word on screen now comes from a language file. Settings › General › Language offers Español (México). It is a machine-made draft that no one has reviewed yet, so some words may be wrong. The help stays in English. Find a tool or setting. Press Ctrl+K, or click Find in the app bar, and type a few letters. See Find a tool or setting. Improved DWG and DXF site plans bring in more. Text (as single-stroke lettering), splines, ellipses, dimensions, hatches (as faces with their holes) and 3D faces now come in. See Model on a site plan. Big DWG files are read in the background, so the page stays responsive. More panel windows. Panels can go to more than one window, and you can drag them between windows. A floating panel can be resized by touch. See More than one panels window. The view cube follows the app's language. Fixed Circles and rectangles on a face, started from a point. A circle started on the midpoint of a pushed box's end face used to fall flat on the ground. Now a rectangle, circle or polygon started on a corner, a midpoint or an edge is drawn on the face under the pointer. See Drawing on a face from a point. Files and compatibility Native files: a 0.4 .modelcraft file keeps curves, colors, hidden things, tags, section planes, axes, storeys and stretch rules. ModelCraft 0.3 can open it but leaves those out. If 0.3 saves it again, they are gone. DWG export writes the 2004 format. DXF export writes R12. Dimensions and guides are not exported. Not in this version yet Textures and image materials. Paint uses solid colors. Trim, Split and Outer shell. More than one section cut at a time. DWG and DXF layers as tags. What is not built yet"},{"slug":"getting-started","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Get started with ModelCraft","summary":"What ModelCraft is for, how to open it, and your first box from a rectangle to a saved model.","section":"start","order":10,"keywords":["start","first model","begin","tutorial","introduction","box","massing","3d","push pull","quick start"],"headings":[{"id":"open-modelcraft","text":"Open ModelCraft","level":2},{"id":"check-the-units-first","text":"Check the units first","level":2},{"id":"draw-your-first-box","text":"Draw your first box","level":2},{"id":"look-around","text":"Look around","level":2},{"id":"undo","text":"Undo","level":2},{"id":"save-your-model","text":"Save your model","level":2},{"id":"get-help","text":"Get help","level":2},{"id":"where-to-go-next","text":"Where to go next","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EModelCraft is a 3D modeler for early design: massing studies, room layouts, quick building forms and the shadows they cast. You draw flat shapes, pull them up into solids, and keep working on them by drawing on their faces. It runs in your web browser, and your models stay on your computer.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf you have used SketchUp, most of what you know carries over: the same tool letters, push/pull, a value box for exact sizes, groups and components. \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/coming-from\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/coming-from\"\u003EComing from other modelers\u003C/a\u003E lists what is different.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"open-modelcraft\"\u003EOpen ModelCraft\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen ModelCraft in a current version of Chrome, Edge, Firefox or Safari. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/browsers\" data-topic=\"basics/browsers\"\u003EBrowsers and computers\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWait for the 3D view to appear: a light (or dark) area in the middle with a card titled &quot;Start modeling&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe screen has a toolbar along the top, the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOutliner\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EComponents\u003C/span\u003E on the left, the 3D view in the middle, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStyle\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScenes\u003C/span\u003E on the right, and a status bar along the bottom with the value box and the units. \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/workspace\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/workspace\"\u003EThe ModelCraft screen\u003C/a\u003E explains each part.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EModelCraft needs WebGL, the 3D drawing feature of your browser. If the middle of the screen stays empty and the tools do nothing, see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/troubleshooting#the-3d-view-is-blank\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/troubleshooting\" data-hash=\"the-3d-view-is-blank\"\u003ETroubleshooting\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"check-the-units-first\"\u003ECheck the units first\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELook at the bottom right corner. The units picker shows what lengths are measured in.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA new model starts in \u003Cstrong\u003Efeet and inches\u003C/strong\u003E when your browser is set to the United States (also Liberia and Myanmar), and in \u003Cstrong\u003Emillimeters\u003C/strong\u003E everywhere else.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo change it, choose another unit in the picker: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eft and in\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Ein\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Em\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Ecm\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Emm\u003C/span\u003E. The model itself does not change size; only how lengths are shown and how bare numbers are read.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA number you type without a unit uses the model&#039;s unit. In a feet-and-inches model a bare number is \u003Cstrong\u003Einches\u003C/strong\u003E, so \u003Ccode\u003E120\u003C/code\u003E means 10 feet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-your-first-box\"\u003EDraw your first box\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERectangle\u003C/span\u003E tool, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERectangle\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraw\u003C/span\u003E group of the toolbar.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick once on the ground near the middle of the view. That is the first corner.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove the pointer away. A rectangle follows it, and the value box at the bottom (labeled \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDimensions\u003C/span\u003E) shows its size.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType the size and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E: for example \u003Ccode\u003E4m,3m\u003C/code\u003E, or \u003Ccode\u003E16&#039;,12&#039;\u003C/code\u003E in feet and inches. You don&#039;t need to click in the value box first; typing a number sends it there.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou get a flat rectangle on the ground. It is a \u003Cstrong\u003Eface\u003C/strong\u003E: a surface with edges around it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"P\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPush/pull\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the rectangle, move the pointer up, and click again. Or click the rectangle and type a height, such as \u003Ccode\u003E3m\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E10&#039;\u003C/code\u003E, then press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe rectangle becomes a box with a top, a bottom and four sides.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to go back to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E, then click the top of the box. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E on the right shows its area.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou can also drag instead of clicking twice: press on the first corner, drag, and release on the second. Both ways work for every drawing tool.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"look-around\"\u003ELook around\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOrbit:\u003C/strong\u003E drag with the middle mouse button (the wheel), or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"O\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and drag with the left button.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPan:\u003C/strong\u003E drag with the right button, or with the middle button while you hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EZoom:\u003C/strong\u003E turn the wheel. It zooms toward whatever is under the pointer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESee everything:\u003C/strong\u003E press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Z\"\u003E⇧Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExtents\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a trackpad or a touch screen the gestures are different. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/orbit-pan-zoom\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/orbit-pan-zoom\"\u003EOrbit, pan and zoom\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"undo\"\u003EUndo\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to undo and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Y\"\u003E⌘Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to redo, or use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERedo\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar. ModelCraft remembers the last 200 steps while the model is open. The history is not saved in the file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"save-your-model\"\u003ESave your model\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E group, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe browser downloads a file named after your model, such as \u003Ccode\u003EUntitled.modelcraft\u003C/code\u003E. The status bar says where it went: &quot;Saved Untitled.modelcraft to your downloads.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESaving is a download.\u003C/strong\u003E Each save makes a new copy in your downloads folder; it does not overwrite the file you opened. To keep a tidy project folder, move or rename the file there yourself. \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/files\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/files\"\u003EOpen, save, import and export\u003C/a\u003E explains why and what else you can do.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-warning\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003EWarning\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EModelCraft does not save on its own. If you close the tab with unsaved changes, the browser asks first (&quot;Leave site?&quot;). Choose to stay, then save.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"get-help\"\u003EGet help\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHelp\u003C/span\u003E in the app bar to open this help beside the model. It works with no connection. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E again, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E inside the help, to close it. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/help\" data-topic=\"basics/help\"\u003EUsing this help\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"where-to-go-next\"\u003EWhere to go next\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/draw-faces\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/draw-faces\"\u003EDraw faces with lines, rectangles and circles\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/push-pull\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/push-pull\"\u003EPush and pull faces into solids\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/value-box\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/value-box\"\u003EType exact sizes in the value box\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/keyboard\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/keyboard\"\u003EEvery key and mouse action\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"ModelCraft is a 3D modeler for early design: massing studies, room layouts, quick building forms and the shadows they cast. You draw flat shapes, pull them up into solids, and keep working on them by drawing on their faces. It runs in your web browser, and your models stay on your computer. If you have used SketchUp, most of what you know carries over: the same tool letters, push/pull, a value box for exact sizes, groups and components. Coming from other modelers lists what is different. Open ModelCraft Open ModelCraft in a current version of Chrome, Edge, Firefox or Safari. See Browsers and computers. Wait for the 3D view to appear: a light (or dark) area in the middle with a card titled \"Start modeling\". The screen has a toolbar along the top, the Outliner and Components on the left, the 3D view in the middle, Entity info, Style and Scenes on the right, and a status bar along the bottom with the value box and the units. The ModelCraft screen explains each part. ModelCraft needs WebGL, the 3D drawing feature of your browser. If the middle of the screen stays empty and the tools do nothing, see Troubleshooting. Check the units first Look at the bottom right corner. The units picker shows what lengths are measured in. A new model starts in feet and inches when your browser is set to the United States (also Liberia and Myanmar), and in millimeters everywhere else. To change it, choose another unit in the picker: ft and in, in, m, cm or mm. The model itself does not change size; only how lengths are shown and how bare numbers are read. A number you type without a unit uses the model's unit. In a feet-and-inches model a bare number is inches, so 120 means 10 feet. Draw your first box Press R for the Rectangle tool, or click Rectangle in the Draw group of the toolbar. Click once on the ground near the middle of the view. That is the first corner. Move the pointer away. A rectangle follows it, and the value box at the bottom (labeled Dimensions) shows its size. Type the size and press Enter: for example 4m,3m, or 16',12' in feet and inches. You don't need to click in the value box first; typing a number sends it there. You get a flat rectangle on the ground. It is a face: a surface with edges around it. Press P for Push/pull. Click the rectangle, move the pointer up, and click again. Or click the rectangle and type a height, such as 3m or 10', then press Enter. The rectangle becomes a box with a top, a bottom and four sides. Press Space to go back to Select, then click the top of the box. Entity info on the right shows its area. You can also drag instead of clicking twice: press on the first corner, drag, and release on the second. Both ways work for every drawing tool. Look around Orbit: drag with the middle mouse button (the wheel), or press O and drag with the left button. Pan: drag with the right button, or with the middle button while you hold Shift. Zoom: turn the wheel. It zooms toward whatever is under the pointer. See everything: press Shift+Z, or click Extents. On a trackpad or a touch screen the gestures are different. See Orbit, pan and zoom. Undo Press Ctrl+Z to undo and Ctrl+Y to redo, or use Undo and Redo in the toolbar. ModelCraft remembers the last 200 steps while the model is open. The history is not saved in the file. Save your model Click Save in the File group, or press Ctrl+S. The browser downloads a file named after your model, such as Untitled.modelcraft. The status bar says where it went: \"Saved Untitled.modelcraft to your downloads.\" Saving is a download. Each save makes a new copy in your downloads folder; it does not overwrite the file you opened. To keep a tidy project folder, move or rename the file there yourself. Open, save, import and export explains why and what else you can do. ModelCraft does not save on its own. If you close the tab with unsaved changes, the browser asks first (\"Leave site?\"). Choose to stay, then save. Get help Press F1 or click Help in the app bar to open this help beside the model. It works with no connection. Press F1 again, or Esc inside the help, to close it. See Using this help. Where to go next Draw faces with lines, rectangles and circles Push and pull faces into solids Type exact sizes in the value box Every key and mouse action"},{"slug":"workspace","scope":"modelcraft","title":"The ModelCraft screen","summary":"A tour of the toolbar, the panels on each side, the 3D view and the status bar, by their on-screen names.","section":"start","order":20,"keywords":["screen","layout","toolbar","panels","outliner","entity info","style","scenes","status bar","value box","units","interface","tour"],"headings":[{"id":"the-app-bar","text":"The app bar","level":2},{"id":"the-toolbar","text":"The toolbar","level":2},{"id":"the-3d-view","text":"The 3D view","level":2},{"id":"the-left-panels","text":"The left panels","level":2},{"id":"the-right-panels","text":"The right panels","level":2},{"id":"the-status-bar","text":"The status bar","level":2},{"id":"on-a-narrow-screen","text":"On a narrow screen","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EFrom top to bottom: the app bar, the toolbar, the three columns (left panels, 3D view, right panels) and the status bar.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-app-bar\"\u003EThe app bar\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe slim bar at the very top belongs to every CadCraft app. It has the app name and version, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAbout ModelCraft\u003C/span\u003E, the screen-mode button and the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaper\u003C/span\u003E / \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E switch.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaper\u003C/span\u003E is the light theme and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E the dark one. The 3D view changes its colors to match; your model does not change.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe screen-mode button (it reads \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFull screen\u003C/span\u003E at first) and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E cycle through three modes: normal, full screen, and full screen with the bars hidden. With the bars hidden, point at the top edge of the screen (or tap it) to bring them back. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E leaves full screen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E uses the Control key on every computer, a Mac too. On iPhone, where browsers have no full screen, the button only hides and shows the bars.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-toolbar\"\u003EThe toolbar\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe toolbar is split into labeled groups. Hover a button to see its name and key.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EGroup\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EButtons\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFile\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImport\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E (a menu of formats)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDraw\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELine\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"L\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERectangle\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECircle\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"C\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPolygon\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"N\"\u003EN\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EN\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EModify\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPush/pull\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"P\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"M\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERotate\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Q\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEraser\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"E\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETape\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"T\"\u003ET\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ET\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOrganize\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EComponent\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"G\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGroup\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift G\"\u003E⇧G\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+G\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExplode\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERedo\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Y\"\u003E⌘Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E (a menu: cut, copy, paste, paste in place \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command V\"\u003E⇧⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, duplicate \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command D\"\u003E⌘D\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, delete, select all, select none, and the key style)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECamera\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOrbit\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"O\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPan\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"H\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EZoom\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Z\"\u003EZ\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EZ\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExtents\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Z\"\u003E⇧Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EViews\u003C/span\u003E (a menu)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe keys shown are the SketchUp-style ones; with Blender style picked in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/settings\"\u003EModelCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E, the tooltips show those keys instead. The tool you are using is shown pressed. On a narrow screen the toolbar scrolls sideways.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EViews\u003C/span\u003E menu has \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIso\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETop\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFront\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERight\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBack\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELeft\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBottom\u003C/span\u003E, each fitted to the model, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPerspective\u003C/span\u003E, which switches between perspective and parallel projection.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-3d-view\"\u003EThe 3D view\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe middle of the screen. The red, green and blue lines are the axes: red and green lie on the ground, blue points up, and \u003Cstrong\u003Enorth is the green axis\u003C/strong\u003E. Where they meet is the origin.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhen you start, a card titled &quot;Start modeling&quot; lists the first few keys. It goes away when you pick a drawing tool or click in the view.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhile a tool is working, the view shows \u003Cstrong\u003Einference markers\u003C/strong\u003E: small colored shapes where the pointer snaps (a green circle at an endpoint, a cyan one at a midpoint, a red square on an edge, a blue diamond on a face). See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/inference\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/inference\"\u003EInference and snapping\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelected things turn blue. A face seen from behind is blue-grey.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhen you open a group to edit it, everything outside it fades, and the axes move to the group&#039;s own axes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhen a model has scenes, a row of scene tabs appears across the top of the view.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrop a file anywhere on the view to open or import it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-left-panels\"\u003EThe left panels\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOutliner\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/strong\u003E lists the groups and components in the model, nested the way they are nested in the model. A component used more than once shows how many times, such as &quot;Window (4)&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a name to select it. Double-click to open it for editing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFind by name\u003C/strong\u003E (shown once there are two or more groups) keeps only the rows whose name matches, with the groups they are in. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E clears it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe \u003Cstrong\u003Eeye\u003C/strong\u003E at the end of a row hides or shows that group or component (as \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHide\u003C/span\u003E does). A tag icon means it is hidden by its \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/tags\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/tags\"\u003Etag\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERename in place:\u003C/strong\u003E click a row, then press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F2\"\u003EF2\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF2\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, type, and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E keeps the old name).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDrag a row onto another group\u003C/strong\u003E to move it inside that group; drag it onto the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOutliner\u003C/span\u003E heading to move it to the top level. It stays where it is in the model, and keeps its color, tag and name. A group cannot go inside itself. Each move is one undo step.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhile you edit inside a group, a line reads &quot;Editing: Model › …&quot;, and a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClose group\u003C/span\u003E button appears. It closes every open level at once.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EComponents\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/strong\u003E lists every component definition with how many copies are placed (&quot;3×&quot;) and a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlace\u003C/span\u003E button that adds another copy where you click.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-right-panels\"\u003EThe right panels\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/strong\u003E describes what is selected. Its heading changes to say what that is:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EHeading\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EShows\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EButtons\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EModel (nothing selected)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFaces, edges, groups, components and how many are placed, guides, overall size\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete all guides\u003C/span\u003E (when there are guides)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFace\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArea, number of holes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReverse face\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdge\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELength\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Enone\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGroup or Component\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EName (type to rename), how many times it is placed, whether it is a closed solid, its volume, its size\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExplode\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMake unique\u003C/span\u003E (a component placed more than once), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMake component\u003C/span\u003E (a group)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGuide point or guide line\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Enothing\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESeveral things\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHow many faces, edges, groups and guides, the total area of the faces\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMake component (G)\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMake group (Shift+G)\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStyle\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/strong\u003E sets how the model is drawn: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFaces\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShaded\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHidden line\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWireframe\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EX-ray\u003C/span\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow edges\u003C/span\u003E, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESun shadows\u003C/span\u003E with the date, time and place of the sun. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/styles-shadows\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/styles-shadows\"\u003EStyles and sun shadows\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScenes\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/strong\u003E saves views: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd scene\u003C/span\u003E, the list of scenes, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPrevious\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlay\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENext\u003C/span\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/scenes\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/scenes\"\u003ESave views as scenes\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-status-bar\"\u003EThe status bar\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAlong the bottom, from left to right:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe tip\u003C/strong\u003E: what the tool wants next, or what the pointer is snapping to (&quot;Endpoint&quot;, &quot;On red axis&quot;).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMessages\u003C/strong\u003E: what just happened (&quot;Undid push/pull.&quot;, &quot;Imported site.dxf: 1697 edges on the ground, in a group.&quot;) or why something did not work.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe value box\u003C/strong\u003E: its label changes with the tool (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELength\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDimensions\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERadius\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESides\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDistance\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAngle\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffset\u003C/span\u003E). It shows the live size while you work, and takes what you type. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/value-box\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/value-box\"\u003EType exact sizes in the value box\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe key style\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKeys: SketchUp\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKeys: Blender\u003C/span\u003E switches the keyboard shortcuts and mouse buttons at once (the same as in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/settings\"\u003EModelCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESnap\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECollide\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/strong\u003E: snapping to points, edges and axes, and solids stopping where they touch. Crossed out = off. Click to switch. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/settings\"\u003EModelCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe units picker\u003C/strong\u003E: the unit lengths are shown in. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/getting-started#check-the-units-first\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/getting-started\" data-hash=\"check-the-units-first\"\u003EGet started\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"on-a-narrow-screen\"\u003EOn a narrow screen\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBelow about 760 pixels wide (a phone, or a narrow window), the columns stack: the toolbar, then the 3D view, the status bar, the right panels and the left panels. Scroll the page to reach the panels.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"From top to bottom: the app bar, the toolbar, the three columns (left panels, 3D view, right panels) and the status bar. The app bar The slim bar at the very top belongs to every CadCraft app. It has the app name and version, About ModelCraft, the screen-mode button and the Paper / Model switch. Paper is the light theme and Model the dark one. The 3D view changes its colors to match; your model does not change. The screen-mode button (it reads Full screen at first) and Ctrl+Shift+F cycle through three modes: normal, full screen, and full screen with the bars hidden. With the bars hidden, point at the top edge of the screen (or tap it) to bring them back. Esc leaves full screen. Ctrl+Shift+F uses the Control key on every computer, a Mac too. On iPhone, where browsers have no full screen, the button only hides and shows the bars. The toolbar The toolbar is split into labeled groups. Hover a button to see its name and key. Group Buttons File New, Open, Save, Import, Export (a menu of formats) Draw Line (L), Rectangle (R), Circle (C), Polygon (N) Modify Select (Space), Push/pull (P), Move (M), Rotate (Q), Scale (S), Eraser (E), Tape (T) Organize Component (G), Group (Shift+G), Explode Edit Undo (Ctrl+Z), Redo (Ctrl+Y), Copy (Ctrl+C), Paste (Ctrl+V), More (a menu: cut, copy, paste, paste in place Ctrl+Shift+V, duplicate Ctrl+D, delete, select all, select none, and the key style) Camera Orbit (O), Pan (H), Zoom (Z), Extents (Shift+Z), Views (a menu) The keys shown are the SketchUp-style ones; with Blender style picked in ModelCraft settings, the tooltips show those keys instead. The tool you are using is shown pressed. On a narrow screen the toolbar scrolls sideways. The Views menu has Iso, Top, Front, Right, Back, Left and Bottom, each fitted to the model, and Perspective, which switches between perspective and parallel projection. The 3D view The middle of the screen. The red, green and blue lines are the axes: red and green lie on the ground, blue points up, and north is the green axis. Where they meet is the origin. When you start, a card titled \"Start modeling\" lists the first few keys. It goes away when you pick a drawing tool or click in the view. While a tool is working, the view shows inference markers: small colored shapes where the pointer snaps (a green circle at an endpoint, a cyan one at a midpoint, a red square on an edge, a blue diamond on a face). See Inference and snapping. Selected things turn blue. A face seen from behind is blue-grey. When you open a group to edit it, everything outside it fades, and the axes move to the group's own axes. When a model has scenes, a row of scene tabs appears across the top of the view. Drop a file anywhere on the view to open or import it. The left panels Outliner lists the groups and components in the model, nested the way they are nested in the model. A component used more than once shows how many times, such as \"Window (4)\". Click a name to select it. Double-click to open it for editing. Find by name (shown once there are two or more groups) keeps only the rows whose name matches, with the groups they are in. Esc clears it. The eye at the end of a row hides or shows that group or component (as Edit › More › Hide does). A tag icon means it is hidden by its tag. Rename in place: click a row, then press F2, type, and press Enter (Esc keeps the old name). Drag a row onto another group to move it inside that group; drag it onto the Outliner heading to move it to the top level. It stays where it is in the model, and keeps its color, tag and name. A group cannot go inside itself. Each move is one undo step. While you edit inside a group, a line reads \"Editing: Model › …\", and a Close group button appears. It closes every open level at once. Components lists every component definition with how many copies are placed (\"3×\") and a Place button that adds another copy where you click. The right panels Entity info describes what is selected. Its heading changes to say what that is: Heading Shows Buttons Model (nothing selected) Faces, edges, groups, components and how many are placed, guides, overall size Delete all guides (when there are guides) Face Area, number of holes Reverse face Edge Length none Group or Component Name (type to rename), how many times it is placed, whether it is a closed solid, its volume, its size Edit, Explode, Make unique (a component placed more than once), Make component (a group) Guide point or guide line nothing Delete Several things How many faces, edges, groups and guides, the total area of the faces Make component (G), Make group (Shift+G) Style sets how the model is drawn: Faces (Shaded, Hidden line, Wireframe, X-ray), Show edges, and Sun shadows with the date, time and place of the sun. See Styles and sun shadows. Scenes saves views: Add scene, the list of scenes, and Previous, Play and Next. See Save views as scenes. The status bar Along the bottom, from left to right: The tip: what the tool wants next, or what the pointer is snapping to (\"Endpoint\", \"On red axis\"). Messages: what just happened (\"Undid push/pull.\", \"Imported site.dxf: 1697 edges on the ground, in a group.\") or why something did not work. The value box: its label changes with the tool (Length, Dimensions, Radius, Sides, Distance, Angle, Scale, Offset). It shows the live size while you work, and takes what you type. See Type exact sizes in the value box. The key style: Keys: SketchUp or Keys: Blender switches the keyboard shortcuts and mouse buttons at once (the same as in ModelCraft settings). Snap and Collide: snapping to points, edges and axes, and solids stopping where they touch. Crossed out = off. Click to switch. See ModelCraft settings. The units picker: the unit lengths are shown in. See Get started. On a narrow screen Below about 760 pixels wide (a phone, or a narrow window), the columns stack: the toolbar, then the 3D view, the status bar, the right panels and the left panels. Scroll the page to reach the panels."},{"slug":"files","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Open, save, import and export","summary":"The ModelCraft file, what you can bring in and send out, where files go, and what changes offline and between browsers.","section":"start","order":30,"keywords":["open","save","import","export","file","recover","autosave","recent","desktop","open in 3d","handoff","download","modelcraft file","dae","collada","obj","gltf","glb","stl","ifc","dwg","dxf","formats","offline","save as"],"headings":[{"id":"formats-at-a-glance","text":"Formats at a glance","level":2},{"id":"the-modelcraft-file","text":"The ModelCraft file","level":2},{"id":"open-a-model","text":"Open a model","level":3},{"id":"save-a-model","text":"Save a model","level":3},{"id":"start-a-new-model","text":"Start a new model","level":3},{"id":"if-the-browser-closes-before-you-save","text":"If the browser closes before you save","level":3},{"id":"from-cadcraft-open-in-modelcraft","text":"From CadCraft: Open in ModelCraft","level":3},{"id":"import","text":"Import","level":2},{"id":"export","text":"Export","level":2},{"id":"online-and-offline","text":"Online and offline","level":2},{"id":"desktop-app","text":"Desktop app","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EModelCraft reads and writes files on your computer. Nothing is uploaded: a file you open is read inside your browser, and a file you save is a download. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/your-files\" data-topic=\"basics/your-files\"\u003EWhere your files go\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"formats-at-a-glance\"\u003EFormats at a glance\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFormat\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EOpen\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EImport\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EExport\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it is for\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EModelCraft (\u003Ccode\u003E.modelcraft\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eyes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eno\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYour working file. Keeps everything.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECollada (\u003Ccode\u003E.dae\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eno\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eyes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eyes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETrading models with other 3D modelers. Keeps groups, components and colors.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOBJ (\u003Ccode\u003E.obj\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eno\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eyes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eyes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERendering and visualization programs.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EglTF (\u003Ccode\u003E.glb\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.gltf\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eno\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eyes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.glb\u003C/code\u003E only\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWeb viewers, game engines, AR.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESTL (\u003Ccode\u003E.stl\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eno\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eno\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eyes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E3D printing (in millimeters).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIFC (\u003Ccode\u003E.ifc\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eno\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eno\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eyes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBIM programs, and BimCraft.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDWG (\u003Ccode\u003E.dwg\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eno\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eyes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eyes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA site plan or floor plan to model on; sending the model to a CAD program.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDXF (\u003Ccode\u003E.dxf\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eno\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eyes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eyes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same, for any CAD program.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/formats\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/formats\"\u003EFile formats in detail\u003C/a\u003E lists exactly what each format keeps and leaves out.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-modelcraft-file\"\u003EThe ModelCraft file\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA \u003Ccode\u003E.modelcraft\u003C/code\u003E file keeps the whole model: geometry, groups, components, guides, scenes, the units, and the camera and style you were using when you saved. It does not keep the undo history.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"open-a-model\"\u003EOpen a model\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E group, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command O\"\u003E⌘O\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+O\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf the model on screen has unsaved changes, ModelCraft asks: &quot;This model has changes that are not saved. Save a copy first?&quot; Choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave a copy\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDiscard changes\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECancel\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick the \u003Ccode\u003E.modelcraft\u003C/code\u003E file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe model opens in the units it was saved in, with the camera it was saved with. You can also drop a \u003Ccode\u003E.modelcraft\u003C/code\u003E file onto the 3D view, or click one in the list of recent files on the start card (browsers that can reopen a file, and the desktop app).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"shell:desktop\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EDesktop app\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn the desktop app, double-clicking a \u003Ccode\u003E.modelcraft\u003C/code\u003E file opens it in ModelCraft, and so does \u003Cstrong\u003EOpen with\u003C/strong\u003E on a Collada, OBJ, glTF, DWG or DXF file (it is imported into a new model).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"save-a-model\"\u003ESave a model\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The browser downloads \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;name&gt;.modelcraft\u003C/code\u003E, where the name is the file you opened, or &quot;Untitled&quot; for a new model.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"shell:desktop\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EDesktop app\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn the desktop app, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E writes the file you opened in place. A new model asks where to save it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThere is no Save As. To give a new model a name, rename the downloaded file. The next save after you open the renamed file uses the new name.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach save is a new download. Depending on the browser&#039;s settings, it goes straight to your downloads folder or asks where to save. If a file with that name is already there, most browsers add a number, such as \u003Ccode\u003EUntitled (1).modelcraft\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:chromium\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EChrome or Edge\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo choose the folder every time, open the browser&#039;s settings, search for &quot;downloads&quot; and turn on &quot;Ask where to save each file before downloading&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:firefox\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EFirefox\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo choose the folder every time, open \u003Cstrong\u003ESettings\u003C/strong\u003E, then \u003Cstrong\u003EGeneral\u003C/strong\u003E, and under &quot;Downloads&quot; choose &quot;Always ask you where to save files&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:safari\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ESafari\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo change where downloads go, open \u003Cstrong\u003ESafari\u003C/strong\u003E, then \u003Cstrong\u003ESettings\u003C/strong\u003E, then \u003Cstrong\u003EGeneral\u003C/strong\u003E, and set &quot;File download location&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"start-a-new-model\"\u003EStart a new model\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew\u003C/span\u003E. If there are unsaved changes, ModelCraft asks first. A new model starts empty, in your locale&#039;s unit (feet and inches in the United States, millimeters elsewhere), with no scenes.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"if-the-browser-closes-before-you-save\"\u003EIf the browser closes before you save\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EModelCraft keeps a copy of unsaved work in your browser a couple of seconds after each change. If the tab or the browser closes before you save, the next time you open ModelCraft it asks &quot;Recover your work?&quot;. Recovering opens the model as it was, not yet saved to a file: save it to keep it. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/recover\" data-topic=\"basics/recover\"\u003ERecover your work\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"from-cadcraft-open-in-modelcraft\"\u003EFrom CadCraft: Open in ModelCraft\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn the CadCraft drafting app, \u003Cstrong\u003EView\u003C/strong\u003E, then \u003Cstrong\u003EMore\u003C/strong\u003E, then \u003Cstrong\u003EOpen in ModelCraft\u003C/strong\u003E hands the open drawing to ModelCraft in a new tab, as a DWG. It arrives the way an imported DWG does: lines on the ground, in a group, ready to model on. Nothing is uploaded; the file passes through your browser&#039;s own storage.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"import\"\u003EImport\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EImporting adds a file to the model you have open; it does not replace it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImport\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E group, or drop the file on the 3D view.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick a Collada, OBJ, glTF, DWG or DXF file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe file arrives as \u003Cstrong\u003Eone group\u003C/strong\u003E, selected, and the view zooms to show it. The status bar says what came in, and lists anything it could not import, such as &quot;Not imported: 2 texture&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo work on its geometry directly, double-click the group to edit inside it, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExplode\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESome things to know:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESize and up direction:\u003C/strong\u003E Collada files say their unit and which axis is up, and ModelCraft converts them. glTF is always in meters. OBJ files carry no unit: ModelCraft reads them as meters, with Y up. If an OBJ comes in 1000 times too big or too small, use \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/rotate-scale\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/rotate-scale\"\u003EScale\u003C/a\u003E with a factor such as \u003Ccode\u003E0.001\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E1000\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDWG and DXF\u003C/strong\u003E come in as lines (edges) on the ground, not as faces. If the drawing does not say its unit, a dialog titled &quot;Drawing units&quot; asks what one drawing unit is. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/site-plan\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/site-plan\"\u003EModel on a site plan\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELimits:\u003C/strong\u003E files over 300 MB are refused for import, and over 200 MB for opening. Files with more than 2 million vertices or faces are refused as too big.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"export\"\u003EExport\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E group.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose a format: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EglTF binary (.glb)\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECollada (.dae)\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOBJ (.obj)\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESTL for printing (.stl, millimeters)\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIFC for BimCraft (.ifc)\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDWG for CAD (.dwg, 2004)\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDXF for CAD (.dxf, R12)\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe browser downloads the file, named like the model. The whole model is exported, not just the selection. An empty model is not exported: &quot;The model is empty: draw something first.&quot; See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/export-model\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/export-model\"\u003EExport a model for other programs\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"online-and-offline\"\u003EOnline and offline\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEverything in ModelCraft runs on your computer, so modeling, opening, saving, importing and exporting all work without a connection once the app is loaded.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo open ModelCraft with no connection at all, save it for offline use while you are online:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E in the app bar at the top.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe button counts up (&quot;Saving 40 of 212&quot;) while it saves. ModelCraft saves its own files (its DWG reader among them), this help and the 3D library, so it can take a minute.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhen the button reads \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline ready\u003C/span\u003E, ModelCraft opens from the same address with no connection, and DWG import works offline too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline ready\u003C/span\u003E later for \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave again (get the newest version)\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemove the offline copy\u003C/span\u003E. If the button reads \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECould not save\u003C/span\u003E, some files did not download: check the connection and click it to try again. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline not available\u003C/span\u003E means this browser window cannot keep a copy (private windows and some managed computers). See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/offline\" data-topic=\"basics/offline\"\u003EWorking offline\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECloud saving is not available yet. Every model lives where you save it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"net:offline\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EOffline\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou are offline. You can keep modeling and saving. Unless the app bar shows \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline ready\u003C/span\u003E, don&#039;t reload this tab until you are online again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"desktop-app\"\u003EDesktop app\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"shell:desktop\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EDesktop app\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDesktop builds of ModelCraft are not available yet. This help describes ModelCraft in the browser.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"shell:web\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EIn the browser\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDesktop builds are not available yet. ModelCraft runs in the browser on Mac, Windows and Linux.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E","text":"ModelCraft reads and writes files on your computer. Nothing is uploaded: a file you open is read inside your browser, and a file you save is a download. See Where your files go. Formats at a glance Format Open Import Export What it is for ModelCraft (.modelcraft) yes no Save Your working file. Keeps everything. Collada (.dae) no yes yes Trading models with other 3D modelers. Keeps groups, components and colors. OBJ (.obj) no yes yes Rendering and visualization programs. glTF (.glb, .gltf) no yes .glb only Web viewers, game engines, AR. STL (.stl) no no yes 3D printing (in millimeters). IFC (.ifc) no no yes BIM programs, and BimCraft. DWG (.dwg) no yes yes A site plan or floor plan to model on; sending the model to a CAD program. DXF (.dxf) no yes yes The same, for any CAD program. File formats in detail lists exactly what each format keeps and leaves out. The ModelCraft file A .modelcraft file keeps the whole model: geometry, groups, components, guides, scenes, the units, and the camera and style you were using when you saved. It does not keep the undo history. Open a model Click Open in the File group, or press Ctrl+O. If the model on screen has unsaved changes, ModelCraft asks: \"This model has changes that are not saved. Save a copy first?\" Choose Save a copy, Discard changes or Cancel. Pick the .modelcraft file. The model opens in the units it was saved in, with the camera it was saved with. You can also drop a .modelcraft file onto the 3D view, or click one in the list of recent files on the start card (browsers that can reopen a file, and the desktop app). In the desktop app, double-clicking a .modelcraft file opens it in ModelCraft, and so does Open with on a Collada, OBJ, glTF, DWG or DXF file (it is imported into a new model). Save a model Click Save or press Ctrl+S. The browser downloads \u003Cname\u003E.modelcraft, where the name is the file you opened, or \"Untitled\" for a new model. In the desktop app, Save writes the file you opened in place. A new model asks where to save it. There is no Save As. To give a new model a name, rename the downloaded file. The next save after you open the renamed file uses the new name. Each save is a new download. Depending on the browser's settings, it goes straight to your downloads folder or asks where to save. If a file with that name is already there, most browsers add a number, such as Untitled (1).modelcraft. To choose the folder every time, open the browser's settings, search for \"downloads\" and turn on \"Ask where to save each file before downloading\". To choose the folder every time, open Settings, then General, and under \"Downloads\" choose \"Always ask you where to save files\". To change where downloads go, open Safari, then Settings, then General, and set \"File download location\". Start a new model Click New. If there are unsaved changes, ModelCraft asks first. A new model starts empty, in your locale's unit (feet and inches in the United States, millimeters elsewhere), with no scenes. If the browser closes before you save ModelCraft keeps a copy of unsaved work in your browser a couple of seconds after each change. If the tab or the browser closes before you save, the next time you open ModelCraft it asks \"Recover your work?\". Recovering opens the model as it was, not yet saved to a file: save it to keep it. See Recover your work. From CadCraft: Open in ModelCraft In the CadCraft drafting app, View, then More, then Open in ModelCraft hands the open drawing to ModelCraft in a new tab, as a DWG. It arrives the way an imported DWG does: lines on the ground, in a group, ready to model on. Nothing is uploaded; the file passes through your browser's own storage. Import Importing adds a file to the model you have open; it does not replace it. Click Import in the File group, or drop the file on the 3D view. Pick a Collada, OBJ, glTF, DWG or DXF file. The file arrives as one group, selected, and the view zooms to show it. The status bar says what came in, and lists anything it could not import, such as \"Not imported: 2 texture\". To work on its geometry directly, double-click the group to edit inside it, or click Explode. Some things to know: Size and up direction: Collada files say their unit and which axis is up, and ModelCraft converts them. glTF is always in meters. OBJ files carry no unit: ModelCraft reads them as meters, with Y up. If an OBJ comes in 1000 times too big or too small, use Scale with a factor such as 0.001 or 1000. DWG and DXF come in as lines (edges) on the ground, not as faces. If the drawing does not say its unit, a dialog titled \"Drawing units\" asks what one drawing unit is. See Model on a site plan. Limits: files over 300 MB are refused for import, and over 200 MB for opening. Files with more than 2 million vertices or faces are refused as too big. Export Click Export in the File group. Choose a format: glTF binary (.glb), Collada (.dae), OBJ (.obj), STL for printing (.stl, millimeters), IFC for BimCraft (.ifc), DWG for CAD (.dwg, 2004) or DXF for CAD (.dxf, R12). The browser downloads the file, named like the model. The whole model is exported, not just the selection. An empty model is not exported: \"The model is empty: draw something first.\" See Export a model for other programs. Online and offline Everything in ModelCraft runs on your computer, so modeling, opening, saving, importing and exporting all work without a connection once the app is loaded. To open ModelCraft with no connection at all, save it for offline use while you are online: Click Save for offline in the app bar at the top. The button counts up (\"Saving 40 of 212\") while it saves. ModelCraft saves its own files (its DWG reader among them), this help and the 3D library, so it can take a minute. When the button reads Offline ready, ModelCraft opens from the same address with no connection, and DWG import works offline too. Click Offline ready later for Save again (get the newest version) or Remove the offline copy. If the button reads Could not save, some files did not download: check the connection and click it to try again. Offline not available means this browser window cannot keep a copy (private windows and some managed computers). See Working offline. Cloud saving is not available yet. Every model lives where you save it. You are offline. You can keep modeling and saving. Unless the app bar shows Offline ready, don't reload this tab until you are online again. Desktop app Desktop builds of ModelCraft are not available yet. This help describes ModelCraft in the browser. Desktop builds are not available yet. ModelCraft runs in the browser on Mac, Windows and Linux."},{"slug":"orbit-pan-zoom","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Orbit, pan and zoom","summary":"Move around the model with a mouse, a trackpad, a touch screen or the keyboard, and jump to standard views.","section":"tasks","order":10,"keywords":["orbit","pan","zoom","long press","three fingers","two fingers","tap","navigate","camera","view","rotate view","trackpad","touch","pinch","middle button","zoom extents","perspective","parallel","top view","iso"],"headings":[{"id":"with-a-three-button-mouse","text":"With a three-button mouse","level":2},{"id":"without-a-middle-button","text":"Without a middle button","level":2},{"id":"on-a-trackpad","text":"On a trackpad","level":2},{"id":"on-a-touch-screen","text":"On a touch screen","level":2},{"id":"standard-views","text":"Standard views","level":2},{"id":"perspective-and-parallel-projection","text":"Perspective and parallel projection","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EYou can move the camera at any time, even in the middle of drawing a line or pushing a face: the tool waits where it was.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"with-a-three-button-mouse\"\u003EWith a three-button mouse\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETo\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDo this\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOrbit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag with the middle button (press the wheel)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag with the right button, or hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and drag with the middle button\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETurn the wheel. It zooms toward the face under the pointer, or toward the point under it at the depth of the view&#039;s center\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESee the whole model\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Z\"\u003E⇧Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExtents\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOrbit turns the view around the point at the center of the view, not around the pointer. To orbit around a particular part, zoom toward it with the wheel first: zooming moves the center with it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA right-click without dragging does nothing; ModelCraft has no right-click menu.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"without-a-middle-button\"\u003EWithout a middle button\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUse the camera tools in the toolbar, or the keyboard:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"O\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOrbit\u003C/span\u003E) and drag with the left button to orbit.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"H\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPan\u003C/span\u003E) and drag to pan.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Z\"\u003EZ\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EZ\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EZoom\u003C/span\u003E) and drag up to zoom in, down to zoom out.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to go back to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E, or press the key of the tool you want next.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe camera tools replace the tool you were using. To look around in the middle of an operation without losing it, use the keyboard instead:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe arrow keys orbit.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E with the arrow keys pans.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhile a drawing tool is in the middle of an operation (after its first click), the arrow keys lock directions instead of orbiting. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/inference#lock-a-direction\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/inference\" data-hash=\"lock-a-direction\"\u003EInference and snapping\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"on-a-trackpad\"\u003EOn a trackpad\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe easiest way: open \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModelCraft\u003C/span\u003E tab, and set Scrolling to \u003Cstrong\u003ETrackpad\u003C/strong\u003E (or leave it on &quot;Work it out&quot;). Then swipe with two fingers to orbit, hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and swipe to pan, and pinch to zoom — no camera buttons. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/settings#scrolling-mouse-wheel-or-trackpad\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/settings\" data-hash=\"scrolling-mouse-wheel-or-trackpad\"\u003EModelCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith Scrolling set to \u003Cstrong\u003EMouse wheel\u003C/strong\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:mac\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMac\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EZoom:\u003C/strong\u003E scroll with two fingers, or pinch. Both zoom toward the pointer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPan:\u003C/strong\u003E press with two fingers (a secondary click) and keep the fingers down while you drag. Turn on &quot;Secondary click&quot; in \u003Cstrong\u003ESystem Settings\u003C/strong\u003E, then \u003Cstrong\u003ETrackpad\u003C/strong\u003E, if two-finger click does nothing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOrbit:\u003C/strong\u003E press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"O\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and drag with one finger, or use the arrow keys.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EHolding \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while you click may not count as a right-click in ModelCraft, depending on the browser: it can act as a click with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E held instead. Use the two-finger press to pan.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:windows,linux\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EWindows or Linux\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EZoom:\u003C/strong\u003E scroll with two fingers. Pinch zooms too where the touchpad driver sends it as a scroll with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPan:\u003C/strong\u003E drag with the right button (on many touchpads, press the lower-right corner and drag), or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"H\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and drag.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOrbit:\u003C/strong\u003E press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"O\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and drag, or use the arrow keys.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith Mouse wheel, two-finger scrolling zooms; it does not pan. That is the opposite of many web pages, and it is how the mouse wheel behaves in most 3D modelers.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E picks the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E tool in ModelCraft. Holding \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and dragging does not pan, as it does in some other apps.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"on-a-touch-screen\"\u003EOn a touch screen\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:touch\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ETouch\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOne finger\u003C/strong\u003E uses the current tool: \u003Cstrong\u003Etap\u003C/strong\u003E to click, \u003Cstrong\u003Epress and drag\u003C/strong\u003E to draw or drag.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EHold still\u003C/strong\u003E for half a second to finish what the tool is doing, as \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E does: a curve, a line chain.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETwo fingers\u003C/strong\u003E dragged together \u003Cstrong\u003Eorbit\u003C/strong\u003E; \u003Cstrong\u003Epinch\u003C/strong\u003E to zoom. Both work in one gesture.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThree fingers\u003C/strong\u003E dragged together \u003Cstrong\u003Epan\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPutting a second finger down \u003Cstrong\u003Ecancels\u003C/strong\u003E what the first finger started (a rectangle half drawn, a push/pull in progress). Lift your fingers and start again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA small bar at the bottom of the view has \u003Cstrong\u003EDone\u003C/strong\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cstrong\u003ECancel\u003C/strong\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) and \u003Cstrong\u003EUndo\u003C/strong\u003E, for when there is no keyboard.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESnapping and picking reach a little further than with a mouse, and the buttons are bigger, so a finger can hit them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:mouse\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMouse\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a touch screen, one finger uses the current tool (tap to click, hold still to finish), two fingers orbit and pinch to zoom, three fingers pan, and a small bar offers Done, Cancel and Undo.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"standard-views\"\u003EStandard views\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EViews\u003C/span\u003E at the right end of the toolbar.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIso\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETop\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFront\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERight\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBack\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELeft\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBottom\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe view turns to that direction and fits the whole model. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFront\u003C/span\u003E looks north, along the green axis; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETop\u003C/span\u003E looks straight down with north up.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe view cube at the top right of the view does the same in one click, plus edges and corners, a compass ring to turn the model and a home button. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/view-cube\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/view-cube\"\u003EThe view cube\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"perspective-and-parallel-projection\"\u003EPerspective and parallel projection\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EViews\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPerspective\u003C/span\u003E. A check mark means perspective is on. With it off, the view is a parallel (orthographic) projection: lines that are parallel in the model stay parallel on screen, which is what you want for elevations from \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFront\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERight\u003C/span\u003E. The status bar says &quot;Perspective.&quot; or &quot;Parallel projection.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach scene remembers whether it was in perspective. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/scenes\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/scenes\"\u003ESave views as scenes\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"You can move the camera at any time, even in the middle of drawing a line or pushing a face: the tool waits where it was. With a three-button mouse To Do this Orbit Drag with the middle button (press the wheel) Pan Drag with the right button, or hold Shift and drag with the middle button Zoom Turn the wheel. It zooms toward the face under the pointer, or toward the point under it at the depth of the view's center See the whole model Shift+Z or Extents Orbit turns the view around the point at the center of the view, not around the pointer. To orbit around a particular part, zoom toward it with the wheel first: zooming moves the center with it. A right-click without dragging does nothing; ModelCraft has no right-click menu. Without a middle button Use the camera tools in the toolbar, or the keyboard: Press O (or click Orbit) and drag with the left button to orbit. Press H (or click Pan) and drag to pan. Press Z (or click Zoom) and drag up to zoom in, down to zoom out. Press Space to go back to Select, or press the key of the tool you want next. The camera tools replace the tool you were using. To look around in the middle of an operation without losing it, use the keyboard instead: The arrow keys orbit. Shift with the arrow keys pans. While a drawing tool is in the middle of an operation (after its first click), the arrow keys lock directions instead of orbiting. See Inference and snapping. On a trackpad The easiest way: open Settings (Ctrl+,), ModelCraft tab, and set Scrolling to Trackpad (or leave it on \"Work it out\"). Then swipe with two fingers to orbit, hold Shift and swipe to pan, and pinch to zoom — no camera buttons. See ModelCraft settings. With Scrolling set to Mouse wheel: Zoom: scroll with two fingers, or pinch. Both zoom toward the pointer. Pan: press with two fingers (a secondary click) and keep the fingers down while you drag. Turn on \"Secondary click\" in System Settings, then Trackpad, if two-finger click does nothing. Orbit: press O and drag with one finger, or use the arrow keys. Holding Ctrl while you click may not count as a right-click in ModelCraft, depending on the browser: it can act as a click with Ctrl held instead. Use the two-finger press to pan. Zoom: scroll with two fingers. Pinch zooms too where the touchpad driver sends it as a scroll with Ctrl. Pan: drag with the right button (on many touchpads, press the lower-right corner and drag), or press H and drag. Orbit: press O and drag, or use the arrow keys. With Mouse wheel, two-finger scrolling zooms; it does not pan. That is the opposite of many web pages, and it is how the mouse wheel behaves in most 3D modelers. Space picks the Select tool in ModelCraft. Holding Space and dragging does not pan, as it does in some other apps. On a touch screen One finger uses the current tool: tap to click, press and drag to draw or drag. Hold still for half a second to finish what the tool is doing, as Enter does: a curve, a line chain. Two fingers dragged together orbit; pinch to zoom. Both work in one gesture. Three fingers dragged together pan. Putting a second finger down cancels what the first finger started (a rectangle half drawn, a push/pull in progress). Lift your fingers and start again. A small bar at the bottom of the view has Done (Enter), Cancel (Esc) and Undo, for when there is no keyboard. Snapping and picking reach a little further than with a mouse, and the buttons are bigger, so a finger can hit them. On a touch screen, one finger uses the current tool (tap to click, hold still to finish), two fingers orbit and pinch to zoom, three fingers pan, and a small bar offers Done, Cancel and Undo. Standard views Click Views at the right end of the toolbar. Choose Iso, Top, Front, Right, Back, Left or Bottom. The view turns to that direction and fits the whole model. Front looks north, along the green axis; Top looks straight down with north up. The view cube at the top right of the view does the same in one click, plus edges and corners, a compass ring to turn the model and a home button. See The view cube. Perspective and parallel projection Choose Views, then Perspective. A check mark means perspective is on. With it off, the view is a parallel (orthographic) projection: lines that are parallel in the model stay parallel on screen, which is what you want for elevations from Front or Right. The status bar says \"Perspective.\" or \"Parallel projection.\" Each scene remembers whether it was in perspective. See Save views as scenes."},{"slug":"view-cube","scope":"modelcraft","title":"The view cube","summary":"Click a face, edge or corner of the cube in the corner of the view to look from there, drag it to orbit, turn the compass ring, step around a face-on view, and go home.","section":"tasks","order":11,"keywords":["view cube","viewcube","cube","compass","north","home view","standard views","top","front","right","iso","corner","edge","orbit","rotate view","axis icon","corner indicator","navigation"],"headings":[{"id":"look-from-a-side-an-edge-or-a-corner","text":"Look from a side, an edge or a corner","level":2},{"id":"orbit-by-dragging-the-cube","text":"Orbit by dragging the cube","level":2},{"id":"the-compass-ring","text":"The compass ring","level":2},{"id":"face-on-views-arrows","text":"Face-on views: arrows","level":2},{"id":"home","text":"Home","level":2},{"id":"with-the-keyboard","text":"With the keyboard","level":2},{"id":"axis-icon-or-nothing-instead","text":"Axis icon or nothing instead","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe view cube sits at the top right of the 3D view. It turns with the view, so it always shows which way you are looking: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETop\u003C/span\u003E is up, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFront\u003C/span\u003E faces south (you look north, along the green axis, when you look at it), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERight\u003C/span\u003E faces east (red axis). It stays see-through until you point at it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"look-from-a-side-an-edge-or-a-corner\"\u003ELook from a side, an edge or a corner\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPoint at the cube. The part under the pointer lights up: the middle of a face, an edge (it lights on both faces that share it) or a corner (on all three faces).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick it. The view turns to look from there, keeping the same middle and zoom.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThere are 26 places to click: 6 faces (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETop\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBottom\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFront\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBack\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELeft\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERight\u003C/span\u003E), 12 edges and 8 corners. A corner gives the classic three-quarter view; an edge looks halfway between two faces.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClicking \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFront\u003C/span\u003E is the same as \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EViews\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFront\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar, without the zoom to fit. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EViews\u003C/span\u003E still zooms to the whole model.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"orbit-by-dragging-the-cube\"\u003EOrbit by dragging the cube\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDrag the cube to turn the view, the same as orbiting in the view itself. A drag is never taken as a click, so you can grab the cube anywhere.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-compass-ring\"\u003EThe compass ring\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe ring under the cube carries N, E, S and W.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDrag the ring\u003C/strong\u003E to turn the model about the vertical axis, as if it stood on a turntable. The ring follows the pointer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EClick a letter\u003C/strong\u003E to look from that side, keeping how steeply you look down. In a view from straight above, the letter you click goes to the bottom of the screen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"face-on-views-arrows\"\u003EFace-on views: arrows\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen you look straight at a face, small arrows appear round the cube:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe four \u003Cstrong\u003Etriangles\u003C/strong\u003E bring the face on that side to the front: the triangle above the cube shows the face that was at the top, and so on.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe two \u003Cstrong\u003Ecurved arrows\u003C/strong\u003E at the top right turn the picture a quarter turn clockwise or counter-clockwise, without changing which face you look at. Use them to turn a plan so that a long wall runs across the screen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"home\"\u003EHome\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe house button at the top left of the cube goes back to the three-quarter \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIso\u003C/span\u003E view and fits the whole model in. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ←\"\u003Efn ←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EHome\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E does the same while the cube has the keyboard focus.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"with-the-keyboard\"\u003EWith the keyboard\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to the cube, then:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDoes\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"←\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E / \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"→\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Echoose a side, edge or corner round the cube, in eighth-turn steps (screen readers say which)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"↑\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E / \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"↓\"\u003E↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Echoose a higher or lower one, up to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETop\u003C/span\u003E or down to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBottom\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Elook from the one chosen\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift ←\"\u003E⇧←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E / \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift →\"\u003E⇧→\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+→\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eturn the view a quarter turn about the vertical axis\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ←\"\u003Efn ←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EHome\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ethe home view\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eleave the cube\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EKeys pressed on the cube stay with the cube: they do not start ModelCraft tools.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"axis-icon-or-nothing-instead\"\u003EAxis icon or nothing instead\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModelCraft\u003C/span\u003E tab, and set \u003Cstrong\u003ECorner indicator\u003C/strong\u003E to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView cube\u003C/span\u003E (the start), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAxis icon\u003C/span\u003E (a small X, Y, Z marker at the bottom left: X red, Y green, Z blue) or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENone\u003C/span\u003E. This browser remembers the choice. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/settings#corner-indicator\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/settings\" data-hash=\"corner-indicator\"\u003EModelCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EModelCraft has no roll: away from straight-down and straight-up views, up on the screen is always up in the model. The cube and the curved arrows can turn a plan view; they cannot tip a side view onto its edge.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"The view cube sits at the top right of the 3D view. It turns with the view, so it always shows which way you are looking: Top is up, Front faces south (you look north, along the green axis, when you look at it), Right faces east (red axis). It stays see-through until you point at it. Look from a side, an edge or a corner Point at the cube. The part under the pointer lights up: the middle of a face, an edge (it lights on both faces that share it) or a corner (on all three faces). Click it. The view turns to look from there, keeping the same middle and zoom. There are 26 places to click: 6 faces (Top, Bottom, Front, Back, Left, Right), 12 edges and 8 corners. A corner gives the classic three-quarter view; an edge looks halfway between two faces. Clicking Front is the same as Views, Front in the toolbar, without the zoom to fit. Views still zooms to the whole model. Orbit by dragging the cube Drag the cube to turn the view, the same as orbiting in the view itself. A drag is never taken as a click, so you can grab the cube anywhere. The compass ring The ring under the cube carries N, E, S and W. Drag the ring to turn the model about the vertical axis, as if it stood on a turntable. The ring follows the pointer. Click a letter to look from that side, keeping how steeply you look down. In a view from straight above, the letter you click goes to the bottom of the screen. Face-on views: arrows When you look straight at a face, small arrows appear round the cube: The four triangles bring the face on that side to the front: the triangle above the cube shows the face that was at the top, and so on. The two curved arrows at the top right turn the picture a quarter turn clockwise or counter-clockwise, without changing which face you look at. Use them to turn a plan so that a long wall runs across the screen. Home The house button at the top left of the cube goes back to the three-quarter Iso view and fits the whole model in. Home does the same while the cube has the keyboard focus. With the keyboard Tab to the cube, then: Key Does ← / → choose a side, edge or corner round the cube, in eighth-turn steps (screen readers say which) ↑ / ↓ choose a higher or lower one, up to Top or down to Bottom Enter or Space look from the one chosen Shift+← / Shift+→ turn the view a quarter turn about the vertical axis Home the home view Esc leave the cube Keys pressed on the cube stay with the cube: they do not start ModelCraft tools. Axis icon or nothing instead Open Settings (Ctrl+,), the ModelCraft tab, and set Corner indicator to View cube (the start), Axis icon (a small X, Y, Z marker at the bottom left: X red, Y green, Z blue) or None. This browser remembers the choice. See ModelCraft settings. ModelCraft has no roll: away from straight-down and straight-up views, up on the screen is always up in the model. The cube and the curved arrows can turn a plan view; they cannot tip a side view onto its edge."},{"slug":"draw-faces","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Draw faces with lines, rectangles, circles and arcs","summary":"Make flat faces on the ground or on other faces, split them with lines, and draw circles and polygons with the number of sides you want.","section":"tasks","order":20,"keywords":["draw","line","rectangle","circle","polygon","offset","inset","frame","border","arc","curve","spline","smooth","cable","path","bulge","radius","curve","face","floor plan","footprint","split","sides","segments","outline","pencil"],"headings":[{"id":"draw-a-rectangle","text":"Draw a rectangle","level":2},{"id":"draw-a-circle-or-a-polygon","text":"Draw a circle or a polygon","level":2},{"id":"draw-an-arc","text":"Draw an arc","level":2},{"id":"draw-a-smooth-curve","text":"Draw a smooth curve","level":2},{"id":"offset-a-border-inside-or-outside-a-face","text":"Offset: a border inside or outside a face","level":2},{"id":"arcs-circles-and-polygons-are-curves","text":"Arcs, circles and polygons are curves","level":2},{"id":"draw-with-lines","text":"Draw with lines","level":2},{"id":"where-a-line-goes-when-it-touches-nothing","text":"Where a line goes when it touches nothing","level":3},{"id":"split-a-face","text":"Split a face","level":2},{"id":"faces-facing-the-wrong-way","text":"Faces facing the wrong way","level":2},{"id":"what-you-see-while-drawing","text":"What you see while drawing","level":2},{"id":"if-the-shape-is-not-where-you-expected","text":"If the shape is not where you expected","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EEverything in ModelCraft starts as a flat \u003Cstrong\u003Eface\u003C/strong\u003E: a surface closed by edges. You can draw a face directly with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERectangle\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECircle\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPolygon\u003C/span\u003E, or outline one edge at a time with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELine\u003C/span\u003E. Faces are what \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/push-pull\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/push-pull\"\u003EPush/pull\u003C/a\u003E turns into solids.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-a-rectangle\"\u003EDraw a rectangle\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the first corner: on the ground, or on any face of the model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove the pointer. The rectangle stays on the ground (or on the face you started on), and the value box (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDimensions\u003C/span\u003E) shows its width and depth. The tip says &quot;Square&quot; when the sides are equal.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the opposite corner, or type the two sizes separated by a comma and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003E4m,3m\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E4000,3000\u003C/code\u003E in a millimeter model, or \u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;,10&#039;6&quot;\u003C/code\u003E in feet and inches.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA face appears with four edges around it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe typed sizes go in the direction you were pointing: if the pointer is to the left of the first corner when you type, the rectangle grows to the left.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-warning\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003EWarning\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe comma separates the two sizes, so it cannot be a decimal mark. Type \u003Ccode\u003E2.5m,3m\u003C/code\u003E, never \u003Ccode\u003E2,5m\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-a-circle-or-a-polygon\"\u003EDraw a circle or a polygon\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"C\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for a circle or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"N\"\u003EN\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EN\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for a polygon.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo change the number of sides, type it before you click: \u003Ccode\u003E12s\u003C/code\u003E (or just \u003Ccode\u003E12\u003C/code\u003E) and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. A circle starts with 24 sides and a polygon with 6. The tip shows the current number, and ModelCraft keeps it for the next circle until you change it or switch tools.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the center, on the ground or on a face.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick to set the radius, or type it (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERadius\u003C/span\u003E in the value box) and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA circle is a polygon with many sides. When you pull a circle up with Push/pull, its sides are softened so the cylinder reads as one smooth surface.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-an-arc\"\u003EDraw an arc\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"A\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArc\u003C/span\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick where the arc starts, then where it ends (or type the length between them).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove the pointer to bulge the arc out, and click. The tip says &quot;Half circle&quot; when the bulge is half the distance between the ends. Or type how far the middle stands off: \u003Ccode\u003E2\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo draw an arc around a point instead, use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArc center\u003C/span\u003E: click the center, then where the arc starts (or type the radius), then where it ends (or type the angle in degrees, such as \u003Ccode\u003E90\u003C/code\u003E). It snaps to 15° steps.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAn arc started on a face is drawn on that face; otherwise it lies on the ground, or stands upright when its two ends are at different heights. An arc has 12 segments; type \u003Ccode\u003E24s\u003C/code\u003E while drawing for more. Arcs close faces just as lines do: an arc across the corner of a rectangle splits it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-a-smooth-curve\"\u003EDraw a smooth curve\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECurve\u003C/span\u003E draws a smooth line that passes through every point you click: a cable, a handrail&#039;s path, a garden edge. It can leave the ground: points snap to corners, edges and faces anywhere, and the arrow keys lock red, green or blue as with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELine\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECurve\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraw\u003C/span\u003E group.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the points the curve goes through. Type \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;0,0,1&gt;\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for a point 1 above the last one, \u003Ccode\u003E[x,y,z]\u003C/code\u003E for an exact place, or a length for a point that far toward the pointer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or double-click to finish. Click the first point again to close the curve. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes the last point back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA curve selects as one piece. To change it, select it and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit points\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E: its points show as squares. Click one, then click where it goes (or type \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;dx,dy,dz&gt;\u003C/code\u003E). \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E puts a point back, and ends editing. A closed flat curve makes a face; a curve that outlines a face can no longer be edited.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"offset-a-border-inside-or-outside-a-face\"\u003EOffset: a border inside or outside a face\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffset\u003C/span\u003E copies a face&#039;s outline a set distance inside it (or outside it), for a frame, a border, a sill or the glass of a screen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and click the face, or select the face first and then press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove the pointer into the face for an inner outline, or out of it for an outer one. The new outline follows, and the tip says how far.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick, or type the distance and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe face splits in two: the inner face and a frame around it (or a new ring outside). Push/pull either one straight away: for a screen, draw the box, push it to its thickness, offset the front face 30 mm inward and push the inner face in a little for the glass. Holes in the face are offset too, and an arc or a circle stays one (smaller or larger) curve.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDouble-click\u003C/strong\u003E a face with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffset\u003C/span\u003E to offset it by the last distance again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEdges:\u003C/strong\u003E select a chain of connected edges in one plane (for example the two lines of an L), press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, move to the side you want and type the distance: a parallel copy is drawn, with mitred corners.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhen the distance is more than the face allows, ModelCraft says so and draws nothing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"arcs-circles-and-polygons-are-curves\"\u003EArcs, circles and polygons are curves\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick any segment of an arc, a circle or a polygon and the whole curve is selected. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E then shows its radius, its segments, its angle (arcs) and its length. Type a new radius there to resize it about its center; faces drawn in its plane follow. Once a curve&#039;s face has been pushed or pulled, its radius stays as it is.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-with-lines\"\u003EDraw with lines\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"L\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick where the line starts.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick where it ends, or type a length and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The typed length goes in the direction the pointer shows.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EKeep clicking. Each click starts the next line from the end of the last one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClose the outline by clicking the first point again. When the lines close a flat loop, it fills with a face and the tool stops chaining. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to stop at any time.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELines follow the red, green and blue directions when you get close to them; the line turns that color and the tip says &quot;On red axis&quot;. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/inference\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/inference\"\u003EInference and snapping\u003C/a\u003E for how to lock a direction and snap to points.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"where-a-line-goes-when-it-touches-nothing\"\u003EWhere a line goes when it touches nothing\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA line that starts on a face stays in that face&#039;s plane.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA line that starts on the ground, or in empty space, stays level with its start point. If you look at the model edge-on, it goes in the vertical plane facing you instead.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo draw a line to an exact place, type a point: \u003Ccode\u003E[x,y,z]\u003C/code\u003E for a point in the model, or \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;dx,dy,dz&gt;\u003C/code\u003E for an offset from the start. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/value-box\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/value-box\"\u003EType exact sizes\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"split-a-face\"\u003ESplit a face\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDraw a line across a face, from one edge to another (or from edge to edge through other lines). The face splits into two faces. Each keeps its side and color. The same works with a rectangle or circle drawn on a face: the shape becomes its own face inside the bigger one, which now has a hole.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis is how you lay out rooms on a floor slab, a window on a wall, or a step on a stair: draw the outline on the face, then push or pull the new face.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"faces-facing-the-wrong-way\"\u003EFaces facing the wrong way\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery face has a front and a back. The back shows blue-grey. Faces drawn on the ground face up; faces drawn on another face face the same way it does.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf a face shows its back where you expect the front, select it and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReverse face\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-see-while-drawing\"\u003EWhat you see while drawing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA colored marker where the pointer snaps, and its name in the tip at the bottom left.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA preview of the line or shape.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe live size in the value box. You can type over it at any time.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"if-the-shape-is-not-where-you-expected\"\u003EIf the shape is not where you expected\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn a touch screen, see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/troubleshooting#a-shape-starts-in-the-wrong-place-on-a-touch-screen\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/troubleshooting\" data-hash=\"a-shape-starts-in-the-wrong-place-on-a-touch-screen\"\u003ETroubleshooting\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf a line snapped to something far behind, orbit a little: points hidden behind a face never snap, but points on its edge do.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to undo the last shape; one undo removes one line or one shape.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Everything in ModelCraft starts as a flat face: a surface closed by edges. You can draw a face directly with Rectangle, Circle or Polygon, or outline one edge at a time with Line. Faces are what Push/pull turns into solids. Draw a rectangle Press R. Click the first corner: on the ground, or on any face of the model. Move the pointer. The rectangle stays on the ground (or on the face you started on), and the value box (Dimensions) shows its width and depth. The tip says \"Square\" when the sides are equal. Click the opposite corner, or type the two sizes separated by a comma and press Enter: 4m,3m, 4000,3000 in a millimeter model, or 12',10'6\" in feet and inches. A face appears with four edges around it. The typed sizes go in the direction you were pointing: if the pointer is to the left of the first corner when you type, the rectangle grows to the left. The comma separates the two sizes, so it cannot be a decimal mark. Type 2.5m,3m, never 2,5m. Draw a circle or a polygon Press C for a circle or N for a polygon. To change the number of sides, type it before you click: 12s (or just 12) and Enter. A circle starts with 24 sides and a polygon with 6. The tip shows the current number, and ModelCraft keeps it for the next circle until you change it or switch tools. Click the center, on the ground or on a face. Click to set the radius, or type it (Radius in the value box) and press Enter. A circle is a polygon with many sides. When you pull a circle up with Push/pull, its sides are softened so the cylinder reads as one smooth surface. Draw an arc Press A (Arc). Click where the arc starts, then where it ends (or type the length between them). Move the pointer to bulge the arc out, and click. The tip says \"Half circle\" when the bulge is half the distance between the ends. Or type how far the middle stands off: 2 and Enter. To draw an arc around a point instead, use Arc center: click the center, then where the arc starts (or type the radius), then where it ends (or type the angle in degrees, such as 90). It snaps to 15° steps. An arc started on a face is drawn on that face; otherwise it lies on the ground, or stands upright when its two ends are at different heights. An arc has 12 segments; type 24s while drawing for more. Arcs close faces just as lines do: an arc across the corner of a rectangle splits it. Draw a smooth curve Curve draws a smooth line that passes through every point you click: a cable, a handrail's path, a garden edge. It can leave the ground: points snap to corners, edges and faces anywhere, and the arrow keys lock red, green or blue as with Line. Click Curve in the Draw group. Click the points the curve goes through. Type \u003C0,0,1\u003E and Enter for a point 1 above the last one, [x,y,z] for an exact place, or a length for a point that far toward the pointer. Press Enter or double-click to finish. Click the first point again to close the curve. Backspace takes the last point back. A curve selects as one piece. To change it, select it and click Edit points in Entity info: its points show as squares. Click one, then click where it goes (or type \u003Cdx,dy,dz\u003E). Esc puts a point back, and ends editing. A closed flat curve makes a face; a curve that outlines a face can no longer be edited. Offset: a border inside or outside a face Offset copies a face's outline a set distance inside it (or outside it), for a frame, a border, a sill or the glass of a screen. Press F and click the face, or select the face first and then press F. Move the pointer into the face for an inner outline, or out of it for an outer one. The new outline follows, and the tip says how far. Click, or type the distance and press Enter. The face splits in two: the inner face and a frame around it (or a new ring outside). Push/pull either one straight away: for a screen, draw the box, push it to its thickness, offset the front face 30 mm inward and push the inner face in a little for the glass. Holes in the face are offset too, and an arc or a circle stays one (smaller or larger) curve. Double-click a face with Offset to offset it by the last distance again. Edges: select a chain of connected edges in one plane (for example the two lines of an L), press F, move to the side you want and type the distance: a parallel copy is drawn, with mitred corners. When the distance is more than the face allows, ModelCraft says so and draws nothing. Arcs, circles and polygons are curves Click any segment of an arc, a circle or a polygon and the whole curve is selected. Entity info then shows its radius, its segments, its angle (arcs) and its length. Type a new radius there to resize it about its center; faces drawn in its plane follow. Once a curve's face has been pushed or pulled, its radius stays as it is. Draw with lines Press L. Click where the line starts. Click where it ends, or type a length and press Enter. The typed length goes in the direction the pointer shows. Keep clicking. Each click starts the next line from the end of the last one. Close the outline by clicking the first point again. When the lines close a flat loop, it fills with a face and the tool stops chaining. Press Esc to stop at any time. Lines follow the red, green and blue directions when you get close to them; the line turns that color and the tip says \"On red axis\". See Inference and snapping for how to lock a direction and snap to points. Where a line goes when it touches nothing A line that starts on a face stays in that face's plane. A line that starts on the ground, or in empty space, stays level with its start point. If you look at the model edge-on, it goes in the vertical plane facing you instead. To draw a line to an exact place, type a point: [x,y,z] for a point in the model, or \u003Cdx,dy,dz\u003E for an offset from the start. See Type exact sizes. Split a face Draw a line across a face, from one edge to another (or from edge to edge through other lines). The face splits into two faces. Each keeps its side and color. The same works with a rectangle or circle drawn on a face: the shape becomes its own face inside the bigger one, which now has a hole. This is how you lay out rooms on a floor slab, a window on a wall, or a step on a stair: draw the outline on the face, then push or pull the new face. Faces facing the wrong way Every face has a front and a back. The back shows blue-grey. Faces drawn on the ground face up; faces drawn on another face face the same way it does. If a face shows its back where you expect the front, select it and click Reverse face in Entity info. What you see while drawing A colored marker where the pointer snaps, and its name in the tip at the bottom left. A preview of the line or shape. The live size in the value box. You can type over it at any time. If the shape is not where you expected On a touch screen, see Troubleshooting. If a line snapped to something far behind, orbit a little: points hidden behind a face never snap, but points on its edge do. Press Ctrl+Z to undo the last shape; one undo removes one line or one shape."},{"slug":"push-pull","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Push and pull faces into solids","summary":"Turn a face into a box, make a building taller or shorter, add a bay or a recess, and repeat a distance with a double-click.","section":"tasks","order":30,"keywords":["push pull","extrude","height","solid","box","mass","taller","recess","bump out","repeat","double-click","volume"],"headings":[{"id":"make-a-solid-from-a-face","text":"Make a solid from a face","level":2},{"id":"pull-to-the-height-of-something-else","text":"Pull to the height of something else","level":2},{"id":"make-a-box-taller-or-shorter","text":"Make a box taller or shorter","level":2},{"id":"add-a-bay-or-a-recess","text":"Add a bay or a recess","level":2},{"id":"push-all-the-way-through","text":"Push all the way through","level":2},{"id":"type-a-distance","text":"Type a distance","level":2},{"id":"repeat-the-last-distance","text":"Repeat the last distance","level":2},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPush/pull\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"P\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) moves a face along its own direction: up and down for a floor, in and out for a wall. What happens depends on the face.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"make-a-solid-from-a-face\"\u003EMake a solid from a face\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDraw a face, for example a rectangle on the ground (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/draw-faces\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/draw-faces\"\u003EDraw faces\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"P\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EHover the face. It highlights.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the face, then move the pointer. The face rises (or sinks) and sides grow from its edges. The value box (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDistance\u003C/span\u003E) and the tip show the distance.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick to finish, or type a distance and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003E3m\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E10&#039;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E2700\u003C/code\u003E in a millimeter model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou now have a closed solid. Select it all and group it (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/groups-components\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/groups-components\"\u003EGroups and components\u003C/a\u003E): \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E then shows &quot;Solid: Yes&quot; and its volume.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA lone face can go either way: pull it up for a mass above the ground, push it down for a pit or a foundation.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EInstead of clicking twice, press on the face, drag, and release. On a touch screen that is the natural way: press, drag, lift.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"pull-to-the-height-of-something-else\"\u003EPull to the height of something else\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhile you pull, hover a corner, a midpoint, an edge or a guide of another part of the model. The face snaps to that height and a marker shows the point. Use it to make two blocks the same height, or to stop at a guide line you placed with the \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/guides-measure\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/guides-measure\"\u003Etape measure\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHovering a face does not snap; the height follows the pointer.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"make-a-box-taller-or-shorter\"\u003EMake a box taller or shorter\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPush/pull the top of a box. Because the sides already run straight up from it, the top simply moves: the box gets taller or shorter, and no new edges are made.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPushing the top all the way down to the bottom removes the box. ModelCraft stops at zero height; it never turns the box inside out.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe same goes for any face whose neighbors all run along its direction, such as the end of a wall.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"add-a-bay-or-a-recess\"\u003EAdd a bay or a recess\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDraw a rectangle on the side of a box (start it on the face; it stays in the face&#039;s plane).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"P\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and pull the new rectangle \u003Cstrong\u003Eout\u003C/strong\u003E. It adds material: a bay, a chimney, a canopy.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOr push it \u003Cstrong\u003Ein\u003C/strong\u003E. It cuts a recess: a porch, a niche, a set-back entrance.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen the sides of the new part line up with faces already there, they merge into one face, so you don&#039;t get stray lines on a flat wall.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"push-all-the-way-through\"\u003EPush all the way through\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPushing a face into a solid stops at the first face behind it. When it reaches that face, it cuts an opening through. That is how you make windows and doors in a wall with thickness. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/openings\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/openings\"\u003ECut an opening through a wall\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"type-a-distance\"\u003EType a distance\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAfter clicking the face, type the distance and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe typed distance goes the way you were moving. If you haven&#039;t moved yet, it goes toward the front of the face (out of a solid, up from the ground).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType a negative number, such as \u003Ccode\u003E-500\u003C/code\u003E, to go the other way.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf you change your mind before clicking, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The face goes back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"repeat-the-last-distance\"\u003ERepeat the last distance\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAfter one push/pull, double-click another face with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPush/pull\u003C/span\u003E still selected. It moves by the same distance. This is the fast way to give several walls or several floors the same thickness or height.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EModelCraft remembers the distance until you switch to another tool.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPush/pull works on one face at a time. If you click something that is not a face, the status bar says &quot;Push/pull works on a face. Click a face.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo push/pull a face inside a group or component, open it first (double-click it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETwo solids pushed into each other do not cut or join each other: their faces just pass through. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Push/pull (P) moves a face along its own direction: up and down for a floor, in and out for a wall. What happens depends on the face. Make a solid from a face Draw a face, for example a rectangle on the ground (Draw faces). Press P. Hover the face. It highlights. Click the face, then move the pointer. The face rises (or sinks) and sides grow from its edges. The value box (Distance) and the tip show the distance. Click to finish, or type a distance and press Enter: 3m, 10', 2700 in a millimeter model. You now have a closed solid. Select it all and group it (see Groups and components): Entity info then shows \"Solid: Yes\" and its volume. A lone face can go either way: pull it up for a mass above the ground, push it down for a pit or a foundation. Instead of clicking twice, press on the face, drag, and release. On a touch screen that is the natural way: press, drag, lift. Pull to the height of something else While you pull, hover a corner, a midpoint, an edge or a guide of another part of the model. The face snaps to that height and a marker shows the point. Use it to make two blocks the same height, or to stop at a guide line you placed with the tape measure. Hovering a face does not snap; the height follows the pointer. Make a box taller or shorter Push/pull the top of a box. Because the sides already run straight up from it, the top simply moves: the box gets taller or shorter, and no new edges are made. Pushing the top all the way down to the bottom removes the box. ModelCraft stops at zero height; it never turns the box inside out. The same goes for any face whose neighbors all run along its direction, such as the end of a wall. Add a bay or a recess Draw a rectangle on the side of a box (start it on the face; it stays in the face's plane). Press P and pull the new rectangle out. It adds material: a bay, a chimney, a canopy. Or push it in. It cuts a recess: a porch, a niche, a set-back entrance. When the sides of the new part line up with faces already there, they merge into one face, so you don't get stray lines on a flat wall. Push all the way through Pushing a face into a solid stops at the first face behind it. When it reaches that face, it cuts an opening through. That is how you make windows and doors in a wall with thickness. See Cut an opening through a wall. Type a distance After clicking the face, type the distance and press Enter. The typed distance goes the way you were moving. If you haven't moved yet, it goes toward the front of the face (out of a solid, up from the ground). Type a negative number, such as -500, to go the other way. If you change your mind before clicking, press Esc. The face goes back. Repeat the last distance After one push/pull, double-click another face with Push/pull still selected. It moves by the same distance. This is the fast way to give several walls or several floors the same thickness or height. ModelCraft remembers the distance until you switch to another tool. Things to know Push/pull works on one face at a time. If you click something that is not a face, the status bar says \"Push/pull works on a face. Click a face.\" To push/pull a face inside a group or component, open it first (double-click it). Two solids pushed into each other do not cut or join each other: their faces just pass through. See Not built yet."},{"slug":"follow-me","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Follow me: sweep a face along a path","summary":"Make pipes, cables, handrails, moldings and rings by sweeping a face along edges, arcs or a curve.","section":"tasks","order":35,"keywords":["follow me","sweep","path","pipe","cable","tube","handrail","molding","moulding","gutter","ring","torus","lathe","extrude along"],"headings":[{"id":"make-a-cable","text":"Make a cable","level":2},{"id":"without-selecting-the-path-first","text":"Without selecting the path first","level":2},{"id":"what-to-know","text":"What to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFollow me\u003C/span\u003E moves a face along a path and builds the solid it passes through. The face keeps its shape and stays square to the path, and at each corner of the path the pieces meet on a clean mitre.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"make-a-cable\"\u003EMake a cable\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDraw the path: a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECurve\u003C/span\u003E through the points the cable passes (it can rise and fall), or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELine\u003C/span\u003Es and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArc\u003C/span\u003Es joined end to end.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt the start of the path, draw the cable&#039;s section: a small \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECircle\u003C/span\u003E across the path. Start the circle on the path&#039;s end point and turn it to face along the path; or draw it on a face that crosses the path there.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect the path (one click selects a whole curve or arc; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click adds more edges).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFollow me\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModify\u003C/span\u003E group, then click the circle.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe tube is a closed solid, with smooth (soft) sides.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"without-selecting-the-path-first\"\u003EWithout selecting the path first\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFollow me\u003C/span\u003E, click the face, then click any edge of the path. The path is the chain of edges through that edge, up to where three or more edges meet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-to-know\"\u003EWhat to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EClosed paths\u003C/strong\u003E (a circle, a closed curve, a rectangle&#039;s outline) make a ring: the face is used up and the ring closes on itself. Draw a circle on the ground for the path and a small circle standing across it for a donut.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOff the path:\u003C/strong\u003E the face does not have to touch the path. A profile drawn beside a path sweeps along beside it, like a molding along the top of a wall.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe path must not branch\u003C/strong\u003E, and must be one connected chain. ModelCraft says so when it is not.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA face lying along the path\u003C/strong\u003E (in the same plane as the path&#039;s first edge) cannot be swept: turn it to cross the path.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIt is one undo step.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Follow me moves a face along a path and builds the solid it passes through. The face keeps its shape and stays square to the path, and at each corner of the path the pieces meet on a clean mitre. Make a cable Draw the path: a Curve through the points the cable passes (it can rise and fall), or Lines and Arcs joined end to end. At the start of the path, draw the cable's section: a small Circle across the path. Start the circle on the path's end point and turn it to face along the path; or draw it on a face that crosses the path there. Select the path (one click selects a whole curve or arc; Ctrl+click adds more edges). Click Follow me in the Modify group, then click the circle. The tube is a closed solid, with smooth (soft) sides. Without selecting the path first Click Follow me, click the face, then click any edge of the path. The path is the chain of edges through that edge, up to where three or more edges meet. What to know Closed paths (a circle, a closed curve, a rectangle's outline) make a ring: the face is used up and the ring closes on itself. Draw a circle on the ground for the path and a small circle standing across it for a donut. Off the path: the face does not have to touch the path. A profile drawn beside a path sweeps along beside it, like a molding along the top of a wall. The path must not branch, and must be one connected chain. ModelCraft says so when it is not. A face lying along the path (in the same plane as the path's first edge) cannot be swept: turn it to cross the path. It is one undo step."},{"slug":"text3d","scope":"modelcraft","title":"3D text","summary":"Add lettering to a model as faces, flat or solid, standing on any face: a sign, a house number, a name on a door.","section":"tasks","order":36,"keywords":["3d text","text","letters","lettering","sign","signage","house number","label","engrave","font","words"],"headings":[{"id":"add-text","text":"Add text","level":2},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D Text\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraw\u003C/span\u003E group makes lettering out of faces, which you can paint, push/pull or cut into a solid.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"add-text\"\u003EAdd text\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D Text\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType the text. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E in the box for a second line.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELetter height\u003C/span\u003E (the height of a capital letter, such as \u003Ccode\u003E30cm\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E1&#039;\u003C/code\u003E) and the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDepth\u003C/span\u003E:\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E0\u003C/code\u003E gives \u003Cstrong\u003Eflat letters\u003C/strong\u003E: faces lying on whatever you place them on, ready to paint or to push/pull.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA depth such as \u003Ccode\u003E5cm\u003C/code\u003E gives \u003Cstrong\u003Esolid letters\u003C/strong\u003E, closed solids you can use with the \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/solids\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/solids\"\u003Esolid tools\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlace\u003C/span\u003E, then click where the text goes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a face, the text lies on that face, reading left to right: on a wall it stands upright; on the ground or a table it lies flat along red. Away from any face it lies on the ground.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe text is a \u003Cstrong\u003Ecomponent\u003C/strong\u003E named after it, so every copy changes if you edit one, and it shows in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EComponents\u003C/span\u003E list to place again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe letters are ModelCraft&#039;s own single-stroke font, thickened: clean and readable, with no choice of typeface yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELetters, digits and common signs (including accented letters) are drawn; a character the font does not have shows as a small box.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo change the words, delete the text and add it again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo cut lettering into a surface, make solid letters, place them half-way into a solid group, and \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/solids\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/solids\"\u003Esubtract\u003C/a\u003E them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"3D Text in the Draw group makes lettering out of faces, which you can paint, push/pull or cut into a solid. Add text Click 3D Text. Type the text. Press Enter in the box for a second line. Set the Letter height (the height of a capital letter, such as 30cm or 1') and the Depth: 0 gives flat letters: faces lying on whatever you place them on, ready to paint or to push/pull. A depth such as 5cm gives solid letters, closed solids you can use with the solid tools. Click Place, then click where the text goes. On a face, the text lies on that face, reading left to right: on a wall it stands upright; on the ground or a table it lies flat along red. Away from any face it lies on the ground. The text is a component named after it, so every copy changes if you edit one, and it shows in the Components list to place again. Things to know The letters are ModelCraft's own single-stroke font, thickened: clean and readable, with no choice of typeface yet. Letters, digits and common signs (including accented letters) are drawn; a character the font does not have shows as a small box. To change the words, delete the text and add it again. To cut lettering into a surface, make solid letters, place them half-way into a solid group, and subtract them."},{"slug":"starter","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Starter library","summary":"About thirty ready-made things to place in a model: doors and windows, furniture, kitchen and bath, columns and stairs, trees, people and a car.","section":"tasks","order":39,"keywords":["library","starter","components","furniture","door","window","chair","table","sofa","bed","kitchen","bathroom","toilet","stair","column","tree","person","car","warehouse","ready-made"],"headings":[{"id":"place-one","text":"Place one","level":2},{"id":"what-is-in-it","text":"What is in it","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStarter library\u003C/span\u003E gives you everyday things at real sizes, so a model reads at a glance: a door to show the scale of a room, a car in a driveway, people and trees around a building.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"place-one\"\u003EPlace one\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EComponents\u003C/span\u003E on the left, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStarter library\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick a thing, such as \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChair\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick where it goes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a floor, a table or the ground, it stands on it. \u003Cstrong\u003EDoors and windows\u003C/strong\u003E are made to go in walls: click on a wall and they stand in it, facing out. (They sit on the wall&#039;s face; they do not cut an opening. To make one, draw a rectangle on the wall where the window goes and push it through.)\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach thing is a \u003Cstrong\u003Ecomponent\u003C/strong\u003E: picking the same one again places another copy, and every copy changes if you edit one. It also shows in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EComponents\u003C/span\u003E list, with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlace\u003C/span\u003E to add more.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-is-in-it\"\u003EWhat is in it\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EGroup\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EThings\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDoors and windows\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDoor, double door, window, wide window, garage door\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFurniture\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDining table, round table, chair, bar stool, sofa, armchair, double and single bed, desk, bookshelf, wardrobe\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EKitchen and bath\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBase and wall cabinet, kitchen counter, fridge, stove, sink unit, toilet, bathtub, shower, washbasin\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBuilding\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStraight stair (15 risers of 18 cm), round and square column, beam\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESite and people\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETree, shrub, person (1.75 m), car\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThey are simple shapes, made of solids you can paint, scale or \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/groups-components\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/groups-components\"\u003Emake unique\u003C/a\u003E and change. The wall cabinet hangs at 1.45 m above where you click.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The Starter library gives you everyday things at real sizes, so a model reads at a glance: a door to show the scale of a room, a car in a driveway, people and trees around a building. Place one In Components on the left, click Starter library. Pick a thing, such as Chair. Click where it goes. On a floor, a table or the ground, it stands on it. Doors and windows are made to go in walls: click on a wall and they stand in it, facing out. (They sit on the wall's face; they do not cut an opening. To make one, draw a rectangle on the wall where the window goes and push it through.) Each thing is a component: picking the same one again places another copy, and every copy changes if you edit one. It also shows in the Components list, with Place to add more. What is in it Group Things Doors and windows Door, double door, window, wide window, garage door Furniture Dining table, round table, chair, bar stool, sofa, armchair, double and single bed, desk, bookshelf, wardrobe Kitchen and bath Base and wall cabinet, kitchen counter, fridge, stove, sink unit, toilet, bathtub, shower, washbasin Building Straight stair (15 risers of 18 cm), round and square column, beam Site and people Tree, shrub, person (1.75 m), car They are simple shapes, made of solids you can paint, scale or make unique and change. The wall cabinet hangs at 1.45 m above where you click."},{"slug":"openings","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Cut an opening through a wall","summary":"Draw a window or door on a wall with thickness and push it through, so the wall gets a real hole.","section":"tasks","order":40,"keywords":["window","door","opening","hole","wall","cut","push through","void","thickness","facade"],"headings":[{"id":"make-a-wall-to-practice-on","text":"Make a wall to practice on","level":2},{"id":"draw-the-opening","text":"Draw the opening","level":2},{"id":"push-it-through","text":"Push it through","level":2},{"id":"a-door","text":"A door","level":2},{"id":"many-windows-of-the-same-size","text":"Many windows of the same size","level":2},{"id":"if-it-did-not-cut-through","text":"If it did not cut through","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EAn opening needs a wall with two sides: a box, or a wall you made by pulling a long thin rectangle up. A single face standing on its own has nothing to push through to.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"make-a-wall-to-practice-on\"\u003EMake a wall to practice on\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, click a corner on the ground and type \u003Ccode\u003E6m,0.3m\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003E20&#039;,12&quot;\u003C/code\u003E), then \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"P\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, click the rectangle and type \u003Ccode\u003E3m\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003E10&#039;\u003C/code\u003E), then \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou have a wall 300 mm (12 in) thick.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-the-opening\"\u003EDraw the opening\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOrbit so you can see the long side of the wall.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EHover the wall&#039;s face, then click where the window&#039;s lower corner goes. The tip says &quot;On face&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType the window&#039;s width and height, such as \u003Ccode\u003E1.2m,1.5m\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003E4&#039;,5&#039;\u003C/code\u003E), and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe window is a face of its own inside the wall face, which now has a hole around it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo place it at an exact distance from the corner and the floor, use \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/guides-measure\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/guides-measure\"\u003Eguides\u003C/a\u003E first: with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETape\u003C/span\u003E, drag guide lines off the wall&#039;s bottom and end edges, then start the rectangle where two guides cross (the tip says &quot;On guide&quot;).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"push-it-through\"\u003EPush it through\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"P\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the window face and push it into the wall.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove until it stops at the back face, then click. Or type the wall thickness, \u003Ccode\u003E300\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003E12\u003C/code\u003E), and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EModelCraft stops at the face behind and opens it: the wall now has a hole straight through, with four side faces (jambs, head and sill) lining it. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E still calls the wall a closed solid when it is grouped.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf you push less than the full thickness, you get a recess instead of a hole.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-door\"\u003EA door\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDraw the rectangle starting on the bottom edge of the wall face (the tip says &quot;On edge&quot; or &quot;Endpoint&quot;), so the opening reaches the floor. Push it through the same way.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"many-windows-of-the-same-size\"\u003EMany windows of the same size\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMake the first opening.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFor the next ones, draw the rectangles, then double-click each one with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPush/pull\u003C/span\u003E: a double-click repeats the last distance. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/push-pull#repeat-the-last-distance\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/push-pull\" data-hash=\"repeat-the-last-distance\"\u003EPush and pull\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"if-it-did-not-cut-through\"\u003EIf it did not cut through\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe rectangle must be drawn \u003Cstrong\u003Eon\u003C/strong\u003E the wall&#039;s face. If it was drawn in the air in front of it, it is a separate face: undo and draw it again, starting on the face.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe push stops at the first face behind the opening. If something else is in the way inside the wall (an inner face left from earlier drawing), it stops there; erase that face&#039;s edges and try again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EGeometry in a separate group is not cut. Open the group that holds the wall and draw the opening inside it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"An opening needs a wall with two sides: a box, or a wall you made by pulling a long thin rectangle up. A single face standing on its own has nothing to push through to. Make a wall to practice on Press R, click a corner on the ground and type 6m,0.3m (or 20',12\"), then Enter. Press P, click the rectangle and type 3m (or 10'), then Enter. You have a wall 300 mm (12 in) thick. Draw the opening Orbit so you can see the long side of the wall. Press R. Hover the wall's face, then click where the window's lower corner goes. The tip says \"On face\". Type the window's width and height, such as 1.2m,1.5m (or 4',5'), and press Enter. The window is a face of its own inside the wall face, which now has a hole around it. To place it at an exact distance from the corner and the floor, use guides first: with Tape, drag guide lines off the wall's bottom and end edges, then start the rectangle where two guides cross (the tip says \"On guide\"). Push it through Press P. Click the window face and push it into the wall. Move until it stops at the back face, then click. Or type the wall thickness, 300 (or 12), and press Enter. ModelCraft stops at the face behind and opens it: the wall now has a hole straight through, with four side faces (jambs, head and sill) lining it. Entity info still calls the wall a closed solid when it is grouped. If you push less than the full thickness, you get a recess instead of a hole. A door Draw the rectangle starting on the bottom edge of the wall face (the tip says \"On edge\" or \"Endpoint\"), so the opening reaches the floor. Push it through the same way. Many windows of the same size Make the first opening. For the next ones, draw the rectangles, then double-click each one with Push/pull: a double-click repeats the last distance. See Push and pull. If it did not cut through The rectangle must be drawn on the wall's face. If it was drawn in the air in front of it, it is a separate face: undo and draw it again, starting on the face. The push stops at the first face behind the opening. If something else is in the way inside the wall (an inner face left from earlier drawing), it stops there; erase that face's edges and try again. Geometry in a separate group is not cut. Open the group that holds the wall and draw the opening inside it."},{"slug":"stretch","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Components that stretch","summary":"Make one copy of a component wider, deeper or taller while its parts keep their sizes: a longer table with the same legs, a taller bookshelf with its shelves spread out, a railing whose posts repeat.","section":"tasks","order":41,"keywords":["dynamic component","stretch","resize component","parametric","named sizes","repeat","array","legs","shelves","railing","rules","variables"],"headings":[{"id":"stretch-a-copy","text":"Stretch a copy","level":2},{"id":"the-rules","text":"The rules","level":2},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EScaling a component makes everything in it bigger, legs and rails too. \u003Cstrong\u003EStretch this copy\u003C/strong\u003E changes a component&#039;s width, depth or height while each part follows a rule: a table top grows, its legs keep their thickness and stay at the ends.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt works on components made of parts, that is, groups or components inside the component, as every \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/starter\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/starter\"\u003Estarter library\u003C/a\u003E thing is.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"stretch-a-copy\"\u003EStretch a copy\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect the component.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStretch this copy…\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType the new \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWidth (red)\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDepth (green)\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHeight (blue)\u003C/span\u003E, and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStretch\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOnly that copy changes: it becomes a component of its own, which remembers the original it came from. Stretch it again at any time, and it is worked out afresh from the original, so nothing drifts. Other copies of the original stay as they were.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-rules\"\u003EThe rules\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen the component (double-click it) and select one of its parts. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E shows \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWhen the component stretches\u003C/span\u003E, with a rule along red, green and blue:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ERule\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat the part does\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFor example\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStretch\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGrows with the component; its distances to both sides stay\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA table top\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStay at the start\u003C/span\u003E / \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStay at the end\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EKeeps its size and its distance to that side\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELegs\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove in proportion\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EKeeps its size; its middle moves in proportion\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShelves, a door handle\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale in proportion\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIts place and size both in proportion\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe steps of a stair\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERepeat\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECopies of it, every so often (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEvery\u003C/span\u003E), from where it starts to the far side\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe posts of a railing\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAutomatic\u003C/span\u003E (the start) works one out and shows which: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStretch\u003C/span\u003E when the part spans the component, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove in proportion\u003C/span\u003E when it sits about in the middle, otherwise it stays at the side it is nearer to.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe parts&#039; own shapes are shared, so a stretched copy is light. Editing a part changes it in every copy.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA stretched copy does not follow later changes to the original&#039;s rules or layout: stretch it again to bring them in.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFaces drawn loose in the component (not in a part) keep their size; make them a part to have them follow.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERules and what each copy was stretched from are saved in the ModelCraft file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Scaling a component makes everything in it bigger, legs and rails too. Stretch this copy changes a component's width, depth or height while each part follows a rule: a table top grows, its legs keep their thickness and stay at the ends. It works on components made of parts, that is, groups or components inside the component, as every starter library thing is. Stretch a copy Select the component. In Entity info, click Stretch this copy…. Type the new Width (red), Depth (green) and Height (blue), and click Stretch. Only that copy changes: it becomes a component of its own, which remembers the original it came from. Stretch it again at any time, and it is worked out afresh from the original, so nothing drifts. Other copies of the original stay as they were. The rules Open the component (double-click it) and select one of its parts. Entity info shows When the component stretches, with a rule along red, green and blue: Rule What the part does For example Stretch Grows with the component; its distances to both sides stay A table top Stay at the start / Stay at the end Keeps its size and its distance to that side Legs Move in proportion Keeps its size; its middle moves in proportion Shelves, a door handle Scale in proportion Its place and size both in proportion The steps of a stair Repeat Copies of it, every so often (Every), from where it starts to the far side The posts of a railing Automatic (the start) works one out and shows which: Stretch when the part spans the component, Move in proportion when it sits about in the middle, otherwise it stays at the side it is nearer to. Things to know The parts' own shapes are shared, so a stretched copy is light. Editing a part changes it in every copy. A stretched copy does not follow later changes to the original's rules or layout: stretch it again to bring them in. Faces drawn loose in the component (not in a part) keep their size; make them a part to have them follow. Rules and what each copy was stretched from are saved in the ModelCraft file."},{"slug":"paint","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Paint with colors","summary":"Give faces, groups and components a solid color to tell objects apart, and keep the colors when you export.","section":"tasks","order":45,"keywords":["paint","color","colour","material","paint bucket","fill","sample","eyedropper","default","glb","obj","mtl","collada"],"headings":[{"id":"pick-a-color","text":"Pick a color","level":2},{"id":"paint","text":"Paint","level":2},{"id":"components-and-colors","text":"Components and colors","level":2},{"id":"colors-in-files","text":"Colors in files","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EColors help you tell parts of a model apart. ModelCraft paints with \u003Cstrong\u003Esolid colors\u003C/strong\u003E; textures are not built yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"pick-a-color\"\u003EPick a color\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EColors\u003C/span\u003E section on the right shows a row of solid colors, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDefault\u003C/span\u003E (the one crossed by a line: no color) and a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E+\u003C/span\u003E swatch for any other color. Click one to make it the current color. The Paint tool starts with it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"paint\"\u003EPaint\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"B\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaint\u003C/span\u003E), or click a color.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a face to paint it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a group or a component to paint that copy: its faces that have no color of their own take this color. Faces you painted inside it keep theirs.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo paint several things at once, select them first and then click a color, or click any of them with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaint\u003C/span\u003E. It is one undo step.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EHold\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EAnd click a face\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat gets painted\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E—\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThat face\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEvery face connected to it that has the same color\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEvery face of that color in the group you are working in\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option\"\u003E⌥\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENothing: you pick up that color instead\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo take a face back to no color, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDefault\u003C/span\u003E and click it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"components-and-colors\"\u003EComponents and colors\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA color painted on a face \u003Cstrong\u003Einside\u003C/strong\u003E a component shows on every copy, because the copies share one definition. A color painted on a \u003Cstrong\u003Ecopy\u003C/strong\u003E from outside (with the component closed) belongs to that copy only, so two copies of one component can have different colors. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E shows the color of a selected face, group or component.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"colors-in-files\"\u003EColors in files\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe ModelCraft file keeps every color.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EglTF (.glb)\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003ECollada (.dae)\u003C/strong\u003E: each color becomes a material. A component shown in different colors is written once per color.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOBJ\u003C/strong\u003E: exporting downloads two files, the \u003Ccode\u003E.obj\u003C/code\u003E and a \u003Ccode\u003E.mtl\u003C/code\u003E materials file with the colors. Keep them in the same folder.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDWG\u003C/strong\u003E keeps the exact colors; \u003Cstrong\u003EDXF\u003C/strong\u003E uses the nearest of the 255 standard CAD colors.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESTL\u003C/strong\u003E has no colors.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo model a screen, paint the frame charcoal and the glass blue after you \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/draw-faces\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/draw-faces\"\u003Eoffset\u003C/a\u003E the front face.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"Colors help you tell parts of a model apart. ModelCraft paints with solid colors; textures are not built yet. Pick a color The Colors section on the right shows a row of solid colors, Default (the one crossed by a line: no color) and a + swatch for any other color. Click one to make it the current color. The Paint tool starts with it. Paint Press B (Paint), or click a color. Click a face to paint it. Click a group or a component to paint that copy: its faces that have no color of their own take this color. Faces you painted inside it keep theirs. To paint several things at once, select them first and then click a color, or click any of them with Paint. It is one undo step. Hold And click a face What gets painted — That face Ctrl Every face connected to it that has the same color Shift Every face of that color in the group you are working in Alt Nothing: you pick up that color instead To take a face back to no color, choose Default and click it. Components and colors A color painted on a face inside a component shows on every copy, because the copies share one definition. A color painted on a copy from outside (with the component closed) belongs to that copy only, so two copies of one component can have different colors. Entity info shows the color of a selected face, group or component. Colors in files The ModelCraft file keeps every color. glTF (.glb) and Collada (.dae): each color becomes a material. A component shown in different colors is written once per color. OBJ: exporting downloads two files, the .obj and a .mtl materials file with the colors. Keep them in the same folder. DWG keeps the exact colors; DXF uses the nearest of the 255 standard CAD colors. STL has no colors. To model a screen, paint the frame charcoal and the glass blue after you offset the front face."},{"slug":"select","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Select things","summary":"Click, double-click and triple-click, drag a window or a crossing box, add and remove with modifier keys, and delete.","section":"tasks","order":45,"keywords":["select","selection","window","crossing","add to selection","deselect","select all","triple click","double click","delete","erase"],"headings":[{"id":"click","text":"Click","level":2},{"id":"drag-a-box","text":"Drag a box","level":2},{"id":"add-and-remove","text":"Add and remove","level":2},{"id":"clear-the-selection","text":"Clear the selection","level":2},{"id":"delete-what-is-selected","text":"Delete what is selected","level":2},{"id":"select-inside-a-group","text":"Select inside a group","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) is the tool ModelCraft starts with, and the one \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E brings you back to. Selected things turn blue, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E on the right says what they are.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"click\"\u003EClick\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDo this\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESelects\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick a face or an edge\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThat face or edge\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick a group or component\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe whole group or component\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDouble-click a face\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe face and its edges\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDouble-click an edge\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe edge and the faces on either side\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDouble-click a group or component\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpens it for editing (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/groups-components\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/groups-components\"\u003EGroups and components\u003C/a\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETriple-click a face or edge\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEverything connected to it: the whole object\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick empty space\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENothing (clears the selection)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDouble-click empty space while editing a group\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECloses the group\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEdges are easier to hit than faces: when the pointer is within a few pixels of an edge, the click takes the edge.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"drag-a-box\"\u003EDrag a box\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELeft to right\u003C/strong\u003E draws a solid box: a \u003Cstrong\u003Ewindow\u003C/strong\u003E selection. Only things wholly inside it are selected.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERight to left\u003C/strong\u003E draws a dashed box: a \u003Cstrong\u003Ecrossing\u003C/strong\u003E selection. Anything the box touches is selected, and a face is selected when the box sits entirely inside it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA group or component counts as one thing: a window must hold all of it, a crossing only has to touch it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"add-and-remove\"\u003EAdd and remove\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHold a key while you click or drag:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EEffect\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EToggles: adds what is not selected, removes what is\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAdds\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E together\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERemoves\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo select everything in the model (or everything in the group you are editing), press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command A\"\u003E⌘A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"clear-the-selection\"\u003EClear the selection\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E as the tool), or click empty space.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"delete-what-is-selected\"\u003EDelete what is selected\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Faces, edges, groups and guides in the selection are removed in one step; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E brings them back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDeleting an edge removes the faces that need it, unless the edge only separated two faces in the same plane: then the two faces join into one. Deleting a face leaves its edges. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/erase\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/erase\"\u003EErase and clean up\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"select-inside-a-group\"\u003ESelect inside a group\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClicking a group selects the whole group. To select a face inside it, double-click the group to open it, then click. While a group is open, clicks only reach what is inside it; everything else is faded. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E with nothing selected, or double-click empty space, to close it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOutliner\u003C/span\u003E on the left selects groups and components by name, which helps when they are hidden behind other things.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Select (Space) is the tool ModelCraft starts with, and the one Esc brings you back to. Selected things turn blue, and Entity info on the right says what they are. Click Do this Selects Click a face or an edge That face or edge Click a group or component The whole group or component Double-click a face The face and its edges Double-click an edge The edge and the faces on either side Double-click a group or component Opens it for editing (see Groups and components) Triple-click a face or edge Everything connected to it: the whole object Click empty space Nothing (clears the selection) Double-click empty space while editing a group Closes the group Edges are easier to hit than faces: when the pointer is within a few pixels of an edge, the click takes the edge. Drag a box Left to right draws a solid box: a window selection. Only things wholly inside it are selected. Right to left draws a dashed box: a crossing selection. Anything the box touches is selected, and a face is selected when the box sits entirely inside it. A group or component counts as one thing: a window must hold all of it, a crossing only has to touch it. Add and remove Hold a key while you click or drag: Key Effect Shift Toggles: adds what is not selected, removes what is Ctrl Adds Shift and Ctrl together Removes To select everything in the model (or everything in the group you are editing), press Ctrl+A. Clear the selection Press Esc (with Select as the tool), or click empty space. Delete what is selected Press Delete or Backspace. Faces, edges, groups and guides in the selection are removed in one step; Ctrl+Z brings them back. Deleting an edge removes the faces that need it, unless the edge only separated two faces in the same plane: then the two faces join into one. Deleting a face leaves its edges. See Erase and clean up. Select inside a group Clicking a group selects the whole group. To select a face inside it, double-click the group to open it, then click. While a group is open, clicks only reach what is inside it; everything else is faded. Press Esc with nothing selected, or double-click empty space, to close it. The Outliner on the left selects groups and components by name, which helps when they are hidden behind other things."},{"slug":"solids","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Solid tools: union, subtract, intersect","summary":"Join closed groups and components into one, cut one out of another, or keep only what they share.","section":"tasks","order":46,"keywords":["solid","solids","solid inspector","not solid","fix solid","hole","stray edge","inside out","union","join","combine","merge","subtract","difference","cut","hole","intersect","intersection","boolean","outer shell"],"headings":[{"id":"when-something-is-not-a-solid","text":"When something is not a solid","level":2},{"id":"union","text":"Union","level":2},{"id":"subtract","text":"Subtract","level":2},{"id":"intersect","text":"Intersect","level":2},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe solid tools combine \u003Cstrong\u003Esolids\u003C/strong\u003E: groups and components whose faces close a volume, with every edge joining exactly two faces. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E says \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESolid\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EYes\u003C/span\u003E for one, and shows its volume.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThey are in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESolids\u003C/span\u003E menu in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOrganize\u003C/span\u003E group.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-something-is-not-a-solid\"\u003EWhen something is not a solid\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect the group or component. When it is not a solid, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E says why, for example &quot;Not a solid: 4 edges border a hole · 1 stray edge&quot;:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EProblem\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it means\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdges border a hole\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA face is missing\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdges shared by three faces or more\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA face divides the inside, or two shapes touch along an edge\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFaces inside the shape\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA wall left inside the volume\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStray edges\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELines that bound no face\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFaces turned inside out\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETheir front faces in, against their neighbours\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow problems\u003C/span\u003E opens it with those edges and faces selected, so you can see them. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFix\u003C/span\u003E mends the simple cases in one undo step: it removes stray edges and faces inside the shape, turns faces the right way out, and closes holes whose rim lies flat. Anything it cannot mend safely (a hole that is not flat, an edge shared by many faces) it leaves for you, and says what is left.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"union\"\u003EUnion\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect two or more solids (click one, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click the others).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESolids\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnion\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThey become one solid, in one group. Where they overlapped, the faces inside are gone.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"subtract\"\u003ESubtract\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESolids\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESubtract\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the solid to \u003Cstrong\u003Etake away\u003C/strong\u003E (the cutter).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the solid to \u003Cstrong\u003Ecut\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhat is left of the second solid stays; the cutter is used up. For example, to make a hole through a block, draw a post through it, make the post a group, then subtract the post from the block.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"intersect\"\u003EIntersect\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect two or more solids, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESolids\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIntersect\u003C/span\u003E. Only the part they all share is kept.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe result is a new group. It keeps the name, color and tag of the first solid (for Subtract: the one you cut). Colors painted on its faces are not kept.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFlat sides of the result are single faces. Round sides come out as the flat facets they were drawn with.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf a group or component is not a solid, the tool says which one and changes nothing. Look for a missing face or a stray face inside it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EInside an open group, the tools work on the groups and components in that group.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach tool is one undo step.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe first time you use a solid tool, its engine (manifold-3d) loads, which can take a moment.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"The solid tools combine solids: groups and components whose faces close a volume, with every edge joining exactly two faces. Entity info says Solid: Yes for one, and shows its volume. They are in the Solids menu in the Organize group. When something is not a solid Select the group or component. When it is not a solid, Entity info says why, for example \"Not a solid: 4 edges border a hole · 1 stray edge\": Problem What it means Edges border a hole A face is missing Edges shared by three faces or more A face divides the inside, or two shapes touch along an edge Faces inside the shape A wall left inside the volume Stray edges Lines that bound no face Faces turned inside out Their front faces in, against their neighbours Show problems opens it with those edges and faces selected, so you can see them. Fix mends the simple cases in one undo step: it removes stray edges and faces inside the shape, turns faces the right way out, and closes holes whose rim lies flat. Anything it cannot mend safely (a hole that is not flat, an edge shared by many faces) it leaves for you, and says what is left. Union Select two or more solids (click one, Ctrl+click the others). Solids › Union. They become one solid, in one group. Where they overlapped, the faces inside are gone. Subtract Solids › Subtract. Click the solid to take away (the cutter). Click the solid to cut. What is left of the second solid stays; the cutter is used up. For example, to make a hole through a block, draw a post through it, make the post a group, then subtract the post from the block. Intersect Select two or more solids, then Solids › Intersect. Only the part they all share is kept. Things to know The result is a new group. It keeps the name, color and tag of the first solid (for Subtract: the one you cut). Colors painted on its faces are not kept. Flat sides of the result are single faces. Round sides come out as the flat facets they were drawn with. If a group or component is not a solid, the tool says which one and changes nothing. Look for a missing face or a stray face inside it. Inside an open group, the tools work on the groups and components in that group. Each tool is one undo step. The first time you use a solid tool, its engine (manifold-3d) loads, which can take a moment."},{"slug":"hide-isolate","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Hide, isolate and unhide","summary":"Put things out of the way to work between them, or show only what you are working on; hidden things stay hidden in the file.","section":"tasks","order":47,"keywords":["hide","unhide","unhide all","isolate","hide others","show","hidden","local view","visibility","out of the way"],"headings":[{"id":"hide","text":"Hide","level":2},{"id":"isolate","text":"Isolate","level":2},{"id":"unhide-all","text":"Unhide all","level":2},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EHiding puts things out of the way without erasing them. A hidden face, edge, group or component is not drawn, and you cannot snap to it, click it or select it, so you can work on what is behind or between things.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"hide\"\u003EHide\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect what to hide: faces, edges, groups or components.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHide\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor example, to work in the narrow gap between two groups standing close together, select the front one and hide it: clicks and snaps now reach the face behind it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"isolate\"\u003EIsolate\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect what you want to keep in view, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIsolate (hide the rest)\u003C/span\u003E. Everything else in the model hides — or, while you are editing inside a group, everything else in that group. A selected face keeps its edges.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"unhide-all\"\u003EUnhide all\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnhide all\u003C/span\u003E shows everything again, in every group. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E says how many things are hidden when nothing is selected, with an \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnhide all\u003C/span\u003E button there too.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOutliner\u003C/span\u003E shows hidden groups and components in italics, with a crossed-out eye; click the eye to show one again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith Blender-style keys, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"H\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E hides, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift H\"\u003E⇧H\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+H\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E isolates and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option H\"\u003E⌥H\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt+H\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E unhides all. (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModelCraft\u003C/span\u003E chooses the key style.)\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhat is hidden is saved in the ModelCraft file, and comes back hidden when you open it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EHidden things are still in the model: exports include them, and so do volumes and sizes in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach hide, isolate or unhide is one undo step.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Hiding puts things out of the way without erasing them. A hidden face, edge, group or component is not drawn, and you cannot snap to it, click it or select it, so you can work on what is behind or between things. Hide Select what to hide: faces, edges, groups or components. Edit › More › Hide. For example, to work in the narrow gap between two groups standing close together, select the front one and hide it: clicks and snaps now reach the face behind it. Isolate Select what you want to keep in view, then Edit › More › Isolate (hide the rest). Everything else in the model hides — or, while you are editing inside a group, everything else in that group. A selected face keeps its edges. Unhide all Edit › More › Unhide all shows everything again, in every group. Entity info says how many things are hidden when nothing is selected, with an Unhide all button there too. The Outliner shows hidden groups and components in italics, with a crossed-out eye; click the eye to show one again. With Blender-style keys, H hides, Shift+H isolates and Alt+H unhides all. (Settings › ModelCraft chooses the key style.) Things to know What is hidden is saved in the ModelCraft file, and comes back hidden when you open it. Hidden things are still in the model: exports include them, and so do volumes and sizes in Entity info. Each hide, isolate or unhide is one undo step."},{"slug":"tags","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Tags: show and hide sets of things","summary":"Give groups and components a tag, such as Furniture or Walls, and hide or show everything with that tag at once.","section":"tasks","order":48,"keywords":["tags","tag","layers","layer","show","hide","visibility","organize","furniture","walls","untagged","rename tag","delete tag"],"headings":[{"id":"make-a-tag","text":"Make a tag","level":2},{"id":"tag-groups-and-components","text":"Tag groups and components","level":2},{"id":"hide-and-show","text":"Hide and show","level":2},{"id":"delete-a-tag","text":"Delete a tag","level":2},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA tag is a name you give groups and components, such as \u003Cstrong\u003EFurniture\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EWalls\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003ESite\u003C/strong\u003E, so that you can hide or show everything with that tag at once. Tags are what CAD programs call layers. For hiding one thing at a time, see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/hide-isolate\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/hide-isolate\"\u003EHide, isolate and unhide\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"make-a-tag\"\u003EMake a tag\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETags\u003C/span\u003E on the right, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd tag\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType its name and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo rename a tag, click its name in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETags\u003C/span\u003E, type the new one and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Two tags cannot share a name.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"tag-groups-and-components\"\u003ETag groups and components\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect one or more groups or components.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E, choose the tag in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETag\u003C/span\u003E. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew tag…\u003C/span\u003E makes a new one and tags the selection with it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUntagged\u003C/span\u003E takes the tag away. The number next to each tag in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETags\u003C/span\u003E says how many groups and components have it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETag groups and components, not loose faces and edges: draw the things you want to tag as groups first (select them, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift G\"\u003E⇧G\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+G\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"hide-and-show\"\u003EHide and show\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick the eye next to a tag. Everything with that tag goes out of view, wherever it is in the model (also inside other groups), and clicks and snaps go past it. Click the eye again to show it. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUntagged\u003C/span\u003E things are always shown.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf you are editing inside a group whose tag you hide, the group closes.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"delete-a-tag\"\u003EDelete a tag\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick the bin next to it. What had the tag becomes \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUntagged\u003C/span\u003E, and shows.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETags, their names and which are hidden are saved in the ModelCraft file. Copying a tagged group into another model brings its tag along.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EHiding and showing a tag, and every other change to tags, is one undo step.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EExports include everything, hidden tags too, as they do for hidden things.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELayers in DWG and DXF site plans do not come in as tags yet: the whole drawing comes in as one group.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"A tag is a name you give groups and components, such as Furniture, Walls or Site, so that you can hide or show everything with that tag at once. Tags are what CAD programs call layers. For hiding one thing at a time, see Hide, isolate and unhide. Make a tag In Tags on the right, click Add tag. Type its name and press Enter. To rename a tag, click its name in Tags, type the new one and press Enter. Two tags cannot share a name. Tag groups and components Select one or more groups or components. In Entity info, choose the tag in Tag. New tag… makes a new one and tags the selection with it. Untagged takes the tag away. The number next to each tag in Tags says how many groups and components have it. Tag groups and components, not loose faces and edges: draw the things you want to tag as groups first (select them, Shift+G). Hide and show Click the eye next to a tag. Everything with that tag goes out of view, wherever it is in the model (also inside other groups), and clicks and snaps go past it. Click the eye again to show it. Untagged things are always shown. If you are editing inside a group whose tag you hide, the group closes. Delete a tag Click the bin next to it. What had the tag becomes Untagged, and shows. Things to know Tags, their names and which are hidden are saved in the ModelCraft file. Copying a tagged group into another model brings its tag along. Hiding and showing a tag, and every other change to tags, is one undo step. Exports include everything, hidden tags too, as they do for hidden things. Layers in DWG and DXF site plans do not come in as tags yet: the whole drawing comes in as one group."},{"slug":"sections","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Section planes: cut the model open","summary":"Place a section plane to see inside the model, slide it through, reverse it or turn the cut off.","section":"tasks","order":49,"keywords":["section","section plane","section cut","cut","cutaway","clip","inside","floor plan","slice","cross section"],"headings":[{"id":"place-one","text":"Place one","level":2},{"id":"slide-it-reverse-it-turn-it-off","text":"Slide it, reverse it, turn it off","level":2},{"id":"show-and-hide","text":"Show and hide","level":2},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA section plane cuts the model open, so you can see and work inside it: a floor plan of a house, the inside of a cabinet. The model is not changed; the cut is only a view.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"place-one\"\u003EPlace one\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESection\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECamera\u003C/span\u003E group.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a face. The plane is placed on that face, with four arrows pointing at you.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick away from any face for a plane square to the direction you look in most. Press an arrow key first to stand it square to red (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"→\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), green (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"←\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) or blue (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"↑\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) instead.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe side the arrows point at is \u003Cstrong\u003Ecut away\u003C/strong\u003E. A plane placed on a face cuts nothing yet: slide it into the model.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"slide-it-reverse-it-turn-it-off\"\u003ESlide it, reverse it, turn it off\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick the plane&#039;s outline to select it. In \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAlong its arrows\u003C/span\u003E says where it sits. Type a new distance and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to slide it through the model; typing \u003Ccode\u003E-1m\u003C/code\u003E on a plane you placed on a front face moves it 1 m into the model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReverse\u003C/span\u003E turns the arrows round, so the other side is cut away.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EActive (cuts the model)\u003C/span\u003E: one plane cuts at a time. Tick another plane&#039;s box, or place a new one, and that one cuts instead.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERename it in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EName\u003C/span\u003E; delete it with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEraser\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhere the plane passes through a closed solid, the cut is filled in a dark color. Geometry that is not closed (a single face, an open box) is cut but not filled.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"show-and-hide\"\u003EShow and hide\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EViews\u003C/span\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow section planes\u003C/span\u003E hides the planes&#039; outlines and arrows (the cut stays).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow section cuts\u003C/span\u003E turns the cut off and shows the whole model, without deleting the planes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/styles-shadows#presentation-view\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/styles-shadows\" data-hash=\"presentation-view\"\u003EPresentation view\u003C/a\u003E hides the outlines too.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhat is cut away cannot be clicked or snapped to, so you can draw on what the cut shows.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESection planes, which one is active and the two switches are saved in the ModelCraft file. Each change is one undo step.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EExports always contain the whole model: a section is a view, not an edit.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"A section plane cuts the model open, so you can see and work inside it: a floor plan of a house, the inside of a cabinet. The model is not changed; the cut is only a view. Place one Click Section in the Camera group. Click a face. The plane is placed on that face, with four arrows pointing at you. Click away from any face for a plane square to the direction you look in most. Press an arrow key first to stand it square to red (→), green (←) or blue (↑) instead. The side the arrows point at is cut away. A plane placed on a face cuts nothing yet: slide it into the model. Slide it, reverse it, turn it off Click the plane's outline to select it. In Entity info: Along its arrows says where it sits. Type a new distance and press Enter to slide it through the model; typing -1m on a plane you placed on a front face moves it 1 m into the model. Reverse turns the arrows round, so the other side is cut away. Active (cuts the model): one plane cuts at a time. Tick another plane's box, or place a new one, and that one cuts instead. Rename it in Name; delete it with Delete or the Eraser. Where the plane passes through a closed solid, the cut is filled in a dark color. Geometry that is not closed (a single face, an open box) is cut but not filled. Show and hide In Views: Show section planes hides the planes' outlines and arrows (the cut stays). Show section cuts turns the cut off and shows the whole model, without deleting the planes. Presentation view hides the outlines too. Things to know What is cut away cannot be clicked or snapped to, so you can draw on what the cut shows. Section planes, which one is active and the two switches are saved in the ModelCraft file. Each change is one undo step. Exports always contain the whole model: a section is a view, not an edit."},{"slug":"move-copy","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Move, copy and make arrays","summary":"Move things by clicking or by an exact distance, copy them with a modifier key, and make a row of evenly spaced copies.","section":"tasks","order":50,"keywords":["move","copy","duplicate","array","row","spacing","divide","offset","stretch","columns","repeat","multiple copy"],"headings":[{"id":"move-something","text":"Move something","level":2},{"id":"moving-part-of-a-shape-stretches-it","text":"Moving part of a shape stretches it","level":2},{"id":"copy-something","text":"Copy something","level":2},{"id":"copy-cut-paste-and-duplicate","text":"Copy, cut, paste and duplicate","level":2},{"id":"a-row-of-copies","text":"A row of copies","level":2},{"id":"example-columns-every-6-meters","text":"Example: columns every 6 meters","level":3},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"M\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) moves whatever is selected from one point to another. Holding a modifier key makes it a copy, and after a copy you can type how many more you want.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"move-something\"\u003EMove something\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect what you want to move (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/select\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/select\"\u003ESelect things\u003C/a\u003E). If nothing is selected, Move takes the thing you click on.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"M\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a point to move from. Pick something meaningful, such as a corner: that point will land exactly where you click next.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove the pointer. The selection follows. It snaps to points, edges and the red, green and blue directions like a line does.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick where the point goes, or type a distance and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe status bar&#039;s tip returns to &quot;Click a point to move from…&quot; for the next move.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA typed distance goes in the direction you are pointing. You can also type an exact offset, \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;2m,0,0&gt;\u003C/code\u003E (2 m along red), or an exact destination for the point you picked, \u003Ccode\u003E[0,0,0]\u003C/code\u003E (the origin). See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/value-box\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/value-box\"\u003EType exact sizes\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo move along one axis only, press an arrow key after the first click: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"→\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E locks to red, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"←\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to green, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"↑\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to blue. Press the same arrow again to unlock.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"moving-part-of-a-shape-stretches-it\"\u003EMoving part of a shape stretches it\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EGeometry that is not grouped is connected. If you select one face or a few edges and move them, the faces attached to them stretch to follow. Move the top face of a box to the side and the box leans; move one edge of the roof and the roof changes pitch. Faces that can no longer stay flat fold into triangles.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo move a whole object without stretching it, select all of it first (triple-click it), or better, make it a group or component (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/groups-components\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/groups-components\"\u003EGroups and components\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"copy-something\"\u003ECopy something\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect what you want to copy.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"M\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EHold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option\"\u003E⌥\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) and click the point to move from. On a Mac, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option\"\u003E⌥\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E does the same. The tip says &quot;Ctrl moves instead&quot;, which means you are copying.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou can also press and release \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option\"\u003E⌥\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E after the first click to switch between moving and copying.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick where the copy goes, or type a distance and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe original stays where it was, and the copy is selected.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"copy-cut-paste-and-duplicate\"\u003ECopy, cut, paste and duplicate\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E copies the selection, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command X\"\u003E⌘X\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+X\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E cuts it (copies, then erases), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E are also buttons in the toolbar&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E group, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E beside them has cut, paste in place and duplicate.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E pastes: an outline of the copy follows the pointer, snapping like any tool, and a click puts it down with its lowest corner there. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E cancels.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command V\"\u003E⇧⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E pastes in place, exactly where the copy came from — useful for moving something into a group you have opened.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command D\"\u003E⌘D\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift D\"\u003E⇧D\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E in Blender style) duplicates: a copy is made and moves with the pointer at once; click to put it down, type a distance, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to drop it. Right after, type \u003Ccode\u003E3x\u003C/code\u003E for more copies in a row.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA pasted group becomes a new group. A pasted component is another placement of the same component, so editing one still changes them all. Copies also paste into another ModelCraft tab in this browser (they come in as new definitions there).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-row-of-copies\"\u003EA row of copies\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ERight after a copy, while \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove\u003C/span\u003E is still the tool:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E3x\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003E*3\u003C/code\u003E) and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for three copies in a row, each at the same spacing as the first copy. You get three new copies besides the original.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E/3\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to divide instead: three copies spread evenly from the original to where you put the first copy, the last one at that spot. Use this to fill a span: place one copy at the far end, then divide.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou can type a different number right after: \u003Ccode\u003E5x\u003C/code\u003E replaces the \u003Ccode\u003E3x\u003C/code\u003E row. The tip reminds you: &quot;Copied. Type 3x for more copies in a row, or /3 to divide the distance.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"example-columns-every-6-meters\"\u003EExample: columns every 6 meters\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMake a column and make it a component (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"G\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), so all copies change together later.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"M\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and click the column&#039;s base corner.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove along the red axis and type \u003Ccode\u003E6m\u003C/code\u003E, then \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E4x\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. You have five columns, 6 m apart.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E before the second click to cancel; everything goes back where it was.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove works inside the group you are editing. To move a whole group, close it first and select it from outside.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith \u003Cstrong\u003ECollide\u003C/strong\u003E on (bottom right, and in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/settings#solids-stop-where-they-touch\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/settings\" data-hash=\"solids-stop-where-they-touch\"\u003EModelCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E), a move stops where the selection touches another solid instead of passing through it. Click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECollide\u003C/span\u003E to turn it off for a move that should go through.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo put a copy inside another group: copy it, open the other group, and paste (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command V\"\u003E⇧⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E keeps it in the same place).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Move (M) moves whatever is selected from one point to another. Holding a modifier key makes it a copy, and after a copy you can type how many more you want. Move something Select what you want to move (see Select things). If nothing is selected, Move takes the thing you click on. Press M. Click a point to move from. Pick something meaningful, such as a corner: that point will land exactly where you click next. Move the pointer. The selection follows. It snaps to points, edges and the red, green and blue directions like a line does. Click where the point goes, or type a distance and press Enter. The status bar's tip returns to \"Click a point to move from…\" for the next move. A typed distance goes in the direction you are pointing. You can also type an exact offset, \u003C2m,0,0\u003E (2 m along red), or an exact destination for the point you picked, [0,0,0] (the origin). See Type exact sizes. To move along one axis only, press an arrow key after the first click: → locks to red, ← to green, ↑ to blue. Press the same arrow again to unlock. Moving part of a shape stretches it Geometry that is not grouped is connected. If you select one face or a few edges and move them, the faces attached to them stretch to follow. Move the top face of a box to the side and the box leans; move one edge of the roof and the roof changes pitch. Faces that can no longer stay flat fold into triangles. To move a whole object without stretching it, select all of it first (triple-click it), or better, make it a group or component (see Groups and components). Copy something Select what you want to copy. Press M. Hold Ctrl (or Alt) and click the point to move from. On a Mac, Ctrl or Alt does the same. The tip says \"Ctrl moves instead\", which means you are copying. You can also press and release Ctrl or Alt after the first click to switch between moving and copying. Click where the copy goes, or type a distance and press Enter. The original stays where it was, and the copy is selected. Copy, cut, paste and duplicate Ctrl+C copies the selection, Ctrl+X cuts it (copies, then erases), Copy and Paste are also buttons in the toolbar's Edit group, and More beside them has cut, paste in place and duplicate. Ctrl+V pastes: an outline of the copy follows the pointer, snapping like any tool, and a click puts it down with its lowest corner there. Esc cancels. Ctrl+Shift+V pastes in place, exactly where the copy came from — useful for moving something into a group you have opened. Ctrl+D (Shift+D in Blender style) duplicates: a copy is made and moves with the pointer at once; click to put it down, type a distance, or press Esc to drop it. Right after, type 3x for more copies in a row. A pasted group becomes a new group. A pasted component is another placement of the same component, so editing one still changes them all. Copies also paste into another ModelCraft tab in this browser (they come in as new definitions there). A row of copies Right after a copy, while Move is still the tool: Type 3x (or *3) and press Enter for three copies in a row, each at the same spacing as the first copy. You get three new copies besides the original. Type /3 and press Enter to divide instead: three copies spread evenly from the original to where you put the first copy, the last one at that spot. Use this to fill a span: place one copy at the far end, then divide. You can type a different number right after: 5x replaces the 3x row. The tip reminds you: \"Copied. Type 3x for more copies in a row, or /3 to divide the distance.\" Example: columns every 6 meters Make a column and make it a component (G), so all copies change together later. Press M, hold Ctrl and click the column's base corner. Move along the red axis and type 6m, then Enter. Type 4x and Enter. You have five columns, 6 m apart. Things to know Press Esc before the second click to cancel; everything goes back where it was. Move works inside the group you are editing. To move a whole group, close it first and select it from outside. With Collide on (bottom right, and in ModelCraft settings), a move stops where the selection touches another solid instead of passing through it. Click Collide to turn it off for a move that should go through. To put a copy inside another group: copy it, open the other group, and paste (Ctrl+Shift+V keeps it in the same place)."},{"slug":"soften","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Soften and smooth edges","summary":"Hide the edges between faces that meet at a shallow angle, so curved shapes read as one smooth surface, in ModelCraft and in exported files.","section":"tasks","order":52,"keywords":["soften","smooth","soft edges","smooth shading","hide edges","facets","faceted","curved","normals","harden","angle"],"headings":[{"id":"soften-by-angle","text":"Soften by angle","level":2},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA curved shape in ModelCraft is made of flat faces. \u003Cstrong\u003ESoft\u003C/strong\u003E edges between those faces are not drawn, and the faces they join are shaded as one smooth surface, so a 24-sided cylinder looks round.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen you pull a circle up with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPush/pull\u003C/span\u003E, its sides are softened for you. For other shapes, soften by angle.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"soften-by-angle\"\u003ESoften by angle\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect what to soften: faces and edges, or a group or component (all of its edges).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E, type an angle in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESoften up to\u003C/span\u003E, such as \u003Ccode\u003E20\u003C/code\u003E, and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESoften\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery edge in the selection whose two faces turn by that angle or less becomes soft; edges with a sharper turn become hard. A 12-sided prism&#039;s sides turn by 30° and its rims by 90°, so \u003Ccode\u003E31\u003C/code\u003E softens only the sides.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHarden all\u003C/span\u003E makes every edge in the selection hard again. Each is one undo step.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOnly edges between exactly two faces are softened. Loose edges stay as they are.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESmooth shading works across soft edges only: a face with no soft edge shades flat.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESoft edges are kept in the ModelCraft file. \u003Cstrong\u003EglTF (.glb)\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EOBJ\u003C/strong\u003E exports carry the smooth shading (a normal per corner), so other programs show the same surface. Collada and STL export flat faces.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESoft edges are hidden in every face style, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EX-ray\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWireframe\u003C/span\u003E too. To see them, select the shape and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHarden all\u003C/span\u003E; undo brings the softening back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"A curved shape in ModelCraft is made of flat faces. Soft edges between those faces are not drawn, and the faces they join are shaded as one smooth surface, so a 24-sided cylinder looks round. When you pull a circle up with Push/pull, its sides are softened for you. For other shapes, soften by angle. Soften by angle Select what to soften: faces and edges, or a group or component (all of its edges). In Entity info, type an angle in Soften up to, such as 20, and click Soften. Every edge in the selection whose two faces turn by that angle or less becomes soft; edges with a sharper turn become hard. A 12-sided prism's sides turn by 30° and its rims by 90°, so 31 softens only the sides. Harden all makes every edge in the selection hard again. Each is one undo step. Things to know Only edges between exactly two faces are softened. Loose edges stay as they are. Smooth shading works across soft edges only: a face with no soft edge shades flat. Soft edges are kept in the ModelCraft file. glTF (.glb) and OBJ exports carry the smooth shading (a normal per corner), so other programs show the same surface. Collada and STL export flat faces. Soft edges are hidden in every face style, X-ray and Wireframe too. To see them, select the shape and click Harden all; undo brings the softening back."},{"slug":"intersect","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Intersect faces","summary":"Draw the lines where faces cut through each other, so you can push, erase or paint the pieces.","section":"tasks","order":53,"keywords":["intersect","intersect faces","intersect with model","intersect with selection","cut","opening","hole","join","lines where faces cross","boolean"],"headings":[{"id":"intersect-with-model","text":"Intersect with model","level":2},{"id":"intersect-with-selection","text":"Intersect with selection","level":2},{"id":"where-the-lines-go","text":"Where the lines go","level":2},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EWhen one shape passes through another, ModelCraft does not draw the line where their faces meet: they just overlap. \u003Cstrong\u003EIntersect\u003C/strong\u003E draws those lines, as edges, so the faces split there. Then you can push/pull, erase or paint the pieces.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBoth commands are in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"intersect-with-model\"\u003EIntersect with model\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECuts what you select against \u003Cstrong\u003Eevery face on screen\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor example, a beam (a group) passes through a wall (another group), and you want an opening in the wall:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDouble-click the wall to open it, and select all of its faces (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control A\"\u003E⌃A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIntersect with model\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe beam&#039;s outline is drawn on both faces of the wall, as two new faces. Push one of them through the wall to make the opening, then close the wall.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"intersect-with-selection\"\u003EIntersect with selection\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECuts the selected faces, groups and components \u003Cstrong\u003Eonly against each other\u003C/strong\u003E. Select at least two.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"where-the-lines-go\"\u003EWhere the lines go\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe lines are always drawn in the \u003Cstrong\u003Eopen context\u003C/strong\u003E: in the model when no group is open, or in the group you are editing. Groups and components that are not open are not changed. So to cut a group&#039;s faces, open it first, as in the example above.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFaces that lie in the same plane are not intersected.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELines that close a loop on a face make a new face, as drawn lines do.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach intersect is one undo step. If nothing crosses, nothing changes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFor closed shapes, the \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/solids\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/solids\"\u003Esolid tools\u003C/a\u003E (Union, Subtract) do the whole job in one step.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"When one shape passes through another, ModelCraft does not draw the line where their faces meet: they just overlap. Intersect draws those lines, as edges, so the faces split there. Then you can push/pull, erase or paint the pieces. Both commands are in Edit › More. Intersect with model Cuts what you select against every face on screen. For example, a beam (a group) passes through a wall (another group), and you want an opening in the wall: Double-click the wall to open it, and select all of its faces (Ctrl+A). Edit › More › Intersect with model. The beam's outline is drawn on both faces of the wall, as two new faces. Push one of them through the wall to make the opening, then close the wall. Intersect with selection Cuts the selected faces, groups and components only against each other. Select at least two. Where the lines go The lines are always drawn in the open context: in the model when no group is open, or in the group you are editing. Groups and components that are not open are not changed. So to cut a group's faces, open it first, as in the example above. Things to know Faces that lie in the same plane are not intersected. Lines that close a loop on a face make a new face, as drawn lines do. Each intersect is one undo step. If nothing crosses, nothing changes. For closed shapes, the solid tools (Union, Subtract) do the whole job in one step."},{"slug":"axes","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Move and turn the drawing axes","summary":"Turn the red, green and blue axes to match a building that is not square to them, or align them to a face, so drawing and typing follow it.","section":"tasks","order":54,"keywords":["axes","drawing axes","origin","align axes","rotate axes","turn axes","reset axes","angled building","skewed","ucs","coordinate system"],"headings":[{"id":"place-axes","text":"Place axes","level":2},{"id":"align-axes-to-a-face","text":"Align axes to a face","level":2},{"id":"reset-axes","text":"Reset axes","level":2},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe red, green and blue axes guide everything you draw: rectangles line up with them, lines lock to them, and the ground is the red–green plane. When a building sits at an angle (a site plan that is not square to north, a wing turned 30°), move the axes onto it, so drawing follows its walls. The model itself does not move.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAll three commands are in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"place-axes\"\u003EPlace axes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlace axes\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick where the origin goes, such as a corner of the building.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a point along the new red axis, such as further along a wall.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a point toward the new green axis. Green is made square to red, and blue stands square to both.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe axes are drawn in their new place. A rectangle you now draw on the ground, or type as \u003Ccode\u003E4m,3m\u003C/code\u003E, runs along the turned red and green; the arrow keys lock to them; and the origin snap is at the new origin.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"align-axes-to-a-face\"\u003EAlign axes to a face\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect one face, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAlign axes to face\u003C/span\u003E. Blue then points out of the face, red runs along its longest edge, and the origin is at that edge&#039;s start. Use it to draw on a sloping roof as if it were the ground.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"reset-axes\"\u003EReset axes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset axes\u003C/span\u003E puts them back where the model began.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EInside an open group or component, its own axes are used (they turn with it); the model&#039;s axes apply at the top level.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe axes are saved in the ModelCraft file, and each change is one undo step.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EExports and the standard views (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETop\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFront\u003C/span\u003E…) still use the model&#039;s own directions.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"The red, green and blue axes guide everything you draw: rectangles line up with them, lines lock to them, and the ground is the red–green plane. When a building sits at an angle (a site plan that is not square to north, a wing turned 30°), move the axes onto it, so drawing follows its walls. The model itself does not move. All three commands are in Edit › More. Place axes Edit › More › Place axes. Click where the origin goes, such as a corner of the building. Click a point along the new red axis, such as further along a wall. Click a point toward the new green axis. Green is made square to red, and blue stands square to both. The axes are drawn in their new place. A rectangle you now draw on the ground, or type as 4m,3m, runs along the turned red and green; the arrow keys lock to them; and the origin snap is at the new origin. Align axes to a face Select one face, then Edit › More › Align axes to face. Blue then points out of the face, red runs along its longest edge, and the origin is at that edge's start. Use it to draw on a sloping roof as if it were the ground. Reset axes Edit › More › Reset axes puts them back where the model began. Things to know Inside an open group or component, its own axes are used (they turn with it); the model's axes apply at the top level. The axes are saved in the ModelCraft file, and each change is one undo step. Exports and the standard views (Top, Front…) still use the model's own directions."},{"slug":"terrain","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Terrain: ground from contours, and sculpting it","summary":"Make ground from contour lines or as a grid, then raise and lower it with a soft brush.","section":"tasks","order":55,"keywords":["terrain","ground","landscape","topography","contours","contour lines","site","slope","hill","mound","sandbox","smoove","sculpt","grade","TIN"],"headings":[{"id":"from-contour-lines","text":"From contour lines","level":2},{"id":"a-grid-to-sculpt","text":"A grid to sculpt","level":2},{"id":"sculpt-raise-and-lower-the-ground","text":"Sculpt: raise and lower the ground","level":2},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ETerrain is ground made of triangles, so it can follow any shape. It is a group: double-click it to edit it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"from-contour-lines\"\u003EFrom contour lines\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EGet the contour lines at their heights: draw them, or \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/site-plan\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/site-plan\"\u003Eimport a site plan\u003C/a\u003E and move each contour up to its level.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect them (all the edges of all the contours).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETerrain from selected contours\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EModelCraft joins the contour points with triangles (a TIN), and softens every edge inside so the ground shades smooth. The lines themselves stay; erase or hide them when you no longer need them. Very dense contours are thinned to 8 000 points.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-grid-to-sculpt\"\u003EA grid to sculpt\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETerrain grid…\u003C/span\u003E asks for a width, a depth and a cell size, then places a flat grid of triangles where you click its corner. Small cells sculpt smoother but make more faces (at most 200 × 200 cells).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"sculpt-raise-and-lower-the-ground\"\u003ESculpt: raise and lower the ground\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDouble-click the terrain to open it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESculpt terrain\u003C/span\u003E. A circle shows the brush on the ground under the pointer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag up to raise the ground under the brush, or down to lower it. The middle moves most and the ground eases to nothing at the circle&#039;s edge, so mounds and hollows come out smooth.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo make a mound an exact height, click once, then type the height, such as \u003Ccode\u003E1.5m\u003C/code\u003E (a minus lowers it), and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. To change the brush, type a length before you click: \u003Ccode\u003E5m\u003C/code\u003E gives a brush 5 m across its radius.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach sculpt is one undo step.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESculpt moves only triangle faces, so every face stays flat. Faces drawn by hand (rectangles, polygons) are not terrain: the tool says so.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESculpting moves points straight up and down; it does not add points. For finer detail, make a grid with smaller cells.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETerrain exports like any other group. In IFC it is a building element proxy, like everything else.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Terrain is ground made of triangles, so it can follow any shape. It is a group: double-click it to edit it. From contour lines Get the contour lines at their heights: draw them, or import a site plan and move each contour up to its level. Select them (all the edges of all the contours). Edit › More › Terrain from selected contours. ModelCraft joins the contour points with triangles (a TIN), and softens every edge inside so the ground shades smooth. The lines themselves stay; erase or hide them when you no longer need them. Very dense contours are thinned to 8 000 points. A grid to sculpt Edit › More › Terrain grid… asks for a width, a depth and a cell size, then places a flat grid of triangles where you click its corner. Small cells sculpt smoother but make more faces (at most 200 × 200 cells). Sculpt: raise and lower the ground Double-click the terrain to open it. Edit › More › Sculpt terrain. A circle shows the brush on the ground under the pointer. Drag up to raise the ground under the brush, or down to lower it. The middle moves most and the ground eases to nothing at the circle's edge, so mounds and hollows come out smooth. To make a mound an exact height, click once, then type the height, such as 1.5m (a minus lowers it), and press Enter. To change the brush, type a length before you click: 5m gives a brush 5 m across its radius. Each sculpt is one undo step. Things to know Sculpt moves only triangle faces, so every face stays flat. Faces drawn by hand (rectangles, polygons) are not terrain: the tool says so. Sculpting moves points straight up and down; it does not add points. For finer detail, make a grid with smaller cells. Terrain exports like any other group. In IFC it is a building element proxy, like everything else."},{"slug":"rotate-scale","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Rotate and scale","summary":"Turn things about a point by clicking or by typing degrees, and resize them evenly by a factor.","section":"tasks","order":60,"keywords":["scale grips","stretch","non-uniform","per axis","flip","mirror","mirrored copy","flip along","rotate","turn","angle","degrees","protractor","scale","resize","enlarge","shrink","factor","uniform scale"],"headings":[{"id":"rotate","text":"Rotate","level":2},{"id":"scale","text":"Scale","level":2},{"id":"stretch-or-scale-with-the-grips","text":"Stretch or scale with the grips","level":3},{"id":"scale-about-any-point","text":"Scale about any point","level":3},{"id":"scale-to-an-exact-size","text":"Scale to an exact size","level":3},{"id":"fix-an-import-that-came-in-the-wrong-size","text":"Fix an import that came in the wrong size","level":3},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2},{"id":"flip","text":"Flip","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"rotate\"\u003ERotate\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERotate\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Q\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) turns the selection about a center point. It takes three clicks: the center, a starting direction, and the end direction.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect what you want to turn. If nothing is selected, Rotate takes the thing you click first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Q\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. A ring follows the pointer, showing the plane you will turn in.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the center of rotation.\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn the ground or in empty space, the ring lies flat (blue): the turn is about a vertical axis, like turning a building on its site.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn a face, the ring lies in that face&#039;s plane: the turn is about the face&#039;s own direction. Click on a wall to turn something about a horizontal axis.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a point that sets where the angle starts, for example along an edge of the thing you are turning.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove the pointer around the center. The angle shows in the tip and in the value box (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAngle\u003C/span\u003E). It clicks into steps of 15° when you get close to one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick to finish, or type the angle in degrees and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003E90\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E45\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E-30\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPositive angles turn counterclockwise, seen from the side the ring faces.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELike Move, rotating part of connected geometry twists the faces attached to it. Group things first to turn them whole.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe tip says arrow keys pick the rotation axis, but in this build they orbit the view instead before the first click. To choose the axis, click the center on a face that faces the way you want. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"scale\"\u003EScale\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) resizes the selection. Select what you want to resize first, then press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E: small squares (grips) appear on the corners, the edges and the middles of the sides of its box.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"stretch-or-scale-with-the-grips\"\u003EStretch or scale with the grips\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA \u003Cstrong\u003Ecorner\u003C/strong\u003E grip scales evenly in every direction, about the opposite corner.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe grip in the middle of a \u003Cstrong\u003Eside\u003C/strong\u003E stretches along that direction only, about the opposite side: make a box longer without making it taller.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe grip in the middle of a \u003Cstrong\u003Ebox edge\u003C/strong\u003E stretches in the two directions across it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EHold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E when you click a grip to scale about the middle of the box instead.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick a grip, move the pointer, and click again; or click the grip and type a factor (\u003Ccode\u003E2\u003C/code\u003E doubles, \u003Ccode\u003E0.5\u003C/code\u003E halves) and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. To give each direction its own factor, type three, for red, green and blue: \u003Ccode\u003E2,1,0.5\u003C/code\u003E. Inside an open group, the directions are the group&#039;s own.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"scale-about-any-point\"\u003EScale about any point\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick anywhere other than a grip to scale evenly about a point of your own. It takes three clicks:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect what you want to resize. If nothing is selected, Scale takes the thing you click first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the point that stays put (not on a grip), for example a bottom corner.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a reference point. Its distance from the first point counts as 1×.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove the pointer toward or away from the first point. The factor shows in the tip and in the value box (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale\u003C/span\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick to finish, or type a factor and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003E2\u003C/code\u003E doubles the size, \u003Ccode\u003E0.5\u003C/code\u003E halves it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"scale-to-an-exact-size\"\u003EScale to an exact size\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo make something an exact length, work out the factor: new length divided by current length. The value box does not do arithmetic, so work it out first: to make a 3.6 m wall 4.2 m long, 4.2 divided by 3.6 is about 1.1667, so type \u003Ccode\u003E1.1667\u003C/code\u003E. Select the edge first to read its current length in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"fix-an-import-that-came-in-the-wrong-size\"\u003EFix an import that came in the wrong size\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOBJ files have no unit, so a model drawn in millimeters comes in 1000 times too big. Select the imported group, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, click a corner, click any other point, and type \u003Ccode\u003E0.001\u003C/code\u003E. For a model drawn in inches, type \u003Ccode\u003E0.0254\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EScale is uniform only: there is no stretching in one direction and no grips on a box around the selection. To make a box longer in one direction, push/pull its end, or move its end face.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EScaling a group or component changes the copy you scaled; other copies of a component keep their size.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E before the last click to cancel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"flip\"\u003EFlip\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFlip along red\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFlip along green\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFlip along blue\u003C/span\u003E mirrors the selection in place, through the middle of its box: a left-hand stair becomes a right-hand one. Faces keep facing out, and a group or component is mirrored as a whole (its insides are not rewritten).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFlipped copy along red\u003C/span\u003E (and green, blue) leaves the original where it is and puts a mirrored copy beside it, across the far side of the selection&#039;s box: the other door of a pair, the other wing of a building. Loose geometry touching there joins the original, as touching geometry always does; make the original a group first to keep them apart.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EInside an open group, red, green and blue are the group&#039;s own axes.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Rotate Rotate (Q) turns the selection about a center point. It takes three clicks: the center, a starting direction, and the end direction. Select what you want to turn. If nothing is selected, Rotate takes the thing you click first. Press Q. A ring follows the pointer, showing the plane you will turn in. Click the center of rotation. On the ground or in empty space, the ring lies flat (blue): the turn is about a vertical axis, like turning a building on its site. On a face, the ring lies in that face's plane: the turn is about the face's own direction. Click on a wall to turn something about a horizontal axis. Click a point that sets where the angle starts, for example along an edge of the thing you are turning. Move the pointer around the center. The angle shows in the tip and in the value box (Angle). It clicks into steps of 15° when you get close to one. Click to finish, or type the angle in degrees and press Enter: 90, 45, -30. Positive angles turn counterclockwise, seen from the side the ring faces. Like Move, rotating part of connected geometry twists the faces attached to it. Group things first to turn them whole. The tip says arrow keys pick the rotation axis, but in this build they orbit the view instead before the first click. To choose the axis, click the center on a face that faces the way you want. See Not built yet. Scale Scale (S) resizes the selection. Select what you want to resize first, then press S: small squares (grips) appear on the corners, the edges and the middles of the sides of its box. Stretch or scale with the grips A corner grip scales evenly in every direction, about the opposite corner. The grip in the middle of a side stretches along that direction only, about the opposite side: make a box longer without making it taller. The grip in the middle of a box edge stretches in the two directions across it. Hold Ctrl when you click a grip to scale about the middle of the box instead. Click a grip, move the pointer, and click again; or click the grip and type a factor (2 doubles, 0.5 halves) and press Enter. To give each direction its own factor, type three, for red, green and blue: 2,1,0.5. Inside an open group, the directions are the group's own. Scale about any point Click anywhere other than a grip to scale evenly about a point of your own. It takes three clicks: Select what you want to resize. If nothing is selected, Scale takes the thing you click first. Press S. Click the point that stays put (not on a grip), for example a bottom corner. Click a reference point. Its distance from the first point counts as 1×. Move the pointer toward or away from the first point. The factor shows in the tip and in the value box (Scale). Click to finish, or type a factor and press Enter: 2 doubles the size, 0.5 halves it. Scale to an exact size To make something an exact length, work out the factor: new length divided by current length. The value box does not do arithmetic, so work it out first: to make a 3.6 m wall 4.2 m long, 4.2 divided by 3.6 is about 1.1667, so type 1.1667. Select the edge first to read its current length in Entity info. Fix an import that came in the wrong size OBJ files have no unit, so a model drawn in millimeters comes in 1000 times too big. Select the imported group, press S, click a corner, click any other point, and type 0.001. For a model drawn in inches, type 0.0254. Things to know Scale is uniform only: there is no stretching in one direction and no grips on a box around the selection. To make a box longer in one direction, push/pull its end, or move its end face. Scaling a group or component changes the copy you scaled; other copies of a component keep their size. Press Esc before the last click to cancel. Flip Edit › More › Flip along red, Flip along green or Flip along blue mirrors the selection in place, through the middle of its box: a left-hand stair becomes a right-hand one. Faces keep facing out, and a group or component is mirrored as a whole (its insides are not rewritten). Flipped copy along red (and green, blue) leaves the original where it is and puts a mirrored copy beside it, across the far side of the selection's box: the other door of a pair, the other wing of a building. Loose geometry touching there joins the original, as touching geometry always does; make the original a group first to keep them apart. Inside an open group, red, green and blue are the group's own axes."},{"slug":"groups-components","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Groups and components","summary":"Keep objects from sticking together, edit one copy to change them all, and use the Outliner and Components panels.","section":"tasks","order":70,"keywords":["group","component","make group","make component","explode","make unique","outliner","instances","copies","block","nested","edit group","place"],"headings":[{"id":"make-a-group-or-a-component","text":"Make a group or a component","level":2},{"id":"edit-inside-a-group-or-component","text":"Edit inside a group or component","level":2},{"id":"place-more-copies-of-a-component","text":"Place more copies of a component","level":2},{"id":"make-one-copy-different","text":"Make one copy different","level":2},{"id":"turn-a-group-into-a-component","text":"Turn a group into a component","level":2},{"id":"explode","text":"Explode","level":2},{"id":"solid-and-volume","text":"Solid and volume","level":2},{"id":"when-the-last-copy-goes","text":"When the last copy goes","level":2},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ELoose geometry in ModelCraft sticks together: lines that touch split each other, and faces that share an edge move together. That is what makes drawing on faces work, but it also means a box drawn against another box merges with it. \u003Cstrong\u003EGroups\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003Ecomponents\u003C/strong\u003E keep things apart.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA \u003Cstrong\u003Egroup\u003C/strong\u003E wraps some geometry so it acts as one object. Nothing outside it can stick to it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA \u003Cstrong\u003Ecomponent\u003C/strong\u003E is a group that can be placed many times. Every copy shares the same contents: edit one and they all change. Use components for anything repeated: windows, columns, desks, trees.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"make-a-group-or-a-component\"\u003EMake a group or a component\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect the geometry. A triple-click on a face selects the whole connected object; a window drag selects everything in the box.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift G\"\u003E⇧G\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+G\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for a group, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"G\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for a component. You can also click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGroup\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EComponent\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOrganize\u003C/span\u003E group, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMake group (Shift+G)\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMake component (G)\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe status bar says &quot;Grouped as Group#1.&quot; or &quot;Made component Component#1.&quot;, the new object is selected, and it appears in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOutliner\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo name it, type a name in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EName\u003C/span\u003E field of \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or click away.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelecting a face brings its edges along. If nothing is selected, the status bar says &quot;Select some geometry first (drag a window, or triple-click to select everything connected).&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou can also group groups: select several groups and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift G\"\u003E⇧G\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+G\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOutliner\u003C/span\u003E shows the nesting.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe key letters match SketchUp&#039;s defaults: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"G\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E makes a component, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift G\"\u003E⇧G\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+G\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E a group.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"edit-inside-a-group-or-component\"\u003EEdit inside a group or component\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDouble-click it (in the view or in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOutliner\u003C/span\u003E), or select it and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe rest of the model fades, the axes move to the group&#039;s own axes, and the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOutliner\u003C/span\u003E reads &quot;Editing: Model › Group#1&quot;. The status bar says &quot;Editing Group#1. Esc or double-click outside closes it.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDraw, push/pull, move and erase as usual. Everything you do stays inside the group.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClose it: press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (twice if something is selected), double-click empty space, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClose group\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOutliner\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhile a component is open, every copy of it changes as you work.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"place-more-copies-of-a-component\"\u003EPlace more copies of a component\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECopy it with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/move-copy\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/move-copy\"\u003EMove and copy\u003C/a\u003E). Each copy is another placement of the same component.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOr click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlace\u003C/span\u003E next to it in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EComponents\u003C/span\u003E panel, then click where it goes. Its origin, the lowest corner of the box around what you selected when you made it, lands where you click. The tool then returns to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EComponents\u003C/span\u003E shows how many copies are placed (&quot;4×&quot;), and the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOutliner\u003C/span\u003E shows the count after the name.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"make-one-copy-different\"\u003EMake one copy different\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect the copy and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMake unique\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E (shown when the component is placed more than once). That copy gets its own definition, named like the original with a new number. Edit it without touching the others.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"turn-a-group-into-a-component\"\u003ETurn a group into a component\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect the group and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMake component\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E. It gets a new name, such as Component#2, even if you had named the group; type the name again in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EName\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"explode\"\u003EExplode\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect a group or component and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExplode\u003C/span\u003E (in the toolbar or in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E). Its contents become loose geometry in the place it was, and stick to what they touch. Groups inside it stay groups.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"solid-and-volume\"\u003ESolid and volume\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen you select a group or component, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E shows \u003Cstrong\u003ESolid: Yes\u003C/strong\u003E if its faces close up with no gaps (every edge is shared by exactly two faces) and there are no groups nested inside it. Then it also shows the \u003Cstrong\u003Evolume\u003C/strong\u003E. A box, a wall with a window cut through it, and a pulled cylinder are solids; a box with its top erased is not.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-the-last-copy-goes\"\u003EWhen the last copy goes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EErasing the last copy of a component also removes it from the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EComponents\u003C/span\u003E panel. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E brings it back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECopies of a \u003Cstrong\u003Egroup\u003C/strong\u003E made with Move share their contents in this build, like component copies do: editing one changes the others. To make one independent, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMake component\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMake unique\u003C/span\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/troubleshooting#editing-one-group-changed-another\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/troubleshooting\" data-hash=\"editing-one-group-changed-another\"\u003ETroubleshooting\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EComponents do not cut openings in walls or glue to faces, and there is no library of ready-made components. You make your own, or import them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EImported files arrive as a group. Collada files keep their own groups and components inside it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Loose geometry in ModelCraft sticks together: lines that touch split each other, and faces that share an edge move together. That is what makes drawing on faces work, but it also means a box drawn against another box merges with it. Groups and components keep things apart. A group wraps some geometry so it acts as one object. Nothing outside it can stick to it. A component is a group that can be placed many times. Every copy shares the same contents: edit one and they all change. Use components for anything repeated: windows, columns, desks, trees. Make a group or a component Select the geometry. A triple-click on a face selects the whole connected object; a window drag selects everything in the box. Press Shift+G for a group, or G for a component. You can also click Group or Component in the toolbar's Organize group, or Make group (Shift+G) or Make component (G) in Entity info. The status bar says \"Grouped as Group#1.\" or \"Made component Component#1.\", the new object is selected, and it appears in the Outliner. To name it, type a name in the Name field of Entity info and press Enter or click away. Selecting a face brings its edges along. If nothing is selected, the status bar says \"Select some geometry first (drag a window, or triple-click to select everything connected).\" You can also group groups: select several groups and press Shift+G. The Outliner shows the nesting. The key letters match SketchUp's defaults: G makes a component, Shift+G a group. Edit inside a group or component Double-click it (in the view or in the Outliner), or select it and click Edit in Entity info. The rest of the model fades, the axes move to the group's own axes, and the Outliner reads \"Editing: Model › Group#1\". The status bar says \"Editing Group#1. Esc or double-click outside closes it.\" Draw, push/pull, move and erase as usual. Everything you do stays inside the group. Close it: press Esc (twice if something is selected), double-click empty space, or click Close group in the Outliner. While a component is open, every copy of it changes as you work. Place more copies of a component Copy it with Move and Ctrl (see Move and copy). Each copy is another placement of the same component. Or click Place next to it in the Components panel, then click where it goes. Its origin, the lowest corner of the box around what you selected when you made it, lands where you click. The tool then returns to Select. Components shows how many copies are placed (\"4×\"), and the Outliner shows the count after the name. Make one copy different Select the copy and click Make unique in Entity info (shown when the component is placed more than once). That copy gets its own definition, named like the original with a new number. Edit it without touching the others. Turn a group into a component Select the group and click Make component in Entity info. It gets a new name, such as Component#2, even if you had named the group; type the name again in Name. Explode Select a group or component and click Explode (in the toolbar or in Entity info). Its contents become loose geometry in the place it was, and stick to what they touch. Groups inside it stay groups. Solid and volume When you select a group or component, Entity info shows Solid: Yes if its faces close up with no gaps (every edge is shared by exactly two faces) and there are no groups nested inside it. Then it also shows the volume. A box, a wall with a window cut through it, and a pulled cylinder are solids; a box with its top erased is not. When the last copy goes Erasing the last copy of a component also removes it from the Components panel. Ctrl+Z brings it back. Things to know Copies of a group made with Move share their contents in this build, like component copies do: editing one changes the others. To make one independent, click Make component, then Make unique. See Troubleshooting. Components do not cut openings in walls or glue to faces, and there is no library of ready-made components. You make your own, or import them. Imported files arrive as a group. Collada files keep their own groups and components inside it."},{"slug":"guides-measure","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Measure and place guides","summary":"Read lengths and areas, measure between two points with the tape, and lay out guide lines and points to draw against.","section":"tasks","order":80,"keywords":["measure","tape measure","protractor","angle","angled guide","guide","guideline","construction line","distance","dimension","length","area","offset","layout","guide point"],"headings":[{"id":"read-sizes-without-measuring","text":"Read sizes without measuring","level":2},{"id":"measure-between-two-points","text":"Measure between two points","level":2},{"id":"place-a-guide-line","text":"Place a guide line","level":2},{"id":"a-guide-at-an-angle-protractor","text":"A guide at an angle: Protractor","level":2},{"id":"draw-against-guides","text":"Draw against guides","level":2},{"id":"remove-guides","text":"Remove guides","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"read-sizes-without-measuring\"\u003ERead sizes without measuring\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect something and look at \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAn edge shows its \u003Cstrong\u003Elength\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA face shows its \u003Cstrong\u003Earea\u003C/strong\u003E and how many holes it has.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESeveral faces show their \u003Cstrong\u003Etotal area\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA group or component shows its \u003Cstrong\u003Esize\u003C/strong\u003E (width × depth × height of the box around it) and, if it is a closed solid, its \u003Cstrong\u003Evolume\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith nothing selected, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E shows the size of the whole model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELengths and areas use the unit in the picker at the bottom right. A millimeter model shows areas of 0.01 m² and up in square meters (a 4 × 3 m room reads &quot;12 m²&quot;).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhile you draw, the value box shows the live length, size, radius or distance, so you rarely need to measure afterward.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo change a size after drawing, select an edge or a group and retype its length or size in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E. Or place a \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/dimensions\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/dimensions\"\u003Edimension\u003C/a\u003E and type a new length into it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"measure-between-two-points\"\u003EMeasure between two points\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"T\"\u003ET\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ET\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETape\u003C/span\u003E measure.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a point: a corner, a midpoint, a point on a face, or the ground. (Clicking an edge does something else; see below.)\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove to the second point. The tip and the value box (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELength\u003C/span\u003E) show the distance. It snaps to points and to the red, green and blue directions.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick. The status bar says &quot;Measured 4&#039; 6&quot;.&quot; and a \u003Cstrong\u003Eguide point\u003C/strong\u003E is left at the second point.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo leave the guide point at an exact distance instead, type it after the first click and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E: it goes in the direction you are pointing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"place-a-guide-line\"\u003EPlace a guide line\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA guide line is an endless dashed line, parallel to an edge, at a distance you choose. Use guides to lay out a setback, a floor height, or where a window starts.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"T\"\u003ET\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ET\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick on an \u003Cstrong\u003Eedge\u003C/strong\u003E (not its end). The tip says &quot;On edge&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove off the edge, to the side the guide should go. A dashed line follows, parallel to the edge, and the value box (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffset\u003C/span\u003E) shows the distance.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick, or type the offset and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003E1.5m\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E5&#039;\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe status bar says &quot;Guide line 1.5 m from the edge.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EStarting on an existing guide line makes another guide parallel to it, so you can step a grid out from one guide.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf you type an offset without moving off the edge first, ModelCraft cannot tell which side you mean: &quot;Move off the edge first to show which side.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-guide-at-an-angle-protractor\"\u003EA guide at an angle: Protractor\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProtractor\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModify\u003C/span\u003E group.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the center: the point the guide passes through. On a face, the protractor lies on that face; elsewhere it lies flat. After this click, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"→\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"←\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"↑\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E stands it square to red, green or blue instead (press again to undo).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a point that sets 0°, for example along an edge.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove to the angle you want and click: it snaps to 15° steps. Or type the angle in degrees, such as \u003Ccode\u003E22.5\u003C/code\u003E, and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E; the guide goes to the side the pointer is on.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe guide line passes through the center at that angle. Draw along it with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELine\u003C/span\u003E for a roof pitch, a chamfer or a splayed wall.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-against-guides\"\u003EDraw against guides\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDrawing tools snap to guide lines (&quot;On guide&quot;) and guide points (&quot;Guide point&quot;), and to where a guide line crosses an axis direction. Start a rectangle where two guides cross to place it exactly.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"remove-guides\"\u003ERemove guides\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOne guide: click it with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or click it with the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEraser\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAll guides: with nothing selected, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete all guides\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EGuides are saved in the \u003Ccode\u003E.modelcraft\u003C/code\u003E file. They are not exported to other formats.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Read sizes without measuring Select something and look at Entity info: An edge shows its length. A face shows its area and how many holes it has. Several faces show their total area. A group or component shows its size (width × depth × height of the box around it) and, if it is a closed solid, its volume. With nothing selected, Entity info shows the size of the whole model. Lengths and areas use the unit in the picker at the bottom right. A millimeter model shows areas of 0.01 m² and up in square meters (a 4 × 3 m room reads \"12 m²\"). While you draw, the value box shows the live length, size, radius or distance, so you rarely need to measure afterward. To change a size after drawing, select an edge or a group and retype its length or size in Entity info. Or place a dimension and type a new length into it. Measure between two points Press T for the Tape measure. Click a point: a corner, a midpoint, a point on a face, or the ground. (Clicking an edge does something else; see below.) Move to the second point. The tip and the value box (Length) show the distance. It snaps to points and to the red, green and blue directions. Click. The status bar says \"Measured 4' 6\".\" and a guide point is left at the second point. To leave the guide point at an exact distance instead, type it after the first click and press Enter: it goes in the direction you are pointing. Place a guide line A guide line is an endless dashed line, parallel to an edge, at a distance you choose. Use guides to lay out a setback, a floor height, or where a window starts. Press T. Click on an edge (not its end). The tip says \"On edge\". Move off the edge, to the side the guide should go. A dashed line follows, parallel to the edge, and the value box (Offset) shows the distance. Click, or type the offset and press Enter: 1.5m, 5'. The status bar says \"Guide line 1.5 m from the edge.\" Starting on an existing guide line makes another guide parallel to it, so you can step a grid out from one guide. If you type an offset without moving off the edge first, ModelCraft cannot tell which side you mean: \"Move off the edge first to show which side.\" A guide at an angle: Protractor Click Protractor in the Modify group. Click the center: the point the guide passes through. On a face, the protractor lies on that face; elsewhere it lies flat. After this click, →, ← or ↑ stands it square to red, green or blue instead (press again to undo). Click a point that sets 0°, for example along an edge. Move to the angle you want and click: it snaps to 15° steps. Or type the angle in degrees, such as 22.5, and press Enter; the guide goes to the side the pointer is on. The guide line passes through the center at that angle. Draw along it with Line for a roof pitch, a chamfer or a splayed wall. Draw against guides Drawing tools snap to guide lines (\"On guide\") and guide points (\"Guide point\"), and to where a guide line crosses an axis direction. Start a rectangle where two guides cross to place it exactly. Remove guides One guide: click it with Select and press Delete, or click it with the Eraser. All guides: with nothing selected, click Delete all guides in Entity info. Guides are saved in the .modelcraft file. They are not exported to other formats."},{"slug":"dimensions","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Dimension and resize what you drew","summary":"Place dimensions between corners, on arcs and circles, or between two edges; type a new length to resize the model. Retype an edge's length or a group's width, depth and height in Entity info.","section":"tasks","order":85,"keywords":["dimension","dimensions","resize","change size","edit size","length","width","depth","height","stretch","driving dimension","annotate","measure","after the fact","radius","diameter","angle","angular","arc","circle","R","Ø"],"headings":[{"id":"place-a-dimension","text":"Place a dimension","level":2},{"id":"radius-diameter-and-angle","text":"Radius, diameter and angle","level":2},{"id":"resize-the-model-with-a-dimension","text":"Resize the model with a dimension","level":2},{"id":"what-moves","text":"What moves","level":3},{"id":"when-a-resize-is-refused","text":"When a resize is refused","level":3},{"id":"retype-a-size-in-entity-info","text":"Retype a size in Entity info","level":2},{"id":"dimensions-follow-the-model","text":"Dimensions follow the model","level":2},{"id":"remove-dimensions","text":"Remove dimensions","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ESizes in ModelCraft are not fixed once something is drawn. A dimension shows a length on the model, and typing a new length into it resizes the model to match. You can also retype an edge&#039;s length, or a group&#039;s width, depth and height, in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"place-a-dimension\"\u003EPlace a dimension\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"D\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDimension\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a corner to measure from. It snaps to corners, midpoints and edges, as drawing does.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the second corner.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOr click once on an \u003Cstrong\u003Eedge\u003C/strong\u003E (the tip says &quot;On edge&quot;) to dimension that whole edge in one click.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove the pointer to where the dimension line should go, and click. You can also type the offset, such as \u003Ccode\u003E0.5m\u003C/code\u003E, and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe dimension line stays level for a level edge, unless you are looking at it from the side. For an upright edge it stays in the upright plane that faces you best.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe status bar says &quot;Dimension 4 m.&quot; Dimensions show their length in the model&#039;s unit and update when you change the unit in the picker.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"radius-diameter-and-angle\"\u003ERadius, diameter and angle\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe same \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDimension\u003C/span\u003E tool dimensions round things and angles:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAn arc:\u003C/strong\u003E click the arc. The tip says \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERadius\u003C/span\u003E. Move the pointer to where the text should go and click: the dimension reads \u003Cstrong\u003ER 1.5 m\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA circle:\u003C/strong\u003E click the circle. You get a \u003Cstrong\u003Ediameter\u003C/strong\u003E (\u003Cstrong\u003EØ 3 m\u003C/strong\u003E) with a line across the circle through its centre. Polygons work the same way, with their corners on the circle.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe angle between two edges:\u003C/strong\u003E click one edge, then click a second edge that meets it at a corner (while you are over it, the tip says \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAngle between the edges\u003C/span\u003E). Then move into the angle you want, and click. Inside the corner you get the angle between the edges; move to one side and you get the angle beside it (for example 150° instead of 30°). How far from the corner you click sets where the arc goes; or type that distance.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo switch a radius to a diameter or back, select the dimension and choose in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow as\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EResizing with them:\u003C/strong\u003E type a new radius or diameter right after placing one, or select it later and type. The arc or circle keeps its centre and changes size; a face it bounds (a disc, the end of a slot) changes with it. Two things only measure:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAn angle dimension\u003C/strong\u003E never changes the model. To change an angle, rotate the edges with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERotate\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe circle at the end of a cylinder\u003C/strong\u003E (a circle you pulled with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPush/pull\u003C/span\u003E) cannot be resized from its dimension, because its sides would have to change too. Scale the cylinder instead, about its axis.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"resize-the-model-with-a-dimension\"\u003EResize the model with a dimension\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ERight after placing a dimension, type a new length and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003E6m\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E3500\u003C/code\u003E in a millimeter model. The model changes to that size.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELater, at any time:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E, click the dimension&#039;s text or line. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E says \u003Cstrong\u003EDimension\u003C/strong\u003E and shows its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELength\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType the new length and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. You can type straight into the value box, or into the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELength\u003C/span\u003E box in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"what-moves\"\u003EWhat moves\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe second corner you clicked moves, and so does everything beyond it:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBoth corners on one piece\u003C/strong\u003E (a box&#039;s width, a wall&#039;s height): the side at the moving corner slides out or in, and faces joined to it stretch. A box stays a closed solid, and anything sitting wholly on that side, such as a window component, moves with it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe corners on two separate pieces\u003C/strong\u003E (the gap between two boxes): the moving corner&#039;s piece moves whole, and the gap changes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe moving corner inside a group or component\u003C/strong\u003E that the other corner is not in: the whole group moves.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBoth corners inside the same group or component\u003C/strong\u003E: it is stretched inside. A component changes everywhere it is placed, as editing it always does.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo change which end moves, select the dimension and choose in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMoves\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EChoice\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat happens\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESecond end\u003C/span\u003E (the default)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe end you clicked second moves\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFirst end\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe end you clicked first moves\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBoth ends (centered)\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEach end moves half the change, so the middle stays put\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"when-a-resize-is-refused\"\u003EWhen a resize is refused\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EModelCraft changes nothing and says why in the status bar:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;a face would bend out of its plane&quot;: the dimension runs at a slant across the shape. Dimension along an edge, or along red, green or blue.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;a face would shrink to nothing or turn inside out&quot;: the new length is too short for the shape.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;the end that moves is not on a corner of the model&quot;: that end was placed in empty space or on the middle of an edge. Choose the other end in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMoves\u003C/span\u003E, or place the dimension on two corners.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;a length must be above zero&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery resize is one step: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"retype-a-size-in-entity-info\"\u003ERetype a size in Entity info\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou don&#039;t always need a dimension:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAn edge:\u003C/strong\u003E select it. Its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELength\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E is a box you can type into. Typing a length into the value box works too. The end further along red, then green, then blue moves, and what is joined to it stretches. For example, retyping the front bottom edge of a box makes the box wider to the right.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA group or component:\u003C/strong\u003E select it. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWidth (red)\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDepth (green)\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHeight (blue)\u003C/span\u003E are boxes. Its far side (right, back, top) moves. These are the group&#039;s own axes, before any rotation you gave it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to apply, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to put the old value back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"dimensions-follow-the-model\"\u003EDimensions follow the model\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA dimension is attached to the two corners it was placed on (an angle: to its corner and the far ends of its two edges; a radius or diameter: to its arc or circle). Move, push/pull or resize the geometry, and the dimension follows and shows the new length. It stays attached when you make the geometry into a group or component.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf an end was placed somewhere that isn&#039;t a corner (on a face, or in empty space), or its corner is erased, that end stays where it was. The dimension still measures, but it can only resize from its other end.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"remove-dimensions\"\u003ERemove dimensions\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOne dimension: select it and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or click it with the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEraser\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAll dimensions: with nothing selected, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete all dimensions\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDimensions are saved in the \u003Ccode\u003E.modelcraft\u003C/code\u003E file. They are not exported to other formats, which carry only the 3D model. A file with dimensions still opens in an older ModelCraft, without them.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Sizes in ModelCraft are not fixed once something is drawn. A dimension shows a length on the model, and typing a new length into it resizes the model to match. You can also retype an edge's length, or a group's width, depth and height, in Entity info. Place a dimension Press D, or click Dimension in the toolbar. Click a corner to measure from. It snaps to corners, midpoints and edges, as drawing does. Click the second corner. Or click once on an edge (the tip says \"On edge\") to dimension that whole edge in one click. Move the pointer to where the dimension line should go, and click. You can also type the offset, such as 0.5m, and press Enter. The dimension line stays level for a level edge, unless you are looking at it from the side. For an upright edge it stays in the upright plane that faces you best. The status bar says \"Dimension 4 m.\" Dimensions show their length in the model's unit and update when you change the unit in the picker. Radius, diameter and angle The same Dimension tool dimensions round things and angles: An arc: click the arc. The tip says Radius. Move the pointer to where the text should go and click: the dimension reads R 1.5 m. A circle: click the circle. You get a diameter (Ø 3 m) with a line across the circle through its centre. Polygons work the same way, with their corners on the circle. The angle between two edges: click one edge, then click a second edge that meets it at a corner (while you are over it, the tip says Angle between the edges). Then move into the angle you want, and click. Inside the corner you get the angle between the edges; move to one side and you get the angle beside it (for example 150° instead of 30°). How far from the corner you click sets where the arc goes; or type that distance. To switch a radius to a diameter or back, select the dimension and choose in Show as in Entity info. Resizing with them: type a new radius or diameter right after placing one, or select it later and type. The arc or circle keeps its centre and changes size; a face it bounds (a disc, the end of a slot) changes with it. Two things only measure: An angle dimension never changes the model. To change an angle, rotate the edges with Rotate. The circle at the end of a cylinder (a circle you pulled with Push/pull) cannot be resized from its dimension, because its sides would have to change too. Scale the cylinder instead, about its axis. Resize the model with a dimension Right after placing a dimension, type a new length and press Enter: 6m, 12', 3500 in a millimeter model. The model changes to that size. Later, at any time: With Select, click the dimension's text or line. Entity info says Dimension and shows its Length. Type the new length and press Enter. You can type straight into the value box, or into the Length box in Entity info. What moves The second corner you clicked moves, and so does everything beyond it: Both corners on one piece (a box's width, a wall's height): the side at the moving corner slides out or in, and faces joined to it stretch. A box stays a closed solid, and anything sitting wholly on that side, such as a window component, moves with it. The corners on two separate pieces (the gap between two boxes): the moving corner's piece moves whole, and the gap changes. The moving corner inside a group or component that the other corner is not in: the whole group moves. Both corners inside the same group or component: it is stretched inside. A component changes everywhere it is placed, as editing it always does. To change which end moves, select the dimension and choose in Moves in Entity info: Choice What happens Second end (the default) The end you clicked second moves First end The end you clicked first moves Both ends (centered) Each end moves half the change, so the middle stays put When a resize is refused ModelCraft changes nothing and says why in the status bar: \"a face would bend out of its plane\": the dimension runs at a slant across the shape. Dimension along an edge, or along red, green or blue. \"a face would shrink to nothing or turn inside out\": the new length is too short for the shape. \"the end that moves is not on a corner of the model\": that end was placed in empty space or on the middle of an edge. Choose the other end in Moves, or place the dimension on two corners. \"a length must be above zero\". Every resize is one step: Ctrl+Z undoes it. Retype a size in Entity info You don't always need a dimension: An edge: select it. Its Length in Entity info is a box you can type into. Typing a length into the value box works too. The end further along red, then green, then blue moves, and what is joined to it stretches. For example, retyping the front bottom edge of a box makes the box wider to the right. A group or component: select it. Width (red), Depth (green) and Height (blue) are boxes. Its far side (right, back, top) moves. These are the group's own axes, before any rotation you gave it. Press Enter to apply, or Esc to put the old value back. Dimensions follow the model A dimension is attached to the two corners it was placed on (an angle: to its corner and the far ends of its two edges; a radius or diameter: to its arc or circle). Move, push/pull or resize the geometry, and the dimension follows and shows the new length. It stays attached when you make the geometry into a group or component. If an end was placed somewhere that isn't a corner (on a face, or in empty space), or its corner is erased, that end stays where it was. The dimension still measures, but it can only resize from its other end. Remove dimensions One dimension: select it and press Delete, or click it with the Eraser. All dimensions: with nothing selected, click Delete all dimensions in Entity info. Dimensions are saved in the .modelcraft file. They are not exported to other formats, which carry only the 3D model. A file with dimensions still opens in an older ModelCraft, without them."},{"slug":"erase","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Erase and clean up","summary":"Erase edges, groups and guides with the eraser or the Delete key, heal split faces, and fix faces that face the wrong way.","section":"tasks","order":90,"keywords":["erase","eraser","delete","remove","heal","merge faces","clean up","stray lines","reverse face","back face","undo"],"headings":[{"id":"erase-with-the-eraser","text":"Erase with the eraser","level":2},{"id":"what-happens-to-the-faces","text":"What happens to the faces","level":2},{"id":"erase-with-the-delete-key","text":"Erase with the Delete key","level":2},{"id":"clean-up-stray-lines-on-a-flat-face","text":"Clean up stray lines on a flat face","level":2},{"id":"a-face-facing-the-wrong-way","text":"A face facing the wrong way","level":2},{"id":"undo-a-mistake","text":"Undo a mistake","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"erase-with-the-eraser\"\u003EErase with the eraser\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"E\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEraser\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick an edge, a group or component, or a guide. It is erased.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo erase several, press and drag across them. Everything you pass over highlights, and all of it goes when you release.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe eraser does not pick faces: a face goes when you erase the edges it needs. Faces are what the eraser looks through to find edges.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-happens-to-the-faces\"\u003EWhat happens to the faces\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAn edge between two faces in the same plane\u003C/strong\u003E (a line you drew across a floor, for example) disappears and the two faces \u003Cstrong\u003Eheal\u003C/strong\u003E into one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAn edge that a face needs to stay closed\u003C/strong\u003E (the edge of a box) takes that face with it. Erase one top edge of a box and the top and one side go, leaving an open box.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELoose ends\u003C/strong\u003E (points no edge uses any more) are cleaned up.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EErasing the last copy of a component also removes the component from the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EComponents\u003C/span\u003E panel.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"erase-with-the-delete-key\"\u003EErase with the Delete key\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect what you want to remove with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis removes faces too. A face deleted this way leaves its edges behind, so you can delete the top of a box and keep its outline, then draw something else there.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"clean-up-stray-lines-on-a-flat-face\"\u003EClean up stray lines on a flat face\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf a wall or floor shows lines you don&#039;t want (left over from drawing), erase them with the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEraser\u003C/span\u003E. Lines lying in the face heal away; the face stays.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-face-facing-the-wrong-way\"\u003EA face facing the wrong way\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA face seen from behind is blue-grey. If a face shows blue-grey on the outside of your model:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect the face.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReverse face\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFace direction matters most when you export: renderers, game engines and 3D printers may hide or misread faces that point inward.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"undo-a-mistake\"\u003EUndo a mistake\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes one step at a time, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Y\"\u003E⌘Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command Z\"\u003E⇧⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) redoes. A drag with the eraser is one step, however many things it took. The status bar names what was undone: &quot;Undid erase.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Erase with the eraser Press E for the Eraser. Click an edge, a group or component, or a guide. It is erased. To erase several, press and drag across them. Everything you pass over highlights, and all of it goes when you release. The eraser does not pick faces: a face goes when you erase the edges it needs. Faces are what the eraser looks through to find edges. What happens to the faces An edge between two faces in the same plane (a line you drew across a floor, for example) disappears and the two faces heal into one. An edge that a face needs to stay closed (the edge of a box) takes that face with it. Erase one top edge of a box and the top and one side go, leaving an open box. Loose ends (points no edge uses any more) are cleaned up. Erasing the last copy of a component also removes the component from the Components panel. Erase with the Delete key Select what you want to remove with Select. Press Delete or Backspace. This removes faces too. A face deleted this way leaves its edges behind, so you can delete the top of a box and keep its outline, then draw something else there. Clean up stray lines on a flat face If a wall or floor shows lines you don't want (left over from drawing), erase them with the Eraser. Lines lying in the face heal away; the face stays. A face facing the wrong way A face seen from behind is blue-grey. If a face shows blue-grey on the outside of your model: Select the face. Click Reverse face in Entity info. Face direction matters most when you export: renderers, game engines and 3D printers may hide or misread faces that point inward. Undo a mistake Ctrl+Z undoes one step at a time, Ctrl+Y (or Ctrl+Shift+Z) redoes. A drag with the eraser is one step, however many things it took. The status bar names what was undone: \"Undid erase.\""},{"slug":"site-plan","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Model on a site plan from a DWG or DXF","summary":"Bring a CAD site or floor plan in as lines on the ground, answer the units question, and trace it into faces and masses.","section":"tasks","order":100,"keywords":["site plan","dwg","dxf","cad","import","text","hatch","spline","ellipse","3d face","trace","floor plan","lot","property line","units","underlay","context"],"headings":[{"id":"import-the-drawing","text":"Import the drawing","level":2},{"id":"what-comes-in","text":"What comes in","level":2},{"id":"trace-it-into-faces","text":"Trace it into faces","level":2},{"id":"troubles","text":"Troubles","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EModelCraft can bring in the lines of a 2D CAD drawing, a site plan or a floor plan, and lay them on the ground so you can model on top of them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"import-the-drawing\"\u003EImport the drawing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImport\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E group (or drop the file on the 3D view).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick a \u003Ccode\u003E.dwg\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E.dxf\u003C/code\u003E file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA large DWG takes a moment: the status bar says &quot;Reading plan.dwg…&quot; until it is in.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf the drawing does not say what its units are, a dialog titled &quot;Drawing units&quot; asks: &quot;What is one drawing unit?&quot; Choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInches\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFeet\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMillimeters\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMeters\u003C/span\u003E. Most US site plans are drawn in feet, architectural plans in inches, and metric plans in millimeters.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe drawing arrives as a group of edges on the ground, selected, and the view zooms to it. The status bar says how many edges came in, for example &quot;Imported site.dwg: 1697 edges on the ground, in a group. Explode it to draw on it directly.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-comes-in\"\u003EWhat comes in\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELines, polylines (including their arcs), circles and arcs, and blocks with everything in them, from \u003Cstrong\u003Emodel space\u003C/strong\u003E only. Curves become short straight pieces.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELines, polylines, arcs, circles, ellipses and splines land flat on the ground, as edges.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EHatches\u003C/strong\u003E become faces on the ground, with their islands as holes, so you can push/pull a hatched area straight away.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E3D faces\u003C/strong\u003E and solid fills become faces; 3D faces keep their heights.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EText\u003C/strong\u003E (single-line text, multiline text and attribute values) is drawn as edges in a simple single-stroke lettering, at the text&#039;s own height, angle and alignment. Its font is not the drawing&#039;s, so the words may run a little longer or shorter. Characters the lettering has no shape for are drawn as small boxes and counted.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDimensions\u003C/strong\u003E are drawn as the drawing shows them (their lines, arrowheads and text, as edges and faces).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENot imported: points, tables, multileaders, regions, 3D solids, images and other object types. The status bar lists them by type, such as &quot;Not imported: 12 POINT.&quot; Anything on a layout (paper space) is left out too, without being listed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELayers are not kept: all lines come in alike.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf the drawing is far from its origin (state plane coordinates, for instance), it still imports, but it may land far from ModelCraft&#039;s origin. Drawings reaching more than 10,000 km from their origin are refused: move the drawing nearer to 0,0 in the CAD program first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"trace-it-into-faces\"\u003ETrace it into faces\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe imported lines rarely close neatly into faces by themselves. Two ways to work:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EKeep the plan as an underlay (recommended)\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELeave the plan as its own group.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDraw your building on top with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERectangle\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELine\u003C/span\u003E, snapping to the plan&#039;s corners (&quot;Endpoint&quot;) and edges (&quot;On edge&quot;).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPush/pull your faces up. The plan stays untouched underneath, and you can erase the whole group later with one click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESnapping works across groups: you can snap to the plan&#039;s corners without opening it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDraw on the plan&#039;s own lines\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect the plan group and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExplode\u003C/span\u003E. Its lines become loose edges.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhere a closed outline should be a face, draw one line over any of its edges with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELine\u003C/span\u003E, from corner to corner. When the outline is closed and flat, the face usually fills in. If it doesn&#039;t, look for a gap or an overlap in the outline and draw over it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPush/pull it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis works best on a clean plan. On a busy survey, tracing on top is faster.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"troubles\"\u003ETroubles\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;This is a binary DXF. Save it as ASCII DXF and try again.&quot; Save the DXF again from the CAD program as ASCII (text).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;…has no lines, arcs or circles in model space to bring in.&quot; The drawing is probably all on layouts, or all text and hatches.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;This drawing could not be read (…). It may be damaged, or saved in a DWG version the reader does not handle…&quot; The brackets say why, for example &quot;DWG version AC1009 is not supported&quot;. Save it as a DXF, or as a DWG from AutoCAD 2000 to 2018, from the CAD program.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe plan is the wrong size: you picked the wrong unit. Undo (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) and import again, or \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/rotate-scale\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/rotate-scale\"\u003Escale\u003C/a\u003E the group by the right factor (12 between feet and inches, 25.4 between inches and millimeters).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo turn contour lines into ground you can sculpt, see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/terrain\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/terrain\"\u003ETerrain\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"ModelCraft can bring in the lines of a 2D CAD drawing, a site plan or a floor plan, and lay them on the ground so you can model on top of them. Import the drawing Click Import in the File group (or drop the file on the 3D view). Pick a .dwg or .dxf file. A large DWG takes a moment: the status bar says \"Reading plan.dwg…\" until it is in. If the drawing does not say what its units are, a dialog titled \"Drawing units\" asks: \"What is one drawing unit?\" Choose Inches, Feet, Millimeters or Meters. Most US site plans are drawn in feet, architectural plans in inches, and metric plans in millimeters. The drawing arrives as a group of edges on the ground, selected, and the view zooms to it. The status bar says how many edges came in, for example \"Imported site.dwg: 1697 edges on the ground, in a group. Explode it to draw on it directly.\" What comes in Lines, polylines (including their arcs), circles and arcs, and blocks with everything in them, from model space only. Curves become short straight pieces. Lines, polylines, arcs, circles, ellipses and splines land flat on the ground, as edges. Hatches become faces on the ground, with their islands as holes, so you can push/pull a hatched area straight away. 3D faces and solid fills become faces; 3D faces keep their heights. Text (single-line text, multiline text and attribute values) is drawn as edges in a simple single-stroke lettering, at the text's own height, angle and alignment. Its font is not the drawing's, so the words may run a little longer or shorter. Characters the lettering has no shape for are drawn as small boxes and counted. Dimensions are drawn as the drawing shows them (their lines, arrowheads and text, as edges and faces). Not imported: points, tables, multileaders, regions, 3D solids, images and other object types. The status bar lists them by type, such as \"Not imported: 12 POINT.\" Anything on a layout (paper space) is left out too, without being listed. Layers are not kept: all lines come in alike. If the drawing is far from its origin (state plane coordinates, for instance), it still imports, but it may land far from ModelCraft's origin. Drawings reaching more than 10,000 km from their origin are refused: move the drawing nearer to 0,0 in the CAD program first. Trace it into faces The imported lines rarely close neatly into faces by themselves. Two ways to work: Keep the plan as an underlay (recommended) Leave the plan as its own group. Draw your building on top with Rectangle and Line, snapping to the plan's corners (\"Endpoint\") and edges (\"On edge\"). Push/pull your faces up. The plan stays untouched underneath, and you can erase the whole group later with one click. Snapping works across groups: you can snap to the plan's corners without opening it. Draw on the plan's own lines Select the plan group and click Explode. Its lines become loose edges. Where a closed outline should be a face, draw one line over any of its edges with Line, from corner to corner. When the outline is closed and flat, the face usually fills in. If it doesn't, look for a gap or an overlap in the outline and draw over it. Push/pull it. This works best on a clean plan. On a busy survey, tracing on top is faster. Troubles \"This is a binary DXF. Save it as ASCII DXF and try again.\" Save the DXF again from the CAD program as ASCII (text). \"…has no lines, arcs or circles in model space to bring in.\" The drawing is probably all on layouts, or all text and hatches. \"This drawing could not be read (…). It may be damaged, or saved in a DWG version the reader does not handle…\" The brackets say why, for example \"DWG version AC1009 is not supported\". Save it as a DXF, or as a DWG from AutoCAD 2000 to 2018, from the CAD program. The plan is the wrong size: you picked the wrong unit. Undo (Ctrl+Z) and import again, or scale the group by the right factor (12 between feet and inches, 25.4 between inches and millimeters). To turn contour lines into ground you can sculpt, see Terrain."},{"slug":"styles-shadows","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Styles and sun shadows","summary":"Show the model shaded, as hidden lines, as a wireframe or see-through, and study the shadows it casts at any date, time and place.","section":"tasks","order":110,"keywords":["presentation view","clean view","hide guides","hide axes","style","shaded","hidden line","wireframe","x-ray","edges","shadows","sun","sun study","solar","date","time","latitude","longitude","time zone","north"],"headings":[{"id":"faces","text":"Faces","level":2},{"id":"turn-on-sun-shadows","text":"Turn on sun shadows","level":2},{"id":"point-the-model-north","text":"Point the model north","level":2},{"id":"a-sun-study","text":"A sun study","level":2},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2},{"id":"presentation-view","text":"Presentation view","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStyle\u003C/span\u003E panel on the right changes how the model is drawn. Nothing in it changes the model itself, and it is not part of undo. Each \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/scenes\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/scenes\"\u003Escene\u003C/a\u003E remembers its own style, and the file remembers the style you had when you saved.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"faces\"\u003EFaces\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose one in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFaces\u003C/span\u003E list:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EStyle\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ELooks like\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShaded\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESolid faces, lit softly. Faces seen from behind are blue-grey. Imported colors show.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHidden line\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFaces in the background color with edges in ink: a clean line drawing with hidden lines removed. White on \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaper\u003C/span\u003E, dark on \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWireframe\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdges only. Faces are not drawn, but they still count when you click and snap: you cannot pick through them.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EX-ray\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESee-through faces, to see what is behind or inside. As in wireframe, clicks and snaps still stop at the nearest face.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow edges\u003C/span\u003E turns the edges on or off in every style except \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWireframe\u003C/span\u003E. With it off, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHidden line\u003C/span\u003E shows almost nothing, since its faces match the background. Soft edges, such as the sides of a pulled circle, are never drawn.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"turn-on-sun-shadows\"\u003ETurn on sun shadows\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESun shadows\u003C/span\u003E. The sun settings below it become active, and the model casts shadows on itself and on the ground.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet the place:\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELatitude\u003C/span\u003E: north is positive, south negative (New York is about 40.7, Sydney about -33.9).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELongitude\u003C/span\u003E: east is positive, west negative (New York is about -74.0).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUTC offset\u003C/span\u003E: the site&#039;s offset from UTC in hours, such as \u003Ccode\u003E-5\u003C/code\u003E for New York in winter. ModelCraft does not apply daylight saving time; use \u003Ccode\u003E-4\u003C/code\u003E for New York in summer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDate\u003C/span\u003E (month and day) and drag the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETime\u003C/span\u003E slider. The time moves in 15-minute steps and shows next to the slider, such as &quot;3:00 pm&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA line under the settings says where the sun is, such as &quot;Sun 52° up, 250° from north. North is the green axis.&quot; At night it says &quot;The sun is down at this time.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA new model starts with the sun on June 21 at 3:00 pm, at latitude 40, longitude -105, UTC offset -7.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"point-the-model-north\"\u003EPoint the model north\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EModelCraft&#039;s north is always the \u003Cstrong\u003Egreen axis\u003C/strong\u003E. To study a real site, turn the model so its north side faces along green: select everything (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command A\"\u003E⌘A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Q\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, click a center on the ground, and turn it by the site&#039;s angle from north (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/rotate-scale\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/rotate-scale\"\u003ERotate and scale\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-sun-study\"\u003EA sun study\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet up the place and the date.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd a scene (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd scene\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScenes\u003C/span\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove the time slider, then add another scene. Repeat for each hour you want.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlay\u003C/span\u003E to step through them. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/scenes\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/scenes\"\u003ESave views as scenes\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EShadows can look jagged on a very large model, since one shadow map covers the whole model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EShadows are not drawn in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWireframe\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe sun position follows the NOAA solar calculation and is accurate to a fraction of a degree, which is plenty for massing studies. Atmospheric refraction is ignored, so very low sun angles near sunrise and sunset can be off slightly.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"presentation-view\"\u003EPresentation view\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EViews\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPresentation view\u003C/span\u003E (or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift P\"\u003E⇧P\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+P\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) shows only the model: the axes, guide lines and guide points, section planes&#039; outlines (the cut stays), the box of an open group, the snapping marks and tips, and the selection highlight all go. Use it to look at the model clearly, or before a screenshot. Tools still work, and still snap; you just do not see the marks. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift P\"\u003E⇧P\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+P\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E again to bring them back. Dimensions stay, as they are part of the drawing.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The Style panel on the right changes how the model is drawn. Nothing in it changes the model itself, and it is not part of undo. Each scene remembers its own style, and the file remembers the style you had when you saved. Faces Choose one in the Faces list: Style Looks like Shaded Solid faces, lit softly. Faces seen from behind are blue-grey. Imported colors show. Hidden line Faces in the background color with edges in ink: a clean line drawing with hidden lines removed. White on Paper, dark on Model. Wireframe Edges only. Faces are not drawn, but they still count when you click and snap: you cannot pick through them. X-ray See-through faces, to see what is behind or inside. As in wireframe, clicks and snaps still stop at the nearest face. Show edges turns the edges on or off in every style except Wireframe. With it off, Hidden line shows almost nothing, since its faces match the background. Soft edges, such as the sides of a pulled circle, are never drawn. Turn on sun shadows Tick Sun shadows. The sun settings below it become active, and the model casts shadows on itself and on the ground. Set the place: Latitude: north is positive, south negative (New York is about 40.7, Sydney about -33.9). Longitude: east is positive, west negative (New York is about -74.0). UTC offset: the site's offset from UTC in hours, such as -5 for New York in winter. ModelCraft does not apply daylight saving time; use -4 for New York in summer. Set the Date (month and day) and drag the Time slider. The time moves in 15-minute steps and shows next to the slider, such as \"3:00 pm\". A line under the settings says where the sun is, such as \"Sun 52° up, 250° from north. North is the green axis.\" At night it says \"The sun is down at this time.\" A new model starts with the sun on June 21 at 3:00 pm, at latitude 40, longitude -105, UTC offset -7. Point the model north ModelCraft's north is always the green axis. To study a real site, turn the model so its north side faces along green: select everything (Ctrl+A), press Q, click a center on the ground, and turn it by the site's angle from north (see Rotate and scale). A sun study Set up the place and the date. Add a scene (Add scene in Scenes). Move the time slider, then add another scene. Repeat for each hour you want. Click Play to step through them. See Save views as scenes. Things to know Shadows can look jagged on a very large model, since one shadow map covers the whole model. Shadows are not drawn in Wireframe. The sun position follows the NOAA solar calculation and is accurate to a fraction of a degree, which is plenty for massing studies. Atmospheric refraction is ignored, so very low sun angles near sunrise and sunset can be off slightly. Presentation view Views › Presentation view (or Shift+P) shows only the model: the axes, guide lines and guide points, section planes' outlines (the cut stays), the box of an open group, the snapping marks and tips, and the selection highlight all go. Use it to look at the model clearly, or before a screenshot. Tools still work, and still snap; you just do not see the marks. Press Shift+P again to bring them back. Dimensions stay, as they are part of the drawing."},{"slug":"scenes","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Save views as scenes","summary":"Save a camera and style as a scene, update and rename it, and step or play through scenes to present a design.","section":"tasks","order":120,"keywords":["scene","view","saved view","camera","presentation","animation","play","slideshow","page up","page down","tabs","walkthrough"],"headings":[{"id":"add-a-scene","text":"Add a scene","level":2},{"id":"go-to-a-scene","text":"Go to a scene","level":2},{"id":"change-rename-or-delete-a-scene","text":"Change, rename or delete a scene","level":2},{"id":"present","text":"Present","level":2},{"id":"what-a-scene-shows","text":"What a scene shows","level":2},{"id":"export-an-animation","text":"Export an animation","level":2},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA \u003Cstrong\u003Escene\u003C/strong\u003E remembers a camera position, a style and what is hidden: where you are looking from, perspective or parallel, the face style, edges, the sun settings, hidden groups, tags turned off and the section cut. Scenes are saved in the \u003Ccode\u003E.modelcraft\u003C/code\u003E file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"add-a-scene\"\u003EAdd a scene\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet up the view: orbit, zoom, pick a style, set the sun.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScenes\u003C/span\u003E panel on the right, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd scene\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe scene appears in the list, named &quot;Scene 1&quot;, &quot;Scene 2&quot; and so on, and as a tab across the top of the 3D view. The status bar says &quot;Scene 1 saved: this camera, style and what is hidden.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"go-to-a-scene\"\u003EGo to a scene\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick its tab over the 3D view, or its name in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScenes\u003C/span\u003E list.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOr press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ↓\"\u003Efn ↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EPage Down\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for the next scene and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ↑\"\u003Efn ↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EPage Up\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for the previous one. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENext\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPrevious\u003C/span\u003E under the list do the same.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe camera glides to the scene in a little over half a second. If your computer is set to reduce motion, it jumps there instead.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"change-rename-or-delete-a-scene\"\u003EChange, rename or delete a scene\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach scene in the list has three small buttons after its name:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUpdate\u003C/strong\u003E (the circular arrows): saves the current camera, style and what is hidden into that scene. &quot;Scene 2 updated.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERename\u003C/strong\u003E (the pencil): opens a &quot;Rename scene&quot; dialog. Type the new name and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDelete\u003C/strong\u003E (the trash can): removes the scene. &quot;Scene 2 deleted. Ctrl+Z brings it back.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAdding, updating, renaming and deleting scenes are steps in undo, like changes to the model.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"present\"\u003EPresent\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd two or more scenes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlay\u003C/span\u003E under the list. ModelCraft moves to the next scene every few seconds and goes back to the first after the last.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStop\u003C/span\u003E (the same button) or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to stop.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor a sun study, add one scene per hour with only the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETime\u003C/span\u003E changed, then play them. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/styles-shadows#a-sun-study\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/styles-shadows\" data-hash=\"a-sun-study\"\u003EStyles and sun shadows\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo present without the bars, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E twice: full screen, then full screen with the bars hidden. The scene tabs stay over the view.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-a-scene-shows\"\u003EWhat a scene shows\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA scene remembers what was hidden when you saved it: groups and components you \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/hide-isolate\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/hide-isolate\"\u003Ehid\u003C/a\u003E, \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/tags\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/tags\"\u003Etags\u003C/a\u003E you turned off, and which \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/sections\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/sections\"\u003Esection plane\u003C/a\u003E cuts. Going to the scene hides and shows them again. So one scene can show the whole house, the next the house with the roof&#039;s tag off, the next a section through it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHidden loose faces and edges are not part of scenes. Going to a scene does not make an undo step. Scenes saved by an earlier build of ModelCraft leave what is hidden as it is; click Update to save it into them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"export-an-animation\"\u003EExport an animation\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport animation\u003C/span\u003E, under the list, plays the scenes from the first to the last, moving the camera smoothly between them, and saves the film as a \u003Cstrong\u003EWebM\u003C/strong\u003E video (two seconds&#039; move and a short pause per scene). It records the view as you see it, at its size on screen, in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/styles-shadows#presentation-view\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/styles-shadows\" data-hash=\"presentation-view\"\u003Epresentation view\u003C/a\u003E (no axes, guides or selection). Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or click the button again to stop without saving. WebM plays in browsers and VLC, and video editors convert it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChanging the style or moving the camera after going to a scene does not change the scene until you click Update.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EScenes are not exported to Collada, OBJ, glTF, STL or IFC.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"A scene remembers a camera position, a style and what is hidden: where you are looking from, perspective or parallel, the face style, edges, the sun settings, hidden groups, tags turned off and the section cut. Scenes are saved in the .modelcraft file. Add a scene Set up the view: orbit, zoom, pick a style, set the sun. In the Scenes panel on the right, click Add scene. The scene appears in the list, named \"Scene 1\", \"Scene 2\" and so on, and as a tab across the top of the 3D view. The status bar says \"Scene 1 saved: this camera, style and what is hidden.\" Go to a scene Click its tab over the 3D view, or its name in the Scenes list. Or press Page Down for the next scene and Page Up for the previous one. Next and Previous under the list do the same. The camera glides to the scene in a little over half a second. If your computer is set to reduce motion, it jumps there instead. Change, rename or delete a scene Each scene in the list has three small buttons after its name: Update (the circular arrows): saves the current camera, style and what is hidden into that scene. \"Scene 2 updated.\" Rename (the pencil): opens a \"Rename scene\" dialog. Type the new name and click Save. Delete (the trash can): removes the scene. \"Scene 2 deleted. Ctrl+Z brings it back.\" Adding, updating, renaming and deleting scenes are steps in undo, like changes to the model. Present Add two or more scenes. Click Play under the list. ModelCraft moves to the next scene every few seconds and goes back to the first after the last. Click Stop (the same button) or press Esc to stop. For a sun study, add one scene per hour with only the Time changed, then play them. See Styles and sun shadows. To present without the bars, press Ctrl+Shift+F twice: full screen, then full screen with the bars hidden. The scene tabs stay over the view. What a scene shows A scene remembers what was hidden when you saved it: groups and components you hid, tags you turned off, and which section plane cuts. Going to the scene hides and shows them again. So one scene can show the whole house, the next the house with the roof's tag off, the next a section through it. Hidden loose faces and edges are not part of scenes. Going to a scene does not make an undo step. Scenes saved by an earlier build of ModelCraft leave what is hidden as it is; click Update to save it into them. Export an animation Export animation, under the list, plays the scenes from the first to the last, moving the camera smoothly between them, and saves the film as a WebM video (two seconds' move and a short pause per scene). It records the view as you see it, at its size on screen, in presentation view (no axes, guides or selection). Press Esc or click the button again to stop without saving. WebM plays in browsers and VLC, and video editors convert it. Things to know Changing the style or moving the camera after going to a scene does not change the scene until you click Update. Scenes are not exported to Collada, OBJ, glTF, STL or IFC."},{"slug":"use-with-ai-assistants","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Use ModelCraft with an AI assistant","summary":"Let your own AI assistant or script look at the model and build simple solids: permissions, connecting through CadCraft Studio, and the activity log.","section":"tasks","order":125,"keywords":["ai","assistant","agent","mcp","model context protocol","claude","claude code","claude desktop","script","automation","modeling","box","cylinder","permissions","disconnect","activity"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"turn-it-on","text":"Turn it on","level":2},{"id":"connect-your-assistant","text":"Connect your assistant","level":2},{"id":"you-stay-in-charge","text":"You stay in charge","level":2},{"id":"what-an-assistant-can-ask-for","text":"What an assistant can ask for","level":2},{"id":"if-something-doesn-t-work","text":"If something doesn't work","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to let an AI assistant (or a script you wrote) work with ModelCraft, and how to decide what it is allowed to touch.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EModelCraft has \u003Cstrong\u003Eno AI of its own\u003C/strong\u003E and sends nothing anywhere. You bring the assistant: Claude Desktop, Claude Code or any program that speaks the Model Context Protocol (MCP). ModelCraft only gives it tools, and you choose which.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"turn-it-on\"\u003ETurn it on\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E (the gear in the app bar, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) and go to the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModelCraft\u003C/span\u003E tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAssistants\u003C/span\u003E, tick \u003Cstrong\u003ELet AI assistants and scripts use ModelCraft\u003C/strong\u003E. It is off until you do.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose what it may do: \u003Cstrong\u003ERead\u003C/strong\u003E is ticked to start with; tick \u003Cstrong\u003EEdit\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ESave\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003EExport\u003C/strong\u003E only when you need them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETools outside the permissions you ticked are not offered to the assistant, and if it asks for one anyway, ModelCraft refuses and writes it in the activity log. Turning the switch off ends every connection at once; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset ModelCraft settings\u003C/span\u003E turns it off too.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"connect-your-assistant\"\u003EConnect your assistant\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAssistants connect through \u003Cstrong\u003ECadCraft Studio\u003C/strong\u003E on your computer: Studio runs a small server only this computer can reach, protected by a token it makes each time it starts. In Studio, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConnect an assistant\u003C/span\u003E and copy the address and the token into your assistant (Studio shows the exact settings for Claude Code, Claude Desktop and other MCP clients). Never share the token.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA browser tab can&#039;t open a connection for an assistant: in the browser apps the switch has no effect until you use CadCraft Studio on your computer.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"you-stay-in-charge\"\u003EYou stay in charge\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEvery change is one undo step\u003C/strong\u003E, labelled with the assistant&#039;s name for it (for example &quot;Assistant: box&quot;). \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes back the assistant&#039;s last change as a whole.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EYou know when one is connected.\u003C/strong\u003E A button in the app bar names the assistant while it is connected; click it to see what it may do, or to \u003Cstrong\u003EDisconnect\u003C/strong\u003E it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EActivity.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModelCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAssistants\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EActivity\u003C/span\u003E lists every call: when, which tool, done or refused. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave the log\u003C/span\u003E keeps a copy.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-an-assistant-can-ask-for\"\u003EWhat an assistant can ask for\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETool\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EPermission\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eapp_info\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Emodel_describe\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERead\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe model&#039;s unit, size, and every group and component with where it is, whether it is a closed solid and its volume\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eview_screenshot\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERead\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA picture of the 3D view as shown\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Ebox_add\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ecylinder_add\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA box or a cylinder as a new group, at a place and size you or it give (&quot;3m&quot;, &quot;10&#039;&quot; or a number in the model&#039;s unit)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Einstance_move\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Einstances_erase\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMoves or erases groups and components\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eundo\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETakes back its own steps\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESolids an assistant makes come in as groups, so they never merge with what you drew. Saving and exporting stay with you.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"if-something-doesn-t-work\"\u003EIf something doesn&#039;t work\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe assistant sees no ModelCraft tools:\u003C/strong\u003E check that the switch is on in this window, and that the window is open in Studio.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;needs the edit permission&quot;:\u003C/strong\u003E tick \u003Cstrong\u003EEdit\u003C/strong\u003E under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAssistants\u003C/span\u003E, then ask again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;401&quot; or &quot;token&quot; errors:\u003C/strong\u003E the token changed (Studio restarted, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew token\u003C/span\u003E). Copy the new one into the assistant&#039;s settings.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to let an AI assistant (or a script you wrote) work with ModelCraft, and how to decide what it is allowed to touch. ModelCraft has no AI of its own and sends nothing anywhere. You bring the assistant: Claude Desktop, Claude Code or any program that speaks the Model Context Protocol (MCP). ModelCraft only gives it tools, and you choose which. Turn it on Open Settings (the gear in the app bar, or Ctrl+,) and go to the ModelCraft tab. Under Assistants, tick Let AI assistants and scripts use ModelCraft. It is off until you do. Choose what it may do: Read is ticked to start with; tick Edit, Save or Export only when you need them. Tools outside the permissions you ticked are not offered to the assistant, and if it asks for one anyway, ModelCraft refuses and writes it in the activity log. Turning the switch off ends every connection at once; Reset ModelCraft settings turns it off too. Connect your assistant Assistants connect through CadCraft Studio on your computer: Studio runs a small server only this computer can reach, protected by a token it makes each time it starts. In Studio, choose Connect an assistant and copy the address and the token into your assistant (Studio shows the exact settings for Claude Code, Claude Desktop and other MCP clients). Never share the token. A browser tab can't open a connection for an assistant: in the browser apps the switch has no effect until you use CadCraft Studio on your computer. You stay in charge Every change is one undo step, labelled with the assistant's name for it (for example \"Assistant: box\"). Ctrl+Z takes back the assistant's last change as a whole. You know when one is connected. A button in the app bar names the assistant while it is connected; click it to see what it may do, or to Disconnect it. Activity. Settings › ModelCraft › Assistants › Activity lists every call: when, which tool, done or refused. Save the log keeps a copy. What an assistant can ask for Tool Permission What it does app_info, model_describe Read The model's unit, size, and every group and component with where it is, whether it is a closed solid and its volume view_screenshot Read A picture of the 3D view as shown box_add, cylinder_add Edit A box or a cylinder as a new group, at a place and size you or it give (\"3m\", \"10'\" or a number in the model's unit) instance_move, instances_erase Edit Moves or erases groups and components undo Edit Takes back its own steps Solids an assistant makes come in as groups, so they never merge with what you drew. Saving and exporting stay with you. If something doesn't work The assistant sees no ModelCraft tools: check that the switch is on in this window, and that the window is open in Studio. \"needs the edit permission\": tick Edit under Assistants, then ask again. \"401\" or \"token\" errors: the token changed (Studio restarted, or New token). Copy the new one into the assistant's settings."},{"slug":"export-model","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Export a model for other programs","summary":"Choose the right export for rendering, the web, 3D printing, BIM and BimCraft, and check what each one keeps.","section":"tasks","order":130,"keywords":["print","pdf","paper","image","png","picture","screenshot","render","save view","export","share","render","rendering","gltf","glb","collada","dae","obj","stl","3d print","ifc","bim","bimcraft","send","other software"],"headings":[{"id":"export","text":"Export","level":2},{"id":"which-format","text":"Which format","level":2},{"id":"before-you-export","text":"Before you export","level":2},{"id":"send-a-model-to-bimcraft","text":"Send a model to BimCraft","level":2},{"id":"lines-without-faces","text":"Lines without faces","level":2},{"id":"save-the-view-as-an-image","text":"Save the view as an image","level":2},{"id":"print-the-view-or-save-it-as-a-pdf","text":"Print the view, or save it as a PDF","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EExport makes a copy of the model in another format. Keep your \u003Ccode\u003E.modelcraft\u003C/code\u003E file too: it is the only file that keeps everything, and the only one ModelCraft opens directly.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"export\"\u003EExport\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESave first (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), so your working file is safe.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E group.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the format. The browser downloads it, named like the model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe whole model is exported. To export one part, save, erase the rest, export, then undo or reopen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"which-format\"\u003EWhich format\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EYou want to\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EChoose\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENotes\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen it in another 3D modeler\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECollada (.dae)\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EKeeps groups, nested groups, components (shared, not copied), names and colors. Z up, meters.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERender it, or use it in a visualization program\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOBJ (.obj)\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EglTF binary (.glb)\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOBJ is the most widely read; it writes each group placement as a separate object, but has no colors and no shared components. glTF keeps components and colors.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPut it on the web, in a game engine or in AR\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EglTF binary (.glb)\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne file with everything in it.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPrint it in 3D\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESTL for printing (.stl, millimeters)\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWritten in millimeters, the unit printer software expects. Export closed solids.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen it in BimCraft or another BIM program\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIFC for BimCraft (.ifc)\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEach top-level group or component becomes one building element.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe status bar confirms each export, for example &quot;Exported House.stl (millimeters).&quot; or &quot;Exported House.dae (Collada 1.4.1, Z up, meters; components stay shared).&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"before-you-export\"\u003EBefore you export\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EGroup your objects.\u003C/strong\u003E Collada, glTF and IFC keep groups and components as separate objects. Loose geometry goes out as one lump; in IFC it becomes one element called &quot;Loose geometry&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EName them.\u003C/strong\u003E Group and component names go into Collada and IFC, so a wall called &quot;North wall&quot; arrives as &quot;North wall&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECheck face direction.\u003C/strong\u003E Faces showing blue-grey on the outside will point inward in the export. Fix them with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReverse face\u003C/span\u003E (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/erase#a-face-facing-the-wrong-way\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/erase\" data-hash=\"a-face-facing-the-wrong-way\"\u003EErase and clean up\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFor printing,\u003C/strong\u003E make the parts closed solids: select each group and check that \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E says &quot;Solid: Yes&quot;. Then scale the model to the printed size, or scale it in the printer software.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"send-a-model-to-bimcraft\"\u003ESend a model to BimCraft\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMake each building part a group or component and name it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIFC for BimCraft (.ifc)\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the downloaded \u003Ccode\u003E.ifc\u003C/code\u003E file in BimCraft.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThere is no direct link between the two apps yet. The IFC has one storey, and every element is a generic building element proxy with no type or properties: it carries shape and name only. Closed groups become solid shapes; open ones become surfaces.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"lines-without-faces\"\u003ELines without faces\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEdges that are not part of any face (guide-like lines, an imported site plan) go into OBJ and Collada as lines. glTF and STL have no place for them and leave them out. Guides and scenes are not exported to any format.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"save-the-view-as-an-image\"\u003ESave the view as an image\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImage of the view (.png)\u003C/span\u003E saves exactly what the 3D view shows, at its size on screen. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImage at twice the size (.png)\u003C/span\u003E saves it twice as wide and twice as tall (for print or a sharp slide), with dimensions and marks redrawn at that size rather than stretched.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor a clean picture, turn on \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/styles-shadows#presentation-view\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/styles-shadows\" data-hash=\"presentation-view\"\u003Epresentation view\u003C/a\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift P\"\u003E⇧P\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+P\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) first: the axes, guides, section outlines and the selection are left out. Set up the camera, style and shadows the way you want them, or go to a \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/scenes\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/scenes\"\u003Escene\u003C/a\u003E; to save a moving film of your scenes, use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport animation\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScenes\u003C/span\u003E panel.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"print-the-view-or-save-it-as-a-pdf\"\u003EPrint the view, or save it as a PDF\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPDF of the view (.pdf)\u003C/span\u003E saves a one-page PDF: the view at twice its screen size, with its dimensions, on an A4 page (Letter when the model is in feet and inches), landscape, with a line under it giving the file name, the date and the unit.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPrint the view…\u003C/span\u003E puts the same page in front of your browser&#039;s print dialog. Choose a printer, or &quot;Save as PDF&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe page shows the view as it is: there is no scale on a perspective view. For a drawing to scale, switch \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPerspective\u003C/span\u003E off and look from \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETop\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFront\u003C/span\u003E, and add \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/dimensions\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/dimensions\"\u003Edimensions\u003C/a\u003E for the sizes that matter. \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/styles-shadows#presentation-view\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/styles-shadows\" data-hash=\"presentation-view\"\u003EPresentation view\u003C/a\u003E leaves out the axes, guides and the selection; dimensions stay.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Export makes a copy of the model in another format. Keep your .modelcraft file too: it is the only file that keeps everything, and the only one ModelCraft opens directly. Export Save first (Ctrl+S), so your working file is safe. Click Export in the File group. Choose the format. The browser downloads it, named like the model. The whole model is exported. To export one part, save, erase the rest, export, then undo or reopen. Which format You want to Choose Notes Open it in another 3D modeler Collada (.dae) Keeps groups, nested groups, components (shared, not copied), names and colors. Z up, meters. Render it, or use it in a visualization program OBJ (.obj) or glTF binary (.glb) OBJ is the most widely read; it writes each group placement as a separate object, but has no colors and no shared components. glTF keeps components and colors. Put it on the web, in a game engine or in AR glTF binary (.glb) One file with everything in it. Print it in 3D STL for printing (.stl, millimeters) Written in millimeters, the unit printer software expects. Export closed solids. Open it in BimCraft or another BIM program IFC for BimCraft (.ifc) Each top-level group or component becomes one building element. The status bar confirms each export, for example \"Exported House.stl (millimeters).\" or \"Exported House.dae (Collada 1.4.1, Z up, meters; components stay shared).\" Before you export Group your objects. Collada, glTF and IFC keep groups and components as separate objects. Loose geometry goes out as one lump; in IFC it becomes one element called \"Loose geometry\". Name them. Group and component names go into Collada and IFC, so a wall called \"North wall\" arrives as \"North wall\". Check face direction. Faces showing blue-grey on the outside will point inward in the export. Fix them with Reverse face (see Erase and clean up). For printing, make the parts closed solids: select each group and check that Entity info says \"Solid: Yes\". Then scale the model to the printed size, or scale it in the printer software. Send a model to BimCraft Make each building part a group or component and name it. Choose Export, then IFC for BimCraft (.ifc). Open the downloaded .ifc file in BimCraft. There is no direct link between the two apps yet. The IFC has one storey, and every element is a generic building element proxy with no type or properties: it carries shape and name only. Closed groups become solid shapes; open ones become surfaces. Lines without faces Edges that are not part of any face (guide-like lines, an imported site plan) go into OBJ and Collada as lines. glTF and STL have no place for them and leave them out. Guides and scenes are not exported to any format. Save the view as an image Export › Image of the view (.png) saves exactly what the 3D view shows, at its size on screen. Image at twice the size (.png) saves it twice as wide and twice as tall (for print or a sharp slide), with dimensions and marks redrawn at that size rather than stretched. For a clean picture, turn on presentation view (Shift+P) first: the axes, guides, section outlines and the selection are left out. Set up the camera, style and shadows the way you want them, or go to a scene; to save a moving film of your scenes, use Export animation in the Scenes panel. Print the view, or save it as a PDF Export › PDF of the view (.pdf) saves a one-page PDF: the view at twice its screen size, with its dimensions, on an A4 page (Letter when the model is in feet and inches), landscape, with a line under it giving the file name, the date and the unit. Export › Print the view… puts the same page in front of your browser's print dialog. Choose a printer, or \"Save as PDF\". The page shows the view as it is: there is no scale on a perspective view. For a drawing to scale, switch Perspective off and look from Top or Front, and add dimensions for the sizes that matter. Presentation view leaves out the axes, guides and the selection; dimensions stay."},{"slug":"coming-from","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Coming from other modelers","summary":"How push/pull modeling habits map to ModelCraft, key by key, and the differences that catch people out.","section":"switching","order":10,"keywords":["switching","migrate","familiar","differences","push pull modeler","skp","shortcuts","habits","compare","measurements box","outliner","entity info"],"headings":[{"id":"habits-that-carry-over","text":"Habits that carry over","level":2},{"id":"differences-that-catch-people-out","text":"Differences that catch people out","level":2},{"id":"saving-is-a-download","text":"Saving is a download","level":3},{"id":"it-cannot-open-skp-files","text":"It cannot open .skp files","level":3},{"id":"no-right-click-menu","text":"No right-click menu","level":3},{"id":"units-live-in-the-status-bar","text":"Units live in the status bar","level":3},{"id":"no-paint-bucket-no-materials","text":"No paint bucket, no materials","level":3},{"id":"scale-is-uniform-without-grips","text":"Scale is uniform, without grips","level":3},{"id":"rotate-picks-its-plane-from-where-you-click","text":"Rotate picks its plane from where you click","level":3},{"id":"inference-from-points-you-pass-over","text":"Inference from points you pass over","level":3},{"id":"parallel-uses-the-edge-you-passed-over-before-starting","text":"Parallel uses the edge you passed over before starting","level":3},{"id":"copies-of-a-group-are-linked","text":"Copies of a group are linked","level":3},{"id":"space-does-not-pan","text":"Space does not pan","level":3},{"id":"two-finger-scroll-zooms","text":"Two-finger scroll zooms","level":3},{"id":"solids-pushed-into-each-other-do-not-cut","text":"Solids pushed into each other do not cut","level":3},{"id":"what-is-missing","text":"What is missing","level":3},{"id":"shortcuts-the-browser-keeps","text":"Shortcuts the browser keeps","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EIf you have modeled in SketchUp, ModelCraft will feel familiar: the same idea of faces and push/pull, the same tool letters, a value box you type into without clicking, inference with red, green and blue axes, groups, components, an Outliner, Entity Info and scenes. This page lists what maps across directly and, more importantly, what does not.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"habits-that-carry-over\"\u003EHabits that carry over\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EYou are used to\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EIn ModelCraft\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPress Space for Select, L Line, R Rectangle, C Circle\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"L\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"C\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPress P Push/Pull, M Move, Q Rotate, S Scale, E Eraser, T Tape Measure\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"P\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"M\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Q\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"E\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"T\"\u003ET\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ET\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPress O Orbit, H Pan, Z Zoom, Shift+Z Zoom Extents\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"O\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"H\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Z\"\u003EZ\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EZ\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Z\"\u003E⇧Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPress G to make a component\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"G\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift G\"\u003E⇧G\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+G\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E makes a group\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EType into the Measurements box without clicking it\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same, in the value box at the bottom. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E4m,3m\u003C/code\u003E for a rectangle, \u003Ccode\u003E12s\u003C/code\u003E for sides\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E3x\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E/3\u003C/code\u003E after a Move copy\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same; \u003Ccode\u003E*3\u003C/code\u003E also works\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E[x,y,z]\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;dx,dy,dz&gt;\u003C/code\u003E for a point or offset\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EType a bare number in an architectural-inch model and get inches\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMiddle-drag to orbit, Shift+middle-drag to pan, wheel to zoom at the cursor\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDouble-click with Push/Pull to repeat the distance\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArrow keys to lock red, green, blue, and parallel\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"→\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E red, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"←\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E green, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"↑\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E blue, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"↓\"\u003E↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E parallel\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHold Shift to lock an inference\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECtrl (Option on a Mac) to copy while moving\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option\"\u003E⌥\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, on any computer\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShift to toggle, Ctrl to add, Shift+Ctrl to remove from the selection\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same keys; on a Mac use \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E where it says Ctrl\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELeft-to-right window, right-to-left crossing\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA Dimension tool to label lengths\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"D\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, and more: type a new length into a dimension and the model resizes to it. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/dimensions\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/dimensions\"\u003EDimension and resize\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDouble-click a face for face plus edges; triple-click for all connected\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDouble-click a group to edit it; Esc to close\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDraw a line across a face to split it; erase an edge between coplanar faces to heal them\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPush a window rectangle through a wall to cut it\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBack faces in blue-grey; Reverse Faces\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same color; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReverse face\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EScenes as tabs over the view, Page Up and Page Down\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same, with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlay\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScenes\u003C/span\u003E panel\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShadows from date, time, latitude and longitude, with north on green\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same, in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStyle\u003C/span\u003E panel\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"differences-that-catch-people-out\"\u003EDifferences that catch people out\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"saving-is-a-download\"\u003ESaving is a download\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThere is no Save that overwrites the file you opened, no Save As dialog with a name, no autosave and no backup file. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) downloads a fresh \u003Ccode\u003E.modelcraft\u003C/code\u003E file every time, usually to your downloads folder. Save often, and rename or move the files yourself. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/files\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/files\"\u003EOpen, save, import and export\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"it-cannot-open-skp-files\"\u003EIt cannot open .skp files\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EModelCraft does not read or write SketchUp files. Export from SketchUp as Collada (\u003Ccode\u003E.dae\u003C/code\u003E), which keeps groups, components and colors, or as OBJ, and import that. To go back, export Collada from ModelCraft and import it in SketchUp.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"no-right-click-menu\"\u003ENo right-click menu\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ERight-drag pans; a right-click does nothing. Commands you would find in the context menu are in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReverse face\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExplode\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMake unique\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMake component\u003C/span\u003E) and in the toolbar&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOrganize\u003C/span\u003E group.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"units-live-in-the-status-bar\"\u003EUnits live in the status bar\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThere is no Model Info or template choice. The unit picker is at the bottom right. A new model starts in feet and inches in the US and in millimeters elsewhere, from your browser&#039;s language setting. Decimals always take a period: \u003Ccode\u003E2.5m\u003C/code\u003E, never \u003Ccode\u003E2,5m\u003C/code\u003E, because the comma always separates values.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"no-paint-bucket-no-materials\"\u003ENo paint bucket, no materials\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThere is no Paint Bucket (B) and no materials or textures in this build. Faces are one neutral color. Colors from imported Collada and glTF files are kept and exported again, but you cannot change them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"scale-is-uniform-without-grips\"\u003EScale is uniform, without grips\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EScale does not show a box with grips. Click the point that stays put, a reference point, then the new size or a typed factor. It always scales evenly in all directions. To stretch in one direction, push/pull or move a face, retype a group&#039;s width, depth or height in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E, or \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/dimensions\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/dimensions\"\u003Eresize with a dimension\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"rotate-picks-its-plane-from-where-you-click\"\u003ERotate picks its plane from where you click\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThere is no protractor that sticks to faces as you hover with arrow keys to pick the plane. Click the center on a face to turn in that face&#039;s plane, or on the ground to turn about the vertical. The arrow keys orbit the view before the first click.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"inference-from-points-you-pass-over\"\u003EInference from points you pass over\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EModelCraft snaps to axis directions from \u003Cstrong\u003Eyour start point\u003C/strong\u003E, and to points, edges and faces under the pointer. It does not track a direction from a point you merely hovered (the dotted &quot;from point&quot; inference). To line up with a far corner, lock the axis with an arrow key and hover that corner: the locked point slides to line up with it. Or place a \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/guides-measure\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/guides-measure\"\u003Eguide\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"parallel-uses-the-edge-you-passed-over-before-starting\"\u003EParallel uses the edge you passed over before starting\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe parallel and perpendicular inference uses the last edge the pointer crossed \u003Cstrong\u003Ebefore the first click\u003C/strong\u003E, not edges you hover while drawing. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/inference#parallel-and-perpendicular\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/inference\" data-hash=\"parallel-and-perpendicular\"\u003EInference and snapping\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"copies-of-a-group-are-linked\"\u003ECopies of a group are linked\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECopying a group with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove\u003C/span\u003E makes a second placement of the same group, so editing one changes both, as with components. SketchUp makes groups independent. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/troubleshooting#editing-one-group-changed-another\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/troubleshooting\" data-hash=\"editing-one-group-changed-another\"\u003ETroubleshooting\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"space-does-not-pan\"\u003ESpace does not pan\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E picks \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E, as in SketchUp. In some other web apps holding Space pans; here it doesn&#039;t. Right-drag pans instead.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"two-finger-scroll-zooms\"\u003ETwo-finger scroll zooms\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a trackpad, set \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModelCraft\u003C/span\u003E › Scrolling to Trackpad: two fingers orbit, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and two fingers pan, a pinch zooms. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/orbit-pan-zoom#on-a-trackpad\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/orbit-pan-zoom\" data-hash=\"on-a-trackpad\"\u003EOrbit, pan and zoom\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf your hands know Blender&#039;s keys rather than SketchUp&#039;s, set Controls to Blender style: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"G\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E act on the selection at once, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"E\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E extrudes (push/pull), \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift D\"\u003E⇧D\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E duplicates. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/settings\"\u003EModelCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"solids-pushed-into-each-other-do-not-cut\"\u003ESolids pushed into each other do not cut\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPush/pull cuts through the face behind it in the same object, but two separate solids pushed into each other don&#039;t intersect. There is no Intersect Faces and no Solid Tools.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"what-is-missing\"\u003EWhat is missing\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFollow Me, Offset, Arc, Freehand, Text, angle and radius dimensions, Protractor guides, Tags (layers), Hide, Section Planes, Soften/Smooth, Intersect, Solid Tools, Paint, a component library, extensions, and printing or a layout tool for sheets are not in this build. \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/a\u003E lists what to do instead.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"shortcuts-the-browser-keeps\"\u003EShortcuts the browser keeps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EModelCraft runs in a browser, so a few shortcuts belong to the browser, not the app. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command N\"\u003E⌘N\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+N\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E opens a browser window (use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew\u003C/span\u003E), and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command W\"\u003E⌘W\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+W\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E closes the tab. If you have unsaved changes, the browser asks before closing. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/keys#shortcuts-the-browser-keeps\" data-topic=\"basics/keys\" data-hash=\"shortcuts-the-browser-keeps\"\u003EKeys, mice and trackpads\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"If you have modeled in SketchUp, ModelCraft will feel familiar: the same idea of faces and push/pull, the same tool letters, a value box you type into without clicking, inference with red, green and blue axes, groups, components, an Outliner, Entity Info and scenes. This page lists what maps across directly and, more importantly, what does not. Habits that carry over You are used to In ModelCraft Press Space for Select, L Line, R Rectangle, C Circle The same: Space, L, R, C Press P Push/Pull, M Move, Q Rotate, S Scale, E Eraser, T Tape Measure The same: P, M, Q, S, E, T Press O Orbit, H Pan, Z Zoom, Shift+Z Zoom Extents The same: O, H, Z, Shift+Z Press G to make a component The same: G. Shift+G makes a group Type into the Measurements box without clicking it The same, in the value box at the bottom. Press Enter Type 4m,3m for a rectangle, 12s for sides The same Type 3x or /3 after a Move copy The same; *3 also works Type [x,y,z] or \u003Cdx,dy,dz\u003E for a point or offset The same Type a bare number in an architectural-inch model and get inches The same Middle-drag to orbit, Shift+middle-drag to pan, wheel to zoom at the cursor The same Double-click with Push/Pull to repeat the distance The same Arrow keys to lock red, green, blue, and parallel The same: → red, ← green, ↑ blue, ↓ parallel Hold Shift to lock an inference The same Ctrl (Option on a Mac) to copy while moving Ctrl, Ctrl or Alt, on any computer Shift to toggle, Ctrl to add, Shift+Ctrl to remove from the selection The same keys; on a Mac use Ctrl where it says Ctrl Left-to-right window, right-to-left crossing The same A Dimension tool to label lengths D, and more: type a new length into a dimension and the model resizes to it. See Dimension and resize Double-click a face for face plus edges; triple-click for all connected The same Double-click a group to edit it; Esc to close The same Draw a line across a face to split it; erase an edge between coplanar faces to heal them The same Push a window rectangle through a wall to cut it The same Back faces in blue-grey; Reverse Faces The same color; Reverse face in Entity info Scenes as tabs over the view, Page Up and Page Down The same, with Play in the Scenes panel Shadows from date, time, latitude and longitude, with north on green The same, in the Style panel Differences that catch people out Saving is a download There is no Save that overwrites the file you opened, no Save As dialog with a name, no autosave and no backup file. Save (Ctrl+S) downloads a fresh .modelcraft file every time, usually to your downloads folder. Save often, and rename or move the files yourself. See Open, save, import and export. It cannot open .skp files ModelCraft does not read or write SketchUp files. Export from SketchUp as Collada (.dae), which keeps groups, components and colors, or as OBJ, and import that. To go back, export Collada from ModelCraft and import it in SketchUp. No right-click menu Right-drag pans; a right-click does nothing. Commands you would find in the context menu are in Entity info (Reverse face, Edit, Explode, Make unique, Make component) and in the toolbar's Organize group. Units live in the status bar There is no Model Info or template choice. The unit picker is at the bottom right. A new model starts in feet and inches in the US and in millimeters elsewhere, from your browser's language setting. Decimals always take a period: 2.5m, never 2,5m, because the comma always separates values. No paint bucket, no materials There is no Paint Bucket (B) and no materials or textures in this build. Faces are one neutral color. Colors from imported Collada and glTF files are kept and exported again, but you cannot change them. Scale is uniform, without grips Scale does not show a box with grips. Click the point that stays put, a reference point, then the new size or a typed factor. It always scales evenly in all directions. To stretch in one direction, push/pull or move a face, retype a group's width, depth or height in Entity info, or resize with a dimension. Rotate picks its plane from where you click There is no protractor that sticks to faces as you hover with arrow keys to pick the plane. Click the center on a face to turn in that face's plane, or on the ground to turn about the vertical. The arrow keys orbit the view before the first click. Inference from points you pass over ModelCraft snaps to axis directions from your start point, and to points, edges and faces under the pointer. It does not track a direction from a point you merely hovered (the dotted \"from point\" inference). To line up with a far corner, lock the axis with an arrow key and hover that corner: the locked point slides to line up with it. Or place a guide. Parallel uses the edge you passed over before starting The parallel and perpendicular inference uses the last edge the pointer crossed before the first click, not edges you hover while drawing. See Inference and snapping. Copies of a group are linked Copying a group with Move makes a second placement of the same group, so editing one changes both, as with components. SketchUp makes groups independent. See Troubleshooting. Space does not pan Space picks Select, as in SketchUp. In some other web apps holding Space pans; here it doesn't. Right-drag pans instead. Two-finger scroll zooms On a trackpad, set Settings › ModelCraft › Scrolling to Trackpad: two fingers orbit, Shift and two fingers pan, a pinch zooms. See Orbit, pan and zoom. If your hands know Blender's keys rather than SketchUp's, set Controls to Blender style: G, R and S act on the selection at once, E extrudes (push/pull), Shift+D duplicates. See ModelCraft settings. Solids pushed into each other do not cut Push/pull cuts through the face behind it in the same object, but two separate solids pushed into each other don't intersect. There is no Intersect Faces and no Solid Tools. What is missing Follow Me, Offset, Arc, Freehand, Text, angle and radius dimensions, Protractor guides, Tags (layers), Hide, Section Planes, Soften/Smooth, Intersect, Solid Tools, Paint, a component library, extensions, and printing or a layout tool for sheets are not in this build. Not built yet lists what to do instead. Shortcuts the browser keeps ModelCraft runs in a browser, so a few shortcuts belong to the browser, not the app. Ctrl+N opens a browser window (use New), and Ctrl+W closes the tab. If you have unsaved changes, the browser asks before closing. See Keys, mice and trackpads."},{"slug":"settings","scope":"modelcraft","title":"ModelCraft settings","summary":"Pick the key style you know, make a trackpad orbit and pan without the camera buttons, and turn snapping and solid collisions on or off.","section":"reference","order":5,"keywords":["settings","preferences","controls","key style","keymap","shortcuts","blender","trackpad","touchpad","scroll","orbit","pinch","snap","snapping","collide","collision","overlap","intersect","pass through"],"headings":[{"id":"controls-which-keys-and-mouse-buttons","text":"Controls: which keys and mouse buttons","level":2},{"id":"scrolling-mouse-wheel-or-trackpad","text":"Scrolling: mouse wheel or trackpad","level":2},{"id":"snapping","text":"Snapping","level":2},{"id":"solids-stop-where-they-touch","text":"Solids stop where they touch","level":2},{"id":"corner-indicator","text":"Corner indicator","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E with the gear in the app bar or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The first tab, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModelCraft\u003C/span\u003E, holds the settings that belong to this app; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E holds the ones every CadCraft app shares (theme, toolbar, panels). ModelCraft&#039;s settings exist only in the app: you change them here, and this browser remembers them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"controls-which-keys-and-mouse-buttons\"\u003EControls: which keys and mouse buttons\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETwo key styles. Switch them here, with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKeys: SketchUp\u003C/span\u003E / \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKeys: Blender\u003C/span\u003E at the bottom right of the view, or at the end of the toolbar&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E menu:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESketchUp style\u003C/strong\u003E (the start): a letter picks a tool — \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"L\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E line, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E rectangle, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"P\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E push/pull, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"M\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E move, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Q\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E rotate, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E scale, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"G\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E make component. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/keyboard\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/keyboard\"\u003EKeys and mouse actions\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBlender style\u003C/strong\u003E: select first, then act on the selection straight away. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"G\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E moves it, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E rotates it and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E scales it from where the pointer is — no first click. While it moves, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"X\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Y\"\u003EY\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EY\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Z\"\u003EZ\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EZ\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E locks the red, green or blue axis, a typed number sets the distance (or degrees, or factor), and a click or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E puts it down; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or a right-click puts it back. Then you are back in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe full Blender-style list is in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/keyboard#blender-style-keys\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/keyboard\" data-hash=\"blender-style-keys\"\u003EKeys and mouse actions\u003C/a\u003E. The tooltips on the toolbar and the start card follow the style you pick.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"scrolling-mouse-wheel-or-trackpad\"\u003EScrolling: mouse wheel or trackpad\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWork it out\u003C/strong\u003E (the start): ModelCraft watches the scrolling. Sideways or fine-grained scrolling, as a trackpad sends, orbits; whole wheel notches zoom.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMouse wheel\u003C/strong\u003E: scrolling always zooms toward the pointer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETrackpad\u003C/strong\u003E: swipe with two fingers to orbit, hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and swipe to pan, pinch to zoom. You never need the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOrbit\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPan\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EZoom\u003C/span\u003E buttons.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA pinch zooms in every choice. If &quot;Work it out&quot; guesses wrong for your mouse or trackpad, pick the one you have.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn Blender style you can also orbit by holding \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option\"\u003E⌥\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and dragging, and pan with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option Shift\"\u003E⌥⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt+Shift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and a drag — handy on a laptop without a middle button.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"snapping\"\u003ESnapping\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESnap to points, edges and axes\u003C/strong\u003E (on at the start) pulls the pointer to endpoints, midpoints, edges, guides and the red, green and blue axes — see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/inference\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/inference\"\u003EInference\u003C/a\u003E. Turn it off when you want to place something freely near other geometry without it jumping. Faces, the ground and the axis locks still work with snapping off.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESnap\u003C/span\u003E button at the bottom right of the view does the same for this session&#039;s work (it is saved too). In Blender style, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Tab\"\u003E⇧ Tab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+Tab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E switches it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"solids-stop-where-they-touch\"\u003ESolids stop where they touch\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EStop solids where they touch\u003C/strong\u003E (on at the start) keeps a wall from being dragged through another wall. With \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove\u003C/span\u003E (and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"G\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, and paste-then-move with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command D\"\u003E⌘D\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) the selection slides up to the other solid and stops touching it; with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPush/pull\u003C/span\u003E a face stops where it meets another solid. Resting on something and sliding along it are fine — only passing into the inside is stopped. Things that already overlap when you start can still be pulled apart.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo push something through on purpose, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECollide\u003C/span\u003E at the bottom right (or clear the setting), make the move, and click it again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECollisions are checked for moves and push/pull. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERotate\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale\u003C/span\u003E do not check them yet, and loose geometry that touches still joins up, as it always has: keep separate things in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/groups-components\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/groups-components\"\u003Egroups\u003C/a\u003E so they stay separate.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"corner-indicator\"\u003ECorner indicator\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhat shows in the corner of the 3D view: \u003Cstrong\u003EView cube\u003C/strong\u003E (the start; top right — click a face, edge or corner to look from there, drag it to orbit, drag its compass ring to turn the model), \u003Cstrong\u003EAxis icon\u003C/strong\u003E (a small X, Y, Z marker at the bottom left that turns with the view: X red, Y green, Z blue) or \u003Cstrong\u003ENone\u003C/strong\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/view-cube\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/view-cube\"\u003EThe view cube\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Open Settings with the gear in the app bar or Ctrl+,. The first tab, ModelCraft, holds the settings that belong to this app; General holds the ones every CadCraft app shares (theme, toolbar, panels). ModelCraft's settings exist only in the app: you change them here, and this browser remembers them. Controls: which keys and mouse buttons Two key styles. Switch them here, with Keys: SketchUp / Keys: Blender at the bottom right of the view, or at the end of the toolbar's More menu: SketchUp style (the start): a letter picks a tool — L line, R rectangle, P push/pull, M move, Q rotate, S scale, G make component. See Keys and mouse actions. Blender style: select first, then act on the selection straight away. G moves it, R rotates it and S scales it from where the pointer is — no first click. While it moves, X, Y or Z locks the red, green or blue axis, a typed number sets the distance (or degrees, or factor), and a click or Enter puts it down; Esc or a right-click puts it back. Then you are back in Select. The full Blender-style list is in Keys and mouse actions. The tooltips on the toolbar and the start card follow the style you pick. Scrolling: mouse wheel or trackpad Work it out (the start): ModelCraft watches the scrolling. Sideways or fine-grained scrolling, as a trackpad sends, orbits; whole wheel notches zoom. Mouse wheel: scrolling always zooms toward the pointer. Trackpad: swipe with two fingers to orbit, hold Shift and swipe to pan, pinch to zoom. You never need the Orbit, Pan or Zoom buttons. A pinch zooms in every choice. If \"Work it out\" guesses wrong for your mouse or trackpad, pick the one you have. In Blender style you can also orbit by holding Alt and dragging, and pan with Alt+Shift and a drag — handy on a laptop without a middle button. Snapping Snap to points, edges and axes (on at the start) pulls the pointer to endpoints, midpoints, edges, guides and the red, green and blue axes — see Inference. Turn it off when you want to place something freely near other geometry without it jumping. Faces, the ground and the axis locks still work with snapping off. The Snap button at the bottom right of the view does the same for this session's work (it is saved too). In Blender style, Shift+Tab switches it. Solids stop where they touch Stop solids where they touch (on at the start) keeps a wall from being dragged through another wall. With Move (and G, and paste-then-move with Ctrl+D) the selection slides up to the other solid and stops touching it; with Push/pull a face stops where it meets another solid. Resting on something and sliding along it are fine — only passing into the inside is stopped. Things that already overlap when you start can still be pulled apart. To push something through on purpose, click Collide at the bottom right (or clear the setting), make the move, and click it again. Collisions are checked for moves and push/pull. Rotate and Scale do not check them yet, and loose geometry that touches still joins up, as it always has: keep separate things in groups so they stay separate. Corner indicator What shows in the corner of the 3D view: View cube (the start; top right — click a face, edge or corner to look from there, drag it to orbit, drag its compass ring to turn the model), Axis icon (a small X, Y, Z marker at the bottom left that turns with the view: X red, Y green, Z blue) or None. See The view cube."},{"slug":"keyboard","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Keys and mouse actions","summary":"Every keyboard shortcut, mouse action, trackpad gesture and touch gesture in ModelCraft, grouped by what they do.","section":"reference","order":10,"keywords":["keyboard","shortcuts","keys","hotkeys","mouse","trackpad","touch","gestures","cheat sheet","ctrl","command","arrow keys"],"headings":[{"id":"tools","text":"Tools","level":2},{"id":"model-and-file","text":"Model and file","level":2},{"id":"esc","text":"Esc","level":2},{"id":"while-a-tool-is-working","text":"While a tool is working","level":2},{"id":"arrow-keys-at-other-times","text":"Arrow keys at other times","level":2},{"id":"mouse","text":"Mouse","level":2},{"id":"trackpad","text":"Trackpad","level":2},{"id":"blender-style-keys","text":"Blender-style keys","level":2},{"id":"touch-screen","text":"Touch screen","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThese are the SketchUp-style keys, the ones ModelCraft starts with. For the other style, see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/keyboard#blender-style-keys\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/keyboard\" data-hash=\"blender-style-keys\"\u003EBlender-style keys\u003C/a\u003E below and \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/settings\"\u003EModelCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EShortcuts work when the 3D view or a toolbar button has focus. If you just typed in a panel field or changed a list in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStyle\u003C/span\u003E, click in the 3D view first, or your keys go to that field. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/keys\" data-topic=\"basics/keys\"\u003EKeys, mice and trackpads\u003C/a\u003E for how keys are written on each computer.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"tools\"\u003ETools\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETool\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"L\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELine\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERectangle\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"C\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECircle\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"N\"\u003EN\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EN\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPolygon\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"A\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffset\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"B\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaint\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"P\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPush/pull\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"M\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Q\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERotate\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"E\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEraser\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"T\"\u003ET\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ET\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETape\u003C/span\u003E measure\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"D\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDimension\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"O\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOrbit\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"H\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPan\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Z\"\u003EZ\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EZ\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EZoom\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETool letters work without modifier keys only: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E with a letter does nothing, except the two below.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"model-and-file\"\u003EModel and file\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDoes\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"G\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMake component from the selection\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift G\"\u003E⇧G\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+G\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMake group from the selection\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Z\"\u003E⇧Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom extents (the whole model)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUndo\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Y\"\u003E⌘Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command Z\"\u003E⇧⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERedo\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESave (downloads the \u003Ccode\u003E.modelcraft\u003C/code\u003E file)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command O\"\u003E⌘O\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+O\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen a model\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command A\"\u003E⌘A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelect everything (in the open group, if one is open)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECopy the selection\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command X\"\u003E⌘X\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+X\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECut the selection\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPaste: the copy follows the pointer, click where it goes\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command V\"\u003E⇧⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPaste in place (where it was copied from)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command D\"\u003E⌘D\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDuplicate: a copy moves with the pointer, click to put it down\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EErase the selection\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ↓\"\u003Efn ↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EPage Down\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E / \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ↑\"\u003Efn ↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EPage Up\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENext / previous scene\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen or close this help (also \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHelp\u003C/span\u003E in the app bar; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E inside the help closes it)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EScreen mode: full screen, then bars hidden, then normal (Control on a Mac too)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E backs out one level each time you press it:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECancels what the tool is in the middle of (a line being drawn, a push/pull, a move). Everything goes back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESwitches to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClears the selection.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECloses the group you are editing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt also stops scenes from playing. In the value box, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E clears what you typed and returns to the view.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"while-a-tool-is-working\"\u003EWhile a tool is working\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDoes\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA digit, or one of \u003Ccode\u003E. , - &#039; &quot; [ &lt; * /\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStarts typing in the value box\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EApplies what you typed in the value box\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"→\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELocks to the red axis (press again to unlock)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"←\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELocks to the green axis\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"↑\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELocks to the blue axis\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"↓\"\u003E↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELocks parallel to the last edge you hovered before the first click\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHolds the direction you are snapped to, while held\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option\"\u003E⌥\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (Move)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESwitches between moving and copying\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EArrow-key locks work in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELine\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERectangle\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECircle\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPolygon\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETape\u003C/span\u003E (when measuring from a point), after the first click. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPush/pull\u003C/span\u003E always moves along the face&#039;s direction and has no locks. In \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERotate\u003C/span\u003E the arrow keys show a &quot;Locked&quot; message after the first click but do not change the turn.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"arrow-keys-at-other-times\"\u003EArrow keys at other times\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen no tool is in the middle of something, the arrow keys move the camera:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDoes\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"←\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"→\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"↑\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"↓\"\u003E↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOrbit\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E with an arrow\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mouse\"\u003EMouse\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EAction\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDoes\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUses the current tool\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPress, drag, release\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESame as two clicks (for drawing tools); a box selection (for \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E); erases along the way (for \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEraser\u003C/span\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDouble-click\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E: face with its edges, edge with its faces, opens a group; empty space closes the group. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPush/pull\u003C/span\u003E: repeats the last distance\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETriple-click\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E: everything connected\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E + click\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E: toggle in and out of the selection\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E + click\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E: add to the selection\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E + \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E + click\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E: remove from the selection\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMiddle-button drag\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOrbit, with any tool\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E + middle-button drag\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan, with any tool\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERight-button drag\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan, with any tool\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWheel\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom toward the pointer\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThere is no right-click menu.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"trackpad\"\u003ETrackpad\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhat a two-finger swipe does depends on \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModelCraft\u003C/span\u003E › Scrolling:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EGesture\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETrackpad (or worked out)\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMouse wheel\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwo-finger swipe\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOrbit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom toward the pointer\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E + two-finger swipe\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPinch\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom toward the pointer\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom toward the pointer\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwo-finger press and drag (secondary click held)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/orbit-pan-zoom#on-a-trackpad\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/orbit-pan-zoom\" data-hash=\"on-a-trackpad\"\u003EOrbit, pan and zoom\u003C/a\u003E and \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/settings#scrolling-mouse-wheel-or-trackpad\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/settings\" data-hash=\"scrolling-mouse-wheel-or-trackpad\"\u003EModelCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"blender-style-keys\"\u003EBlender-style keys\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModelCraft\u003C/span\u003E › Controls set to Blender style:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDoes\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"W\"\u003EW\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EW\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"L\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E / \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"B\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E / \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"C\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E / \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"N\"\u003EN\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EN\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELine\u003C/span\u003E / \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERectangle\u003C/span\u003E / \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECircle\u003C/span\u003E / \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPolygon\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"E\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPush/pull\u003C/span\u003E (extrude)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"T\"\u003ET\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ET\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETape\u003C/span\u003E measure\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"D\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDimension\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"G\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMove the selection at once, from the pointer\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERotate the selection about its middle (blue axis)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EScale the selection about its middle\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"X\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E / \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Y\"\u003EY\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EY\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E / \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Z\"\u003EZ\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EZ\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while moving or rotating\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELock the red / green / blue axis (again to unlock)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPut it down\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or right-click\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPut it back\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift D\"\u003E⇧D\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDuplicate and move the copy\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"X\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDelete the selection\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"A\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E / \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option A\"\u003E⌥A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelect all / select nothing\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen the selected group or component; again to close it\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command G\"\u003E⌘G\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+G\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E / \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command G\"\u003E⇧⌘G\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+G\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMake group / make component\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENumpad \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"1\"\u003E1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E / \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"3\"\u003E3\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E3\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E / \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"7\"\u003E7\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E7\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFront / right / top view (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for back / left / bottom)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENumpad \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"5\"\u003E5\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E5\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPerspective on or off\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENumpad \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\".\"\u003E.\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E.\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom to the selection\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ←\"\u003Efn ←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EHome\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift C\"\u003E⇧C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom extents\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Tab\"\u003E⇧ Tab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+Tab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESnapping on or off\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMiddle-button drag / with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E / with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOrbit / pan / zoom\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option\"\u003E⌥\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E + drag / \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option Shift\"\u003E⌥⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt+Shift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E + drag\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOrbit / pan (no middle button needed)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECopy, paste, undo, save and open keep the keys listed above. Typed values, the value box and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPush/pull\u003C/span\u003E work the same in both styles.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"touch-screen\"\u003ETouch screen\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EGesture\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDoes\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETap\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA click with the current tool\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDouble-tap\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA double-click\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPress, drag, lift\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESame as press, drag, release with a mouse\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwo fingers: pinch\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwo fingers: move together\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA second finger during a one-finger action\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECancels that action\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOrbit\u003C/span\u003E tool, then drag one finger\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOrbit\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo type a value on a touch screen, tap the value box at the bottom, type, and press the keyboard&#039;s Enter (or Go) key.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"These are the SketchUp-style keys, the ones ModelCraft starts with. For the other style, see Blender-style keys below and ModelCraft settings. Shortcuts work when the 3D view or a toolbar button has focus. If you just typed in a panel field or changed a list in Style, click in the 3D view first, or your keys go to that field. See Keys, mice and trackpads for how keys are written on each computer. Tools Key Tool Space Select L Line R Rectangle C Circle N Polygon A Arc F Offset B Paint P Push/pull M Move Q Rotate S Scale E Eraser T Tape measure D Dimension O Orbit H Pan Z Zoom Tool letters work without modifier keys only: Shift with a letter does nothing, except the two below. Model and file Key Does G Make component from the selection Shift+G Make group from the selection Shift+Z Zoom extents (the whole model) Ctrl+Z Undo Ctrl+Y or Ctrl+Shift+Z Redo Ctrl+S Save (downloads the .modelcraft file) Ctrl+O Open a model Ctrl+A Select everything (in the open group, if one is open) Ctrl+C Copy the selection Ctrl+X Cut the selection Ctrl+V Paste: the copy follows the pointer, click where it goes Ctrl+Shift+V Paste in place (where it was copied from) Ctrl+D Duplicate: a copy moves with the pointer, click to put it down Delete or Backspace Erase the selection Page Down / Page Up Next / previous scene F1 Open or close this help (also Help in the app bar; Esc inside the help closes it) Ctrl+Shift+F Screen mode: full screen, then bars hidden, then normal (Control on a Mac too) Esc Esc backs out one level each time you press it: Cancels what the tool is in the middle of (a line being drawn, a push/pull, a move). Everything goes back. Switches to Select. Clears the selection. Closes the group you are editing. It also stops scenes from playing. In the value box, Esc clears what you typed and returns to the view. While a tool is working Key Does A digit, or one of . , - ' \" [ \u003C * / Starts typing in the value box Enter Applies what you typed in the value box → Locks to the red axis (press again to unlock) ← Locks to the green axis ↑ Locks to the blue axis ↓ Locks parallel to the last edge you hovered before the first click Hold Shift Holds the direction you are snapped to, while held Ctrl, Ctrl or Alt (Move) Switches between moving and copying Arrow-key locks work in Line, Rectangle, Circle, Polygon, Move, Scale and Tape (when measuring from a point), after the first click. Push/pull always moves along the face's direction and has no locks. In Rotate the arrow keys show a \"Locked\" message after the first click but do not change the turn. Arrow keys at other times When no tool is in the middle of something, the arrow keys move the camera: Key Does ← → ↑ ↓ Orbit Shift with an arrow Pan Mouse Action Does Click Uses the current tool Press, drag, release Same as two clicks (for drawing tools); a box selection (for Select); erases along the way (for Eraser) Double-click Select: face with its edges, edge with its faces, opens a group; empty space closes the group. Push/pull: repeats the last distance Triple-click Select: everything connected Shift + click Select: toggle in and out of the selection Ctrl + click Select: add to the selection Shift + Ctrl + click Select: remove from the selection Middle-button drag Orbit, with any tool Shift + middle-button drag Pan, with any tool Right-button drag Pan, with any tool Wheel Zoom toward the pointer There is no right-click menu. Trackpad What a two-finger swipe does depends on Settings › ModelCraft › Scrolling: Gesture Trackpad (or worked out) Mouse wheel Two-finger swipe Orbit Zoom toward the pointer Shift + two-finger swipe Pan Zoom Pinch Zoom toward the pointer Zoom toward the pointer Two-finger press and drag (secondary click held) Pan Pan See Orbit, pan and zoom and ModelCraft settings. Blender-style keys With Settings › ModelCraft › Controls set to Blender style: Key Does W or Space Select L / B / C / N Line / Rectangle / Circle / Polygon E Push/pull (extrude) T Tape measure D Dimension G Move the selection at once, from the pointer R Rotate the selection about its middle (blue axis) S Scale the selection about its middle X / Y / Z while moving or rotating Lock the red / green / blue axis (again to unlock) Click or Enter Put it down Esc or right-click Put it back Shift+D Duplicate and move the copy X or Delete Delete the selection A / Alt+A Select all / select nothing Tab Open the selected group or component; again to close it Ctrl+G / Ctrl+Shift+G Make group / make component Numpad 1 / 3 / 7 Front / right / top view (Ctrl for back / left / bottom) Numpad 5 Perspective on or off Numpad . Zoom to the selection Home or Shift+C Zoom extents Shift+Tab Snapping on or off Middle-button drag / with Shift / with Ctrl Orbit / pan / zoom Alt + drag / Alt+Shift + drag Orbit / pan (no middle button needed) Copy, paste, undo, save and open keep the keys listed above. Typed values, the value box and Push/pull work the same in both styles. Touch screen Gesture Does Tap A click with the current tool Double-tap A double-click Press, drag, lift Same as press, drag, release with a mouse Two fingers: pinch Zoom Two fingers: move together Pan A second finger during a one-finger action Cancels that action Orbit tool, then drag one finger Orbit To type a value on a touch screen, tap the value box at the bottom, type, and press the keyboard's Enter (or Go) key."},{"slug":"tools","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Tool list","summary":"Every ModelCraft tool with its key, what each click does, and what you can type into the value box while it works.","section":"reference","order":20,"keywords":["tools","toolbar","commands","list","line","rectangle","circle","polygon","offset","arc","curve","spline","follow me","sweep","2 point arc","arc from center","select","push pull","move","rotate","scale","eraser","tape","dimension","orbit","pan","zoom","place component"],"headings":[{"id":"drawing-tools","text":"Drawing tools","level":2},{"id":"editing-tools","text":"Editing tools","level":2},{"id":"camera-tools","text":"Camera tools","level":2},{"id":"organize","text":"Organize","level":2},{"id":"place-component","text":"Place component","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe status bar&#039;s tip always says what the current tool wants next. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E cancels what a tool is in the middle of.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"drawing-tools\"\u003EDrawing tools\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETool\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EClicks\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EType in the value box\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELine\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"L\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStart, then each next point. Stops when a face closes, or on \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA length toward the pointer; \u003Ccode\u003E[x,y,z]\u003C/code\u003E a point; \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;dx,dy,dz&gt;\u003C/code\u003E an offset (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELength\u003C/span\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERectangle\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFirst corner (ground or a face), opposite corner\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWidth and depth: \u003Ccode\u003E4m,3m\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDimensions\u003C/span\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECircle\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"C\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECenter (ground or a face), a point on the edge\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBefore the first click: sides, \u003Ccode\u003E24s\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E24\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESides\u003C/span\u003E). After: the radius (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERadius\u003C/span\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPolygon\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"N\"\u003EN\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EN\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECenter, a corner\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESame as Circle; starts with 6 sides\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"A\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStart, end, then the bulge\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe chord&#039;s length after the start (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELength\u003C/span\u003E); the bulge after the end (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBulge\u003C/span\u003E); \u003Ccode\u003E12s\u003C/code\u003E for segments\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProtractor\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E—\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECenter, 0° point, then the angle\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe angle in degrees (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAngle\u003C/span\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFollow me\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E—\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelect a path, then click the face (or click the face, then an edge of the path)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E—\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaint\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"B\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA face, group or component\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E— (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E connected, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E all of that color, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option\"\u003E⌥\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E pick up a color)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffset\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA face (or select a face or edges first), then the side and distance\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe distance (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDistance\u003C/span\u003E); into the face is inward\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECurve\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E—\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPoints the curve passes through; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or a double-click finishes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA length along the pointer, \u003Ccode\u003E[x,y,z]\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;dx,dy,dz&gt;\u003C/code\u003E for the next point\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArc center\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E—\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECenter, start, then the end\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe radius after the center; the angle in degrees after the start; \u003Ccode\u003E12s\u003C/code\u003E for segments\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"editing-tools\"\u003EEditing tools\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETool\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EClicks\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EType in the value box\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick, double-click, triple-click, drag a box. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/select\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/select\"\u003ESelect things\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWith one dimension or edge selected: a new length, which resizes the model\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPush/pull\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"P\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe face, then the distance. Double-click a face to repeat the last distance\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA distance; negative goes the other way (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDistance\u003C/span\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"M\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFrom a point, to a point. Hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option\"\u003E⌥\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to copy\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA distance toward the pointer, \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;dx,dy,dz&gt;\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E[x,y,z]\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDistance\u003C/span\u003E). After a copy: \u003Ccode\u003E3x\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E/3\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERotate\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Q\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECenter (on a face to turn in its plane), start direction, end direction\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe angle in degrees (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAngle\u003C/span\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe point that stays, a reference point, the new size\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA factor: \u003Ccode\u003E2\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E0.5\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale\u003C/span\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEraser\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"E\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick or drag over edges, groups, guides and dimensions\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Enothing\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETape\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"T\"\u003ET\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ET\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFrom an edge: a parallel guide line. From a point: measures and leaves a guide point\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe offset (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffset\u003C/span\u003E) or the distance (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELength\u003C/span\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDimension\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"D\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwo corners (or one edge), then where the dimension line goes. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/dimensions\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/dimensions\"\u003EDimension and resize\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe offset (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffset\u003C/span\u003E). Right after: a new length, which resizes the model\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERotate\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale\u003C/span\u003E work on the selection. If nothing is selected, they take what you click first (a face brings its edges).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"camera-tools\"\u003ECamera tools\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETool\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDrag to\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOrbit\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"O\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETurn around the center of the view\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPan\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"H\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESlide the view\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EZoom\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Z\"\u003EZ\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EZ\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom: up is in, down is out\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExtents\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Z\"\u003E⇧Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E(a button, not a tool) Fit the whole model\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou rarely need these with a mouse: middle-drag orbits, right-drag pans and the wheel zooms with any tool. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/orbit-pan-zoom\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/orbit-pan-zoom\"\u003EOrbit, pan and zoom\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"organize\"\u003EOrganize\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EButton\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDoes\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EComponent\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"G\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMakes the selection a component\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGroup\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift G\"\u003E⇧G\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+G\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMakes the selection a group\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExplode\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Enone\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETurns the selected groups and components back into loose geometry\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"place-component\"\u003EPlace component\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENot on the toolbar: click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlace\u003C/span\u003E next to a component in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EComponents\u003C/span\u003E panel. Click once where the component&#039;s origin goes; the tool then returns to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The status bar's tip always says what the current tool wants next. Esc cancels what a tool is in the middle of. Drawing tools Tool Key Clicks Type in the value box Line L Start, then each next point. Stops when a face closes, or on Esc A length toward the pointer; [x,y,z] a point; \u003Cdx,dy,dz\u003E an offset (Length) Rectangle R First corner (ground or a face), opposite corner Width and depth: 4m,3m (Dimensions) Circle C Center (ground or a face), a point on the edge Before the first click: sides, 24s or 24 (Sides). After: the radius (Radius) Polygon N Center, a corner Same as Circle; starts with 6 sides Arc A Start, end, then the bulge The chord's length after the start (Length); the bulge after the end (Bulge); 12s for segments Protractor — Center, 0° point, then the angle The angle in degrees (Angle) Follow me — Select a path, then click the face (or click the face, then an edge of the path) — Paint B A face, group or component — (Ctrl connected, Shift all of that color, Alt pick up a color) Offset F A face (or select a face or edges first), then the side and distance The distance (Distance); into the face is inward Curve — Points the curve passes through; Enter or a double-click finishes A length along the pointer, [x,y,z] or \u003Cdx,dy,dz\u003E for the next point Arc center — Center, start, then the end The radius after the center; the angle in degrees after the start; 12s for segments Editing tools Tool Key Clicks Type in the value box Select Space Click, double-click, triple-click, drag a box. See Select things With one dimension or edge selected: a new length, which resizes the model Push/pull P The face, then the distance. Double-click a face to repeat the last distance A distance; negative goes the other way (Distance) Move M From a point, to a point. Hold Ctrl or Alt to copy A distance toward the pointer, \u003Cdx,dy,dz\u003E or [x,y,z] (Distance). After a copy: 3x or /3 Rotate Q Center (on a face to turn in its plane), start direction, end direction The angle in degrees (Angle) Scale S The point that stays, a reference point, the new size A factor: 2, 0.5 (Scale) Eraser E Click or drag over edges, groups, guides and dimensions nothing Tape T From an edge: a parallel guide line. From a point: measures and leaves a guide point The offset (Offset) or the distance (Length) Dimension D Two corners (or one edge), then where the dimension line goes. See Dimension and resize The offset (Offset). Right after: a new length, which resizes the model Move, Rotate and Scale work on the selection. If nothing is selected, they take what you click first (a face brings its edges). Camera tools Tool Key Drag to Orbit O Turn around the center of the view Pan H Slide the view Zoom Z Zoom: up is in, down is out Extents Shift+Z (a button, not a tool) Fit the whole model You rarely need these with a mouse: middle-drag orbits, right-drag pans and the wheel zooms with any tool. See Orbit, pan and zoom. Organize Button Key Does Component G Makes the selection a component Group Shift+G Makes the selection a group Explode none Turns the selected groups and components back into loose geometry Place component Not on the toolbar: click Place next to a component in the Components panel. Click once where the component's origin goes; the tool then returns to Select."},{"slug":"inference","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Inference and snapping","summary":"What the colored markers mean, which snap wins, and how to lock a direction with the arrow keys or Shift.","section":"reference","order":30,"keywords":["inference","snap","snapping","endpoint","midpoint","center of face","centre","on edge","on face","axis","red axis","green axis","blue axis","parallel","perpendicular","lock","shift","osnap"],"headings":[{"id":"markers-and-tips","text":"Markers and tips","level":2},{"id":"which-snap-wins","text":"Which snap wins","level":2},{"id":"drawing-on-a-face-from-a-point","text":"Drawing on a face from a point","level":2},{"id":"parallel-and-perpendicular","text":"Parallel and perpendicular","level":2},{"id":"lock-a-direction","text":"Lock a direction","level":2},{"id":"inside-a-group","text":"Inside a group","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EWhile a drawing or editing tool is working, ModelCraft looks at what is under the pointer and snaps to the most useful thing nearby. A marker shows what it found and the tip at the bottom left names it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"markers-and-tips\"\u003EMarkers and tips\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETip\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMarker\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESnaps to\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEndpoint\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGreen circle\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe end of an edge\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMidpoint\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECyan circle\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe middle of an edge\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECenter of face\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBlue circle\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe middle of the face under the pointer\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGuide point\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGrey\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA guide point left by the tape measure\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOrigin\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOchre\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWhere the red, green and blue axes meet\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOn red axis / On green axis / On blue axis\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA line in that color\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA line from your start point along that direction\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EParallel to edge / Perpendicular to edge\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMagenta line\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA line from your start point parallel or perpendicular to the remembered edge (see below)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELocked\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMagenta line\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA direction you locked with an arrow key or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOn edge\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERed square\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAnywhere along an edge\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOn guide\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGrey\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAnywhere along a guide line\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOn face\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBlue diamond\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAnywhere on a face\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOn plane\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Enone\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe plane you started on, when the pointer is over empty space\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOn the ground\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Enone\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe ground (the red-green plane), when nothing else is there\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen an axis line from your start point crosses an edge or guide, ModelCraft snaps to the crossing and the tip names both, such as &quot;On red axis, on edge&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"which-snap-wins\"\u003EWhich snap wins\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPoints\u003C/strong\u003E within about 12 pixels: endpoints first, then guide points, midpoints, the center of the face under the pointer and the origin.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDirections\u003C/strong\u003E from the start point: the red, green and blue axes, and parallel or perpendicular to the remembered edge.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOn an edge\u003C/strong\u003E or a guide line.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOn a face.\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe drawing plane\u003C/strong\u003E, or the ground.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThings hidden behind a face never snap. Orbit so you can see the point you want.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"drawing-on-a-face-from-a-point\"\u003EDrawing on a face from a point\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERectangle\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECircle\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPolygon\u003C/span\u003E started on a corner, a midpoint, an edge or the center of a face is drawn \u003Cstrong\u003Ein the plane of the face under the pointer\u003C/strong\u003E, and cuts that face. An edge belongs to two faces, so the side of the edge the pointer is on decides: to draw on the end of a long box, start from the middle of one of its edges with the pointer just inside the end face.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"parallel-and-perpendicular\"\u003EParallel and perpendicular\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EModelCraft remembers the \u003Cstrong\u003Elast edge the pointer passed over before your first click\u003C/strong\u003E. Starting on a corner or on an edge counts as passing over that edge. Once the tool is working, other edges you hover do not replace it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo draw a line parallel to an existing edge:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"L\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and hover the edge you want to match.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove to the start point, without crossing other edges on the way if you can, and click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove in the direction parallel to that edge. The line turns magenta and the tip says &quot;Parallel to edge&quot;. Perpendicular works the same way, within the plane you are drawing on.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf the wrong edge was remembered, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, hover the right edge, and start again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"lock-a-direction\"\u003ELock a direction\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAfter the first click of a tool, you can keep the next point on one line:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ELocks to\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"→\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe red axis\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"←\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe green axis\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"↑\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe blue axis (straight up)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"↓\"\u003E↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EParallel to the remembered edge (see above)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPress the same arrow again to unlock. The status bar says &quot;Locked to the red axis. Press the same arrow to unlock.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhile locked, the point slides along the line toward wherever the pointer is, and still lines up with points you hover: hover the corner of another box to draw a line along red exactly as far as that corner.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/strong\u003E locks whatever direction you are snapped to right now (an axis, or parallel), for as long as you hold it. Let go to unlock.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELocks work in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELine\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERectangle\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECircle\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPolygon\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETape\u003C/span\u003E. Before the first click, the arrow keys orbit the view instead.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"inside-a-group\"\u003EInside a group\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhile you edit inside a group or component, the axes are the group&#039;s own axes (they move with it, and turn with it if it was rotated). Snaps still find points and edges outside the group, so you can line things up with the rest of the model.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"While a drawing or editing tool is working, ModelCraft looks at what is under the pointer and snaps to the most useful thing nearby. A marker shows what it found and the tip at the bottom left names it. Markers and tips Tip Marker Snaps to Endpoint Green circle The end of an edge Midpoint Cyan circle The middle of an edge Center of face Blue circle The middle of the face under the pointer Guide point Grey A guide point left by the tape measure Origin Ochre Where the red, green and blue axes meet On red axis / On green axis / On blue axis A line in that color A line from your start point along that direction Parallel to edge / Perpendicular to edge Magenta line A line from your start point parallel or perpendicular to the remembered edge (see below) Locked Magenta line A direction you locked with an arrow key or Shift On edge Red square Anywhere along an edge On guide Grey Anywhere along a guide line On face Blue diamond Anywhere on a face On plane none The plane you started on, when the pointer is over empty space On the ground none The ground (the red-green plane), when nothing else is there When an axis line from your start point crosses an edge or guide, ModelCraft snaps to the crossing and the tip names both, such as \"On red axis, on edge\". Which snap wins Points within about 12 pixels: endpoints first, then guide points, midpoints, the center of the face under the pointer and the origin. Directions from the start point: the red, green and blue axes, and parallel or perpendicular to the remembered edge. On an edge or a guide line. On a face. The drawing plane, or the ground. Things hidden behind a face never snap. Orbit so you can see the point you want. Drawing on a face from a point A Rectangle, Circle or Polygon started on a corner, a midpoint, an edge or the center of a face is drawn in the plane of the face under the pointer, and cuts that face. An edge belongs to two faces, so the side of the edge the pointer is on decides: to draw on the end of a long box, start from the middle of one of its edges with the pointer just inside the end face. Parallel and perpendicular ModelCraft remembers the last edge the pointer passed over before your first click. Starting on a corner or on an edge counts as passing over that edge. Once the tool is working, other edges you hover do not replace it. To draw a line parallel to an existing edge: Press L and hover the edge you want to match. Move to the start point, without crossing other edges on the way if you can, and click. Move in the direction parallel to that edge. The line turns magenta and the tip says \"Parallel to edge\". Perpendicular works the same way, within the plane you are drawing on. If the wrong edge was remembered, press Esc, hover the right edge, and start again. Lock a direction After the first click of a tool, you can keep the next point on one line: Key Locks to → The red axis ← The green axis ↑ The blue axis (straight up) ↓ Parallel to the remembered edge (see above) Press the same arrow again to unlock. The status bar says \"Locked to the red axis. Press the same arrow to unlock.\" While locked, the point slides along the line toward wherever the pointer is, and still lines up with points you hover: hover the corner of another box to draw a line along red exactly as far as that corner. Shift locks whatever direction you are snapped to right now (an axis, or parallel), for as long as you hold it. Let go to unlock. Locks work in Line, Rectangle, Circle, Polygon, Move, Scale and Tape. Before the first click, the arrow keys orbit the view instead. Inside a group While you edit inside a group or component, the axes are the group's own axes (they move with it, and turn with it if it was rotated). Snaps still find points and edges outside the group, so you can line things up with the rest of the model."},{"slug":"value-box","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Type exact sizes in the value box","summary":"Every way to type lengths, sizes, points, offsets, angles, sides and copies, and how units and bare numbers are read.","section":"reference","order":40,"keywords":["value box","measurements box","vcb","type","exact","dimension","length","units","feet","inches","metric","millimeters","coordinates","offset","array","sides","angle","decimal comma"],"headings":[{"id":"how-to-type","text":"How to type","level":2},{"id":"lengths","text":"Lengths","level":2},{"id":"per-tool","text":"Per tool","level":2},{"id":"points-and-offsets","text":"Points and offsets","level":2},{"id":"copies-after-a-move","text":"Copies after a move","level":2},{"id":"typed-lengths-need-a-direction","text":"Typed lengths need a direction","level":2},{"id":"the-model-s-unit","text":"The model's unit","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe value box sits in the status bar at the bottom, with a label that changes with the tool. While you work it shows the live size; at any moment you can type an exact one.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-to-type\"\u003EHow to type\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart the action with a click (the first corner, the face, the point to move from).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType. You don&#039;t need to click the value box: a digit or one of \u003Ccode\u003E. , - &#039; &quot; [ &lt; * /\u003C/code\u003E sends your typing there.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E in the value box to clear it and go back to the view. On a touch screen, tap the value box to bring up the keyboard.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf ModelCraft cannot read what you typed, the status bar says &quot;Type a length such as 3m, 10&#039;6&quot; or 4m,3m.&quot; If the tool cannot use it, it says &quot;That did not work: …&quot; with the reason.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"lengths\"\u003ELengths\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EType\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeans\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E3m\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E320cm\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E3200mm\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMeters, centimeters, millimeters\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E10&#039;\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E10ft\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E10 feet\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFeet\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E6&quot;\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E6in\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E6 inches\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EInches\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E10&#039;6&quot;\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E10&#039; 6&quot;\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E10&#039;-6 1/2&quot;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFeet and inches, with a fraction\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E6 1/2&quot;\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E6-1/2&quot;\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E3/4&quot;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EInches with a fraction\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E1.5ft\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E.5m\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDecimals with a unit\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E120\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA bare number, in the model&#039;s unit\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA bare number uses the model&#039;s unit\u003C/strong\u003E, as shown in the picker at the bottom right: inches in a feet-and-inches model (so \u003Ccode\u003E120\u003C/code\u003E is 10 feet), millimeters in a millimeter model, and so on. Typing a unit always wins, whatever the model&#039;s unit. Curly quotes and prime marks (’ ” ′ ″) are read as feet and inches too.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-warning\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003EWarning\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAlways use a \u003Cstrong\u003Eperiod\u003C/strong\u003E for decimals: \u003Ccode\u003E2.5m\u003C/code\u003E. A comma separates two values, so \u003Ccode\u003E2,5\u003C/code\u003E means 2 and 5 (two values), whatever your language settings.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"per-tool\"\u003EPer tool\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETool\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ELabel\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EType\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELine\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELength\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA length (toward the pointer), or a point or offset (below)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERectangle\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDimensions\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwo lengths: \u003Ccode\u003E4m,3m\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;,10&#039;\u003C/code\u003E. A semicolon works too: \u003Ccode\u003E4m;3m\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECircle\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPolygon\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESides\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERadius\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBefore the first click, the number of sides: \u003Ccode\u003E12s\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E12\u003C/code\u003E (3 to 999). After it, the radius\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPush/pull\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDistance\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA length. A negative one goes the other way\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDistance\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA length (toward the pointer), or a point or offset. After a copy: \u003Ccode\u003E3x\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E*3\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E/3\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERotate\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAngle\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDegrees: \u003Ccode\u003E90\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E-45\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E22.5\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA factor above 0: \u003Ccode\u003E2\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E0.5\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E1.25\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETape\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffset\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELength\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA length\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDimension\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffset\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELength\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWhere the dimension line goes; right after placing, a new length resizes the model\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EValue\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWith one dimension or edge selected, a new length resizes the model. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/dimensions\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/dimensions\"\u003EDimension and resize\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"points-and-offsets\"\u003EPoints and offsets\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELine\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove\u003C/span\u003E, after the first click:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E[x,y,z]\u003C/code\u003E goes to that exact point in the model (in the group&#039;s own axes when you are editing inside a group). Example: \u003Ccode\u003E[0,0,3m]\u003C/code\u003E is 3 m straight above the origin.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E&lt;dx,dy,dz&gt;\u003C/code\u003E goes that far from the first point along red, green and blue. Example: \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;0,0,10&#039;&gt;\u003C/code\u003E is 10 feet straight up.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach of the three values can have its own unit: \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;12&#039;,0,6&quot;&gt;\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"copies-after-a-move\"\u003ECopies after a move\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ERight after a copy with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove\u003C/span\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EType\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMakes\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E3x\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E*3\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E3 copies in a row at the same spacing\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E/3\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E3 copies spread evenly from the original to the first copy\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUp to 500 copies. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/move-copy#a-row-of-copies\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/move-copy\" data-hash=\"a-row-of-copies\"\u003EMove, copy and make arrays\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"typed-lengths-need-a-direction\"\u003ETyped lengths need a direction\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA single typed length goes the way the pointer is pointing from the first click. If you type right after clicking, before moving at all, there is no direction yet and nothing happens. Move the pointer a little toward where you want to go, then type.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-model-s-unit\"\u003EThe model&#039;s unit\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChange the unit in the picker at the bottom right: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Eft and in\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Ein\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Em\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Ecm\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Emm\u003C/span\u003E. It changes how lengths are shown and how bare numbers are read; the model stays the same size. The unit is saved in the file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow sizes are shown:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EUnit\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ELengths\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EAreas\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EVolumes\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eft and in\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E10&#039; 6 1/2&quot;\u003C/code\u003E (to 1/16&quot;)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Esq ft\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ecu ft\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ein\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E126 1/2&quot;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Esq in\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ecu in\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Em\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E3.2 m\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Em²\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Em³\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ecm\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E320 cm\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ecm²\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ecm³\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Emm\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E3200 mm\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Em² from 0.01 m², else mm²\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Em³ from 0.01 m³, else mm³\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA new model starts in feet and inches when the browser&#039;s language is set to a US region (and Liberia and Myanmar), and in millimeters everywhere else. To start in meters, change the picker after \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The value box sits in the status bar at the bottom, with a label that changes with the tool. While you work it shows the live size; at any moment you can type an exact one. How to type Start the action with a click (the first corner, the face, the point to move from). Type. You don't need to click the value box: a digit or one of . , - ' \" [ \u003C * / sends your typing there. Press Enter. Press Esc in the value box to clear it and go back to the view. On a touch screen, tap the value box to bring up the keyboard. If ModelCraft cannot read what you typed, the status bar says \"Type a length such as 3m, 10'6\" or 4m,3m.\" If the tool cannot use it, it says \"That did not work: …\" with the reason. Lengths Type Means 3m 320cm 3200mm Meters, centimeters, millimeters 10' 10ft 10 feet Feet 6\" 6in 6 inches Inches 10'6\" 10' 6\" 10'-6 1/2\" Feet and inches, with a fraction 6 1/2\" 6-1/2\" 3/4\" Inches with a fraction 1.5ft .5m Decimals with a unit 120 A bare number, in the model's unit A bare number uses the model's unit, as shown in the picker at the bottom right: inches in a feet-and-inches model (so 120 is 10 feet), millimeters in a millimeter model, and so on. Typing a unit always wins, whatever the model's unit. Curly quotes and prime marks (’ ” ′ ″) are read as feet and inches too. Always use a period for decimals: 2.5m. A comma separates two values, so 2,5 means 2 and 5 (two values), whatever your language settings. Per tool Tool Label Type Line Length A length (toward the pointer), or a point or offset (below) Rectangle Dimensions Two lengths: 4m,3m, 12',10'. A semicolon works too: 4m;3m Circle, Polygon Sides, then Radius Before the first click, the number of sides: 12s or 12 (3 to 999). After it, the radius Push/pull Distance A length. A negative one goes the other way Move Distance A length (toward the pointer), or a point or offset. After a copy: 3x, *3, /3 Rotate Angle Degrees: 90, -45, 22.5 Scale Scale A factor above 0: 2, 0.5, 1.25 Tape Offset or Length A length Dimension Offset, then Length Where the dimension line goes; right after placing, a new length resizes the model Select Value With one dimension or edge selected, a new length resizes the model. See Dimension and resize Points and offsets For Line and Move, after the first click: [x,y,z] goes to that exact point in the model (in the group's own axes when you are editing inside a group). Example: [0,0,3m] is 3 m straight above the origin. \u003Cdx,dy,dz\u003E goes that far from the first point along red, green and blue. Example: \u003C0,0,10'\u003E is 10 feet straight up. Each of the three values can have its own unit: \u003C12',0,6\"\u003E. Copies after a move Right after a copy with Move: Type Makes 3x or *3 3 copies in a row at the same spacing /3 3 copies spread evenly from the original to the first copy Up to 500 copies. See Move, copy and make arrays. Typed lengths need a direction A single typed length goes the way the pointer is pointing from the first click. If you type right after clicking, before moving at all, there is no direction yet and nothing happens. Move the pointer a little toward where you want to go, then type. The model's unit Change the unit in the picker at the bottom right: ft and in, in, m, cm or mm. It changes how lengths are shown and how bare numbers are read; the model stays the same size. The unit is saved in the file. How sizes are shown: Unit Lengths Areas Volumes ft and in 10' 6 1/2\" (to 1/16\") sq ft cu ft in 126 1/2\" sq in cu in m 3.2 m m² m³ cm 320 cm cm² cm³ mm 3200 mm m² from 0.01 m², else mm² m³ from 0.01 m³, else mm³ A new model starts in feet and inches when the browser's language is set to a US region (and Liberia and Myanmar), and in millimeters everywhere else. To start in meters, change the picker after New."},{"slug":"formats","scope":"modelcraft","title":"File formats in detail","summary":"Exactly what each format ModelCraft opens, imports and exports keeps, converts and leaves out.","section":"reference","order":50,"keywords":["formats","file types","modelcraft","json","collada","dae","obj","gltf","glb","stl","ifc","dwg","dxf","units","axes","y up","z up","millimeters","limits"],"headings":[{"id":"modelcraft-modelcraft","text":"ModelCraft (.modelcraft)","level":2},{"id":"collada-dae","text":"Collada (.dae)","level":2},{"id":"obj-obj","text":"OBJ (.obj)","level":2},{"id":"gltf-glb-gltf","text":"glTF (.glb, .gltf)","level":2},{"id":"stl-stl","text":"STL (.stl)","level":2},{"id":"dwg-and-dxf-export-dwg-dxf","text":"DWG and DXF export (.dwg, .dxf)","level":2},{"id":"ifc-ifc","text":"IFC (.ifc)","level":2},{"id":"dwg-dwg-and-dxf-dxf","text":"DWG (.dwg) and DXF (.dxf)","level":2},{"id":"not-supported","text":"Not supported","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EModelCraft&#039;s model is in meters with Z (blue) up. Every format below is converted to or from that.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"modelcraft-modelcraft\"\u003EModelCraft (.modelcraft)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDetails\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command O\"\u003E⌘O\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+O\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) or drop on the view. Files ending in \u003Ccode\u003E.json\u003C/code\u003E are accepted too\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESave\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), as a download\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EKeeps\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEverything: geometry, faces and their colors, soft edges, groups, components, names, guides, scenes, the units, and the last camera and style\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELeaves out\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe undo history\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELimit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E200 MB\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA file saved by a newer ModelCraft than yours is refused: &quot;This model was saved by a newer ModelCraft (file version …). Update ModelCraft to open it.&quot; A file that is not a ModelCraft model says &quot;This file is not a ModelCraft model (it is not JSON).&quot; or &quot;This is not a ModelCraft file we can open: …&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"collada-dae\"\u003ECollada (.dae)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EImport and export. The best format for trading models with other 3D modelers.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EExport:\u003C/strong\u003E Collada 1.4.1, Z up, meters. One geometry per group or component definition; components are written once and placed by reference, so they stay shared. Faces with holes or concave outlines are split into triangles. Colors become materials. Loose edges are written as lines. Names that are not valid in the format are adjusted, and the exact name is kept alongside it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EImport:\u003C/strong\u003E triangles, polygons (with holes), triangle fans and strips become faces; lines become edges. Node transforms, the file&#039;s up axis and its unit are applied. A node placed in several places, or a geometry used by several nodes, comes in as a component; other nodes come in as groups. Material colors (diffuse) become face colors.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENot imported:\u003C/strong\u003E textures, skins and morphs, cameras, lights, some transform types (look-at and skew), and references to other files. The status bar counts them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELost on export:\u003C/strong\u003E which edges are soft. \u003Cstrong\u003EOn import,\u003C/strong\u003E triangulated surfaces stay triangles, with the diagonals softened so flat areas still read as one surface.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"obj-obj\"\u003EOBJ (.obj)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EImport and export.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EExport:\u003C/strong\u003E meters, Y up (the way OBJ readers expect), world coordinates. One object per group placement; components are written out in full at every placement. Faces with holes are split into triangles. Loose edges are written as lines. When the model has colors, a \u003Ccode\u003E.mtl\u003C/code\u003E materials file downloads beside the \u003Ccode\u003E.obj\u003C/code\u003E (one material per color); keep the two together. Faces joined by \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/soften\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/soften\"\u003Esoft edges\u003C/a\u003E get a normal per corner, so they shade smooth in other programs.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EImport:\u003C/strong\u003E read as meters with Y up. OBJ has no unit, so a model made in millimeters or inches arrives at the wrong size: scale it after import (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/rotate-scale#fix-an-import-that-came-in-the-wrong-size\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/rotate-scale\" data-hash=\"fix-an-import-that-came-in-the-wrong-size\"\u003ERotate and scale\u003C/a\u003E). Materials are not read.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELimit:\u003C/strong\u003E 2 million vertices or faces.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"gltf-glb-gltf\"\u003EglTF (.glb, .gltf)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EExport\u003C/strong\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003E.glb\u003C/code\u003E only): glTF 2.0 binary, meters, Y up. One mesh per definition, so components stay shared. Colors become materials. Faces joined by \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/soften\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/soften\"\u003Esoft edges\u003C/a\u003E carry smooth normals. Loose edges are left out.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EImport:\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E.glb\u003C/code\u003E, or \u003Ccode\u003E.gltf\u003C/code\u003E with its data inside the file. A \u003Ccode\u003E.gltf\u003C/code\u003E that keeps its data in a separate \u003Ccode\u003E.bin\u003C/code\u003E file is refused: &quot;This glTF file keeps its data in a separate file (…). Export it as a single .glb and open that.&quot; Base colors become face colors; textures and extensions are not read.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELimit:\u003C/strong\u003E 2 million vertices.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"stl-stl\"\u003ESTL (.stl)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EExport only, for 3D printing. Binary STL, \u003Cstrong\u003Emillimeters\u003C/strong\u003E (printer software reads STL as millimeters), Z up. Triangles only: groups, colors and loose edges are left out. Export closed solids for a clean print.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"dwg-and-dxf-export-dwg-dxf\"\u003EDWG and DXF export (.dwg, .dxf)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor CAD programs. The model is written in its own unit: inches for feet-and-inches models, otherwise millimeters, centimeters or meters (the status bar names it).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFaces\u003C/strong\u003E become 3D faces (triangles). Lines inside a face, and soft edges, are written invisible, so the faces look like ModelCraft&#039;s.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELoose edges\u003C/strong\u003E become lines.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EGroups and components\u003C/strong\u003E become blocks: a component is written once and placed as often as it is used, turned and mirrored copies included. A group that was stretched after it was turned (so it is slanted) is written out in place instead.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EColors:\u003C/strong\u003E DWG keeps each face&#039;s color exactly; DXF uses the nearest of the 255 standard CAD colors.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EVersions:\u003C/strong\u003E DWG is written as AutoCAD 2004 (AC1018), which CAD programs from 2004 on open. DXF is R12 (AC1009), the version every CAD program reads; R12 has no unit field, so tell the CAD program which unit to use.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENot written:\u003C/strong\u003E guides, dimensions, scenes, and the camera.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"ifc-ifc\"\u003EIFC (.ifc)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EExport only, for BimCraft and other BIM programs. IFC4 with a project, a site, a building and its storeys. Each top-level group or component becomes one building element proxy with its name; closed ones are written as solid shapes (faceted breps), open ones as surface models; holes in faces are kept. Loose geometry at the top level becomes one element called &quot;Loose geometry&quot;. No types, properties or materials.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EStoreys:\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIFC storeys…\u003C/span\u003E lists them, each with a name and an elevation (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd storey\u003C/span\u003E, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGuess from the model\u003C/span\u003E, which makes one storey for each height your top-level groups stand on). On export, each group or component goes on the storey at or below its lowest point, and keeps its place. With no storeys, everything goes on one storey, &quot;Level 1&quot;, at 0. The storeys are saved in the ModelCraft file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"dwg-dwg-and-dxf-dxf\"\u003EDWG (.dwg) and DXF (.dxf)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EImport as lines on the ground (hatches and 3D faces as faces); export as described above. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/site-plan\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/site-plan\"\u003EModel on a site plan\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERead:\u003C/strong\u003E lines, polylines (with arcs), 3D polylines, circles, arcs, and block references including arrays, from model space. Invisible objects (such as the hidden states of a dynamic block) are left out. Curves become 48 straight pieces per full turn.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERead since 0.4:\u003C/strong\u003E splines and ellipses (as edges), text, multiline text and attribute values (as edges in a single-stroke lettering), hatches (faces with holes), 3D faces and solid fills (faces), and dimensions (drawn from the drawing&#039;s own dimension blocks).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENot read:\u003C/strong\u003E points, tables, multileaders, regions and 3D solids, images, and anything in paper space.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUnits:\u003C/strong\u003E taken from the drawing when it says. If not, ModelCraft asks.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDXF\u003C/strong\u003E must be ASCII. Binary DXF is refused.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDWG\u003C/strong\u003E files from AutoCAD R13 up to the current format (AutoCAD 2018 and later; AC1012 to AC1032) are read by ModelCraft&#039;s own DWG reader, in the page; it is part of ModelCraft, so it works offline whenever ModelCraft does. Older versions (R12 and before) and badly damaged files cannot be read; save them as DXF.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELimits:\u003C/strong\u003E coordinates more than 10,000 km from the origin, or more than 2 million line pieces, are refused.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESaving DWG or DXF\u003C/strong\u003E is not built.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"not-supported\"\u003ENot supported\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EModelCraft cannot open or save SketchUp (\u003Ccode\u003E.skp\u003C/code\u003E), 3DS, FBX, Rhino, STEP or IFC files. To bring in a model from SketchUp, export it from there as Collada (\u003Ccode\u003E.dae\u003C/code\u003E) if your version can, or as OBJ.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"ModelCraft's model is in meters with Z (blue) up. Every format below is converted to or from that. ModelCraft (.modelcraft) What Details Open Open (Ctrl+O) or drop on the view. Files ending in .json are accepted too Save Save (Ctrl+S), as a download Keeps Everything: geometry, faces and their colors, soft edges, groups, components, names, guides, scenes, the units, and the last camera and style Leaves out The undo history Limit 200 MB A file saved by a newer ModelCraft than yours is refused: \"This model was saved by a newer ModelCraft (file version …). Update ModelCraft to open it.\" A file that is not a ModelCraft model says \"This file is not a ModelCraft model (it is not JSON).\" or \"This is not a ModelCraft file we can open: …\". Collada (.dae) Import and export. The best format for trading models with other 3D modelers. Export: Collada 1.4.1, Z up, meters. One geometry per group or component definition; components are written once and placed by reference, so they stay shared. Faces with holes or concave outlines are split into triangles. Colors become materials. Loose edges are written as lines. Names that are not valid in the format are adjusted, and the exact name is kept alongside it. Import: triangles, polygons (with holes), triangle fans and strips become faces; lines become edges. Node transforms, the file's up axis and its unit are applied. A node placed in several places, or a geometry used by several nodes, comes in as a component; other nodes come in as groups. Material colors (diffuse) become face colors. Not imported: textures, skins and morphs, cameras, lights, some transform types (look-at and skew), and references to other files. The status bar counts them. Lost on export: which edges are soft. On import, triangulated surfaces stay triangles, with the diagonals softened so flat areas still read as one surface. OBJ (.obj) Import and export. Export: meters, Y up (the way OBJ readers expect), world coordinates. One object per group placement; components are written out in full at every placement. Faces with holes are split into triangles. Loose edges are written as lines. When the model has colors, a .mtl materials file downloads beside the .obj (one material per color); keep the two together. Faces joined by soft edges get a normal per corner, so they shade smooth in other programs. Import: read as meters with Y up. OBJ has no unit, so a model made in millimeters or inches arrives at the wrong size: scale it after import (see Rotate and scale). Materials are not read. Limit: 2 million vertices or faces. glTF (.glb, .gltf) Export (.glb only): glTF 2.0 binary, meters, Y up. One mesh per definition, so components stay shared. Colors become materials. Faces joined by soft edges carry smooth normals. Loose edges are left out. Import: .glb, or .gltf with its data inside the file. A .gltf that keeps its data in a separate .bin file is refused: \"This glTF file keeps its data in a separate file (…). Export it as a single .glb and open that.\" Base colors become face colors; textures and extensions are not read. Limit: 2 million vertices. STL (.stl) Export only, for 3D printing. Binary STL, millimeters (printer software reads STL as millimeters), Z up. Triangles only: groups, colors and loose edges are left out. Export closed solids for a clean print. DWG and DXF export (.dwg, .dxf) For CAD programs. The model is written in its own unit: inches for feet-and-inches models, otherwise millimeters, centimeters or meters (the status bar names it). Faces become 3D faces (triangles). Lines inside a face, and soft edges, are written invisible, so the faces look like ModelCraft's. Loose edges become lines. Groups and components become blocks: a component is written once and placed as often as it is used, turned and mirrored copies included. A group that was stretched after it was turned (so it is slanted) is written out in place instead. Colors: DWG keeps each face's color exactly; DXF uses the nearest of the 255 standard CAD colors. Versions: DWG is written as AutoCAD 2004 (AC1018), which CAD programs from 2004 on open. DXF is R12 (AC1009), the version every CAD program reads; R12 has no unit field, so tell the CAD program which unit to use. Not written: guides, dimensions, scenes, and the camera. IFC (.ifc) Export only, for BimCraft and other BIM programs. IFC4 with a project, a site, a building and its storeys. Each top-level group or component becomes one building element proxy with its name; closed ones are written as solid shapes (faceted breps), open ones as surface models; holes in faces are kept. Loose geometry at the top level becomes one element called \"Loose geometry\". No types, properties or materials. Storeys: Export › IFC storeys… lists them, each with a name and an elevation (Add storey, or Guess from the model, which makes one storey for each height your top-level groups stand on). On export, each group or component goes on the storey at or below its lowest point, and keeps its place. With no storeys, everything goes on one storey, \"Level 1\", at 0. The storeys are saved in the ModelCraft file. DWG (.dwg) and DXF (.dxf) Import as lines on the ground (hatches and 3D faces as faces); export as described above. See Model on a site plan. Read: lines, polylines (with arcs), 3D polylines, circles, arcs, and block references including arrays, from model space. Invisible objects (such as the hidden states of a dynamic block) are left out. Curves become 48 straight pieces per full turn. Read since 0.4: splines and ellipses (as edges), text, multiline text and attribute values (as edges in a single-stroke lettering), hatches (faces with holes), 3D faces and solid fills (faces), and dimensions (drawn from the drawing's own dimension blocks). Not read: points, tables, multileaders, regions and 3D solids, images, and anything in paper space. Units: taken from the drawing when it says. If not, ModelCraft asks. DXF must be ASCII. Binary DXF is refused. DWG files from AutoCAD R13 up to the current format (AutoCAD 2018 and later; AC1012 to AC1032) are read by ModelCraft's own DWG reader, in the page; it is part of ModelCraft, so it works offline whenever ModelCraft does. Older versions (R12 and before) and badly damaged files cannot be read; save them as DXF. Limits: coordinates more than 10,000 km from the origin, or more than 2 million line pieces, are refused. Saving DWG or DXF is not built. Not supported ModelCraft cannot open or save SketchUp (.skp), 3DS, FBX, Rhino, STEP or IFC files. To bring in a model from SketchUp, export it from there as Collada (.dae) if your version can, or as OBJ."},{"slug":"troubleshooting","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Troubleshooting","summary":"Symptoms, causes and fixes, from a blank 3D view to imports at the wrong size, with the messages ModelCraft shows.","section":"trouble","order":10,"keywords":["problem","trouble","error","not working","blank","webgl","graphics","slow","message","import failed","wrong size","cannot select","touch","keys not working","lost work"],"headings":[{"id":"the-3d-view-is-blank","text":"The 3D view is blank","level":2},{"id":"keys-do-nothing-or-type-into-a-box","text":"Keys do nothing, or type into a box","level":2},{"id":"typing-a-number-does-nothing","text":"Typing a number does nothing","level":2},{"id":"a-shape-starts-in-the-wrong-place-on-a-touch-screen","text":"A shape starts in the wrong place on a touch screen","level":2},{"id":"editing-one-group-changed-another","text":"Editing one group changed another","level":2},{"id":"push-pull-does-not-do-what-i-expected","text":"Push/pull does not do what I expected","level":2},{"id":"a-line-or-shape-did-not-make-a-face","text":"A line or shape did not make a face","level":2},{"id":"i-can-t-select-or-erase-a-face","text":"I can't select or erase a face","level":2},{"id":"select-something-to-move-first-or-click-on-it","text":"\"Select something to move first, or click on it.\"","level":2},{"id":"select-some-geometry-first-drag-a-window-or-triple-click-to-select-everything-connected","text":"\"Select some geometry first (drag a window, or triple-click to select everything connected).\"","level":2},{"id":"the-arrow-keys-orbit-instead-of-locking","text":"The arrow keys orbit instead of locking","level":2},{"id":"esc-in-full-screen","text":"Esc in full screen","level":2},{"id":"no-middle-button","text":"No middle button","level":2},{"id":"imports","text":"Imports","level":2},{"id":"the-import-is-the-wrong-size-or-lying-on-its-side","text":"The import is the wrong size or lying on its side","level":3},{"id":"opening-files","text":"Opening files","level":2},{"id":"i-lost-work","text":"I lost work","level":2},{"id":"my-downloads-folder-fills-up-with-copies","text":"My downloads folder fills up with copies","level":2},{"id":"it-gets-slow","text":"It gets slow","level":2},{"id":"no-shadows","text":"No shadows","level":2},{"id":"this-browser-did-not-allow-full-screen","text":"\"This browser did not allow full screen.\"","level":2},{"id":"modelcraft-does-not-open-without-a-connection","text":"ModelCraft does not open without a connection","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"the-3d-view-is-blank\"\u003EThe 3D view is blank\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESymptom:\u003C/strong\u003E the middle of the screen stays empty, or shows only the background color, and clicking tools does nothing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECause:\u003C/strong\u003E ModelCraft draws with WebGL 2, the browser&#039;s 3D graphics. It is missing when the browser is old, when hardware acceleration is turned off, or when the graphics driver is blocked.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFix:\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:chromium\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EChrome or Edge\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the browser&#039;s settings and search for &quot;graphics acceleration&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETurn on &quot;Use graphics acceleration when available&quot; and relaunch the browser.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf it is already on, type \u003Ccode\u003Echrome://gpu\u003C/code\u003E in the address bar (\u003Ccode\u003Eedge://gpu\u003C/code\u003E in Edge) and look for &quot;WebGL2&quot; in the list. If it says disabled, update your graphics driver.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:firefox\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EFirefox\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen \u003Cstrong\u003ESettings\u003C/strong\u003E, then \u003Cstrong\u003EGeneral\u003C/strong\u003E, and scroll to &quot;Performance&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUntick &quot;Use recommended performance settings&quot;, then tick &quot;Use hardware acceleration when available&quot;. Restart Firefox.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf it still fails, type \u003Ccode\u003Eabout:support\u003C/code\u003E in the address bar and look at the &quot;Graphics&quot; section for WebGL 2.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:safari\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ESafari\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUpdate macOS and Safari. Recent versions of Safari always have WebGL 2 on. If you changed experimental features under \u003Cstrong\u003ESafari\u003C/strong\u003E, then \u003Cstrong\u003ESettings\u003C/strong\u003E, then \u003Cstrong\u003EAdvanced\u003C/strong\u003E (or \u003Cstrong\u003EFeature Flags\u003C/strong\u003E), reset them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a work computer, remote desktop or virtual machine, 3D graphics may be blocked by policy. Try another computer. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/browsers\" data-topic=\"basics/browsers\"\u003EBrowsers and computers\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"keys-do-nothing-or-type-into-a-box\"\u003EKeys do nothing, or type into a box\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECause:\u003C/strong\u003E the keyboard focus is in a panel field: the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFaces\u003C/span\u003E list in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStyle\u003C/span\u003E, a sun setting, the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EName\u003C/span\u003E field in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E, or the units picker. Letters and arrow keys then go to that field.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFix:\u003C/strong\u003E click in the 3D view, then press the key again. Keys also do nothing while a dialog is open.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"typing-a-number-does-nothing\"\u003ETyping a number does nothing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENo direction yet.\u003C/strong\u003E A typed length goes the way the pointer points from the first click. Move the pointer a little in the direction you want, then type.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWrong moment.\u003C/strong\u003E Most tools take a typed value only after the first click: click the first corner, the face or the point to move from first. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECircle\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPolygon\u003C/span\u003E take a number of sides before the first click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;Type a length such as 3m, 10&#039;6&quot; or 4m,3m.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E ModelCraft could not read it. Check for a decimal comma (\u003Ccode\u003E2,5\u003C/code\u003E): use a period.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-shape-starts-in-the-wrong-place-on-a-touch-screen\"\u003EA shape starts in the wrong place on a touch screen\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESymptom:\u003C/strong\u003E with a finger, the first rectangle is fine, but the next rectangle, circle or line starts where your previous touch ended instead of where you touched.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECause:\u003C/strong\u003E a bug in this build: on a touch screen the tool remembers the last snap point from the previous gesture.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFix:\u003C/strong\u003E tap the tool&#039;s button in the toolbar again before each new shape. That starts the tool fresh, and the first point lands where you touch.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"editing-one-group-changed-another\"\u003EEditing one group changed another\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESymptom:\u003C/strong\u003E you copied a group with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, opened one copy and edited it, and the other copy changed too. The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOutliner\u003C/span\u003E shows both with the same name.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECause:\u003C/strong\u003E in this build, copying a group makes a second placement of the same group, as with a component. ModelCraft&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMake unique\u003C/span\u003E button is only offered for components.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFix:\u003C/strong\u003E select the copy you want to change, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMake component\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEntity info\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMake unique\u003C/span\u003E. That copy now has its own contents. Or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExplode\u003C/span\u003E it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"push-pull-does-not-do-what-i-expected\"\u003EPush/pull does not do what I expected\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;Push/pull works on a face. Click a face.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E You clicked an edge or empty space. Hover until the face highlights, then click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe face is inside a group.\u003C/strong\u003E Double-click the group to open it first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA window did not cut through the wall.\u003C/strong\u003E The rectangle must be drawn on the wall&#039;s face, not in front of it, and the wall must have a back face. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/openings#if-it-did-not-cut-through\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/openings\" data-hash=\"if-it-did-not-cut-through\"\u003ECut an opening through a wall\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETwo boxes overlap without merging.\u003C/strong\u003E Solids pushed into each other do not cut or join each other in this build.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-line-or-shape-did-not-make-a-face\"\u003EA line or shape did not make a face\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA face fills in only when the edges close a flat loop. Check that the last point snapped to the first (&quot;Endpoint&quot;), and that all the edges lie in one plane. A loop that is slightly out of plane stays as lines. Draw one of its edges again, snapping to the corners.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"i-can-t-select-or-erase-a-face\"\u003EI can&#039;t select or erase a face\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E, a click near an edge takes the edge. Click nearer the middle of the face.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEraser\u003C/span\u003E does not pick faces. Select the face and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or erase one of its edges.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf it belongs to a group or component, the click selects the whole thing. Double-click to open it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"select-something-to-move-first-or-click-on-it\"\u003E&quot;Select something to move first, or click on it.&quot;\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERotate\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale\u003C/span\u003E need something to work on: select it first, or start the tool by clicking on the thing itself (not on empty space or a guide).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"select-some-geometry-first-drag-a-window-or-triple-click-to-select-everything-connected\"\u003E&quot;Select some geometry first (drag a window, or triple-click to select everything connected).&quot;\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"G\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift G\"\u003E⇧G\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+G\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E need a selection. Guides cannot be grouped.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-arrow-keys-orbit-instead-of-locking\"\u003EThe arrow keys orbit instead of locking\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELocks work only after the first click of a drawing or editing tool. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPush/pull\u003C/span\u003E has no locks, and in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERotate\u003C/span\u003E they do not change the turn. At other times the arrow keys orbit the view.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"esc-in-full-screen\"\u003EEsc in full screen\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn full screen, the browser may use the first \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to leave full screen. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E again to cancel the tool.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"no-middle-button\"\u003ENo middle button\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"O\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOrbit\u003C/span\u003E and drag, or use the arrow keys. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/orbit-pan-zoom#without-a-middle-button\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/orbit-pan-zoom\" data-hash=\"without-a-middle-button\"\u003EOrbit, pan and zoom\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"imports\"\u003EImports\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMessage\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat to do\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;ModelCraft imports Collada (.dae), OBJ, glTF (.glb/.gltf), DWG and DXF. … is none of these.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExport the model from the other program in one of these formats\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;This file is over 300 MB.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESimplify or split the model in the other program\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;…too big to model with here (over 2 million vertices).&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;This glTF file keeps its data in a separate file…&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExport it as a single \u003Ccode\u003E.glb\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;This is not a Collada (.dae) file…&quot; or &quot;…not well-formed XML…&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe file is damaged or not Collada. Export it again\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;This Collada file has no geometry ModelCraft can import.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIt holds only cameras, lights or references to other files\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;This is a binary DXF. Save it as ASCII DXF and try again.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESave it again from the CAD program as ASCII\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;This is not a DWG drawing…&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe file is not a DWG, or it is damaged\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;This drawing could not be read (…). It may be damaged, or saved in a DWG version the reader does not handle…&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe brackets give the reason. Save it as DXF, or as a DWG from AutoCAD 2000 to 2018, from the CAD program\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;This drawing reaches more than 10,000 km from its origin…&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMove the drawing near 0,0 in the CAD program\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"the-import-is-the-wrong-size-or-lying-on-its-side\"\u003EThe import is the wrong size or lying on its side\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOBJ:\u003C/strong\u003E OBJ has no unit; ModelCraft reads meters. A millimeter model comes in 1000 times too big: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/rotate-scale#fix-an-import-that-came-in-the-wrong-size\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/rotate-scale\" data-hash=\"fix-an-import-that-came-in-the-wrong-size\"\u003Escale\u003C/a\u003E it by \u003Ccode\u003E0.001\u003C/code\u003E; an inch model by \u003Ccode\u003E0.0254\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOBJ on its side:\u003C/strong\u003E ModelCraft expects OBJ files with Y up. A file written with Z up comes in lying down. Rotate it 90° about the red axis: click the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERotate\u003C/span\u003E center on a face that faces along red, or open the file in its program and export with Y up.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDWG or DXF:\u003C/strong\u003E you picked the wrong unit in the &quot;Drawing units&quot; dialog. Undo and import again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"opening-files\"\u003EOpening files\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMessage\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat to do\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;This file is not a ModelCraft model (it is not JSON).&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUse \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImport\u003C/span\u003E for other formats\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;This is not a ModelCraft file we can open: …&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe file is damaged or was changed by hand\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;This model was saved by a newer ModelCraft…&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen it in the newer version\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;This file is over 200 MB.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe model is too large to open here\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"i-lost-work\"\u003EI lost work\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EModelCraft does not save by itself. When you close or reload a tab with unsaved changes, the browser asks first (&quot;Leave site?&quot; or &quot;Reload site?&quot;); choose to stay and save. New and Open ask too (&quot;Unsaved changes&quot;). If the browser or computer crashed, the work since your last save is gone. Save often with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"my-downloads-folder-fills-up-with-copies\"\u003EMy downloads folder fills up with copies\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach save is a new download, named \u003Ccode\u003EUntitled (1).modelcraft\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EUntitled (2).modelcraft\u003C/code\u003E and so on. Delete the old ones, or set the browser to ask where to save each file and overwrite the old file yourself. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/files#save-a-model\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/files\" data-hash=\"save-a-model\"\u003EOpen, save, import and export\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"it-gets-slow\"\u003EIt gets slow\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEvery step of undo keeps a full copy of the model, so on a very large import each edit takes longer. Explode or erase what you don&#039;t need, save, and reopen: reopening also clears the undo history.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESun shadows cost the most. Untick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESun shadows\u003C/span\u003E while you model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"no-shadows\"\u003ENo shadows\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESun shadows\u003C/span\u003E is off, the sun is down at the time you chose (&quot;The sun is down at this time.&quot;), or the style is \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWireframe\u003C/span\u003E, which draws no shadows.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"this-browser-did-not-allow-full-screen\"\u003E&quot;This browser did not allow full screen.&quot;\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESome browsers and some embedded views refuse full screen. Use the browser&#039;s own full screen instead (\u003Cspan class=\"hp\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:mac\" title=\"Mac\"\u003EControl-Command-F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hp-sep\"\u003E / \u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:windows,linux\" title=\"Windows or Linux\"\u003EF11\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/span\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"modelcraft-does-not-open-without-a-connection\"\u003EModelCraft does not open without a connection\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EModelCraft opens offline only after it was saved for offline use in that browser: the app bar&#039;s button must read \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline ready\u003C/span\u003E. Open it while online and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E. If the button reads \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECould not save\u003C/span\u003E, click it again on a better connection; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline not available\u003C/span\u003E means this window (a private window, or a managed computer) cannot keep a copy. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/offline\" data-topic=\"basics/offline\"\u003EWorking offline\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The 3D view is blank Symptom: the middle of the screen stays empty, or shows only the background color, and clicking tools does nothing. Cause: ModelCraft draws with WebGL 2, the browser's 3D graphics. It is missing when the browser is old, when hardware acceleration is turned off, or when the graphics driver is blocked. Fix: Open the browser's settings and search for \"graphics acceleration\". Turn on \"Use graphics acceleration when available\" and relaunch the browser. If it is already on, type chrome://gpu in the address bar (edge://gpu in Edge) and look for \"WebGL2\" in the list. If it says disabled, update your graphics driver. Open Settings, then General, and scroll to \"Performance\". Untick \"Use recommended performance settings\", then tick \"Use hardware acceleration when available\". Restart Firefox. If it still fails, type about:support in the address bar and look at the \"Graphics\" section for WebGL 2. Update macOS and Safari. Recent versions of Safari always have WebGL 2 on. If you changed experimental features under Safari, then Settings, then Advanced (or Feature Flags), reset them. On a work computer, remote desktop or virtual machine, 3D graphics may be blocked by policy. Try another computer. See Browsers and computers. Keys do nothing, or type into a box Cause: the keyboard focus is in a panel field: the Faces list in Style, a sun setting, the Name field in Entity info, or the units picker. Letters and arrow keys then go to that field. Fix: click in the 3D view, then press the key again. Keys also do nothing while a dialog is open. Typing a number does nothing No direction yet. A typed length goes the way the pointer points from the first click. Move the pointer a little in the direction you want, then type. Wrong moment. Most tools take a typed value only after the first click: click the first corner, the face or the point to move from first. Circle and Polygon take a number of sides before the first click. \"Type a length such as 3m, 10'6\" or 4m,3m.\" ModelCraft could not read it. Check for a decimal comma (2,5): use a period. A shape starts in the wrong place on a touch screen Symptom: with a finger, the first rectangle is fine, but the next rectangle, circle or line starts where your previous touch ended instead of where you touched. Cause: a bug in this build: on a touch screen the tool remembers the last snap point from the previous gesture. Fix: tap the tool's button in the toolbar again before each new shape. That starts the tool fresh, and the first point lands where you touch. Editing one group changed another Symptom: you copied a group with Move and Ctrl, opened one copy and edited it, and the other copy changed too. The Outliner shows both with the same name. Cause: in this build, copying a group makes a second placement of the same group, as with a component. ModelCraft's Make unique button is only offered for components. Fix: select the copy you want to change, click Make component in Entity info, then Make unique. That copy now has its own contents. Or Explode it. Push/pull does not do what I expected \"Push/pull works on a face. Click a face.\" You clicked an edge or empty space. Hover until the face highlights, then click. The face is inside a group. Double-click the group to open it first. A window did not cut through the wall. The rectangle must be drawn on the wall's face, not in front of it, and the wall must have a back face. See Cut an opening through a wall. Two boxes overlap without merging. Solids pushed into each other do not cut or join each other in this build. A line or shape did not make a face A face fills in only when the edges close a flat loop. Check that the last point snapped to the first (\"Endpoint\"), and that all the edges lie in one plane. A loop that is slightly out of plane stays as lines. Draw one of its edges again, snapping to the corners. I can't select or erase a face With Select, a click near an edge takes the edge. Click nearer the middle of the face. The Eraser does not pick faces. Select the face and press Delete, or erase one of its edges. If it belongs to a group or component, the click selects the whole thing. Double-click to open it. \"Select something to move first, or click on it.\" Move, Rotate and Scale need something to work on: select it first, or start the tool by clicking on the thing itself (not on empty space or a guide). \"Select some geometry first (drag a window, or triple-click to select everything connected).\" G and Shift+G need a selection. Guides cannot be grouped. The arrow keys orbit instead of locking Locks work only after the first click of a drawing or editing tool. Push/pull has no locks, and in Rotate they do not change the turn. At other times the arrow keys orbit the view. Esc in full screen In full screen, the browser may use the first Esc to leave full screen. Press Esc again to cancel the tool. No middle button Press O for Orbit and drag, or use the arrow keys. See Orbit, pan and zoom. Imports Message What to do \"ModelCraft imports Collada (.dae), OBJ, glTF (.glb/.gltf), DWG and DXF. … is none of these.\" Export the model from the other program in one of these formats \"This file is over 300 MB.\" Simplify or split the model in the other program \"…too big to model with here (over 2 million vertices).\" The same \"This glTF file keeps its data in a separate file…\" Export it as a single .glb \"This is not a Collada (.dae) file…\" or \"…not well-formed XML…\" The file is damaged or not Collada. Export it again \"This Collada file has no geometry ModelCraft can import.\" It holds only cameras, lights or references to other files \"This is a binary DXF. Save it as ASCII DXF and try again.\" Save it again from the CAD program as ASCII \"This is not a DWG drawing…\" The file is not a DWG, or it is damaged \"This drawing could not be read (…). It may be damaged, or saved in a DWG version the reader does not handle…\" The brackets give the reason. Save it as DXF, or as a DWG from AutoCAD 2000 to 2018, from the CAD program \"This drawing reaches more than 10,000 km from its origin…\" Move the drawing near 0,0 in the CAD program The import is the wrong size or lying on its side OBJ: OBJ has no unit; ModelCraft reads meters. A millimeter model comes in 1000 times too big: scale it by 0.001; an inch model by 0.0254. OBJ on its side: ModelCraft expects OBJ files with Y up. A file written with Z up comes in lying down. Rotate it 90° about the red axis: click the Rotate center on a face that faces along red, or open the file in its program and export with Y up. DWG or DXF: you picked the wrong unit in the \"Drawing units\" dialog. Undo and import again. Opening files Message What to do \"This file is not a ModelCraft model (it is not JSON).\" Use Import for other formats \"This is not a ModelCraft file we can open: …\" The file is damaged or was changed by hand \"This model was saved by a newer ModelCraft…\" Open it in the newer version \"This file is over 200 MB.\" The model is too large to open here I lost work ModelCraft does not save by itself. When you close or reload a tab with unsaved changes, the browser asks first (\"Leave site?\" or \"Reload site?\"); choose to stay and save. New and Open ask too (\"Unsaved changes\"). If the browser or computer crashed, the work since your last save is gone. Save often with Ctrl+S. My downloads folder fills up with copies Each save is a new download, named Untitled (1).modelcraft, Untitled (2).modelcraft and so on. Delete the old ones, or set the browser to ask where to save each file and overwrite the old file yourself. See Open, save, import and export. It gets slow Every step of undo keeps a full copy of the model, so on a very large import each edit takes longer. Explode or erase what you don't need, save, and reopen: reopening also clears the undo history. Sun shadows cost the most. Untick Sun shadows while you model. No shadows Sun shadows is off, the sun is down at the time you chose (\"The sun is down at this time.\"), or the style is Wireframe, which draws no shadows. \"This browser did not allow full screen.\" Some browsers and some embedded views refuse full screen. Use the browser's own full screen instead (Control-Command-F / F11). ModelCraft does not open without a connection ModelCraft opens offline only after it was saved for offline use in that browser: the app bar's button must read Offline ready. Open it while online and click Save for offline. If the button reads Could not save, click it again on a better connection; Offline not available means this window (a private window, or a managed computer) cannot keep a copy. See Working offline."},{"slug":"not-built-yet","scope":"modelcraft","title":"Not built yet","summary":"What this build of ModelCraft does not do yet, and what to do instead today.","section":"trouble","order":20,"keywords":["missing","not yet","limitations","roadmap","materials","textures","text","layers","trim","split","cloud","desktop"],"headings":[{"id":"drawing-tools","text":"Drawing tools","level":2},{"id":"organizing-and-viewing","text":"Organizing and viewing","level":2},{"id":"materials-and-colors","text":"Materials and colors","level":2},{"id":"files","text":"Files","level":2},{"id":"performance","text":"Performance","level":2},{"id":"apps-and-platforms","text":"Apps and platforms","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EModelCraft is an early build. This page lists what it does not do yet, so you don&#039;t go looking for it, and the nearest way to get the job done today.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"drawing-tools\"\u003EDrawing tools\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFreehand drawing\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick the points with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECurve\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChanging an arc&#039;s or circle&#039;s number of segments after drawing it\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EErase it and draw it again after typing \u003Ccode\u003E24s\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChoosing a typeface for \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/text3d\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/text3d\"\u003E3D text\u003C/a\u003E, or editing its words after placing it\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDelete it and add the text again\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChanging an angle by typing into an angle dimension, or a cylinder&#039;s size from its circle&#039;s dimension\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERotate the edges with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERotate\u003C/span\u003E; scale the cylinder with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDimensions in exported model files\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDimensions show in images, prints and PDFs of the view; model files carry geometry only\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETrim, Split and Outer shell\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/solids\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/solids\"\u003EUnion, Subtract and Intersect\u003C/a\u003E closed groups, or \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/intersect\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/intersect\"\u003Eintersect faces\u003C/a\u003E and erase what you do not need\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChoosing the rotation axis with the arrow keys\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERotate\u003C/span\u003E center on a face that faces the way the axis should point\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELocks on more than one inference at a time\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELock one direction with an arrow key, then snap to a point\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EResizing along a slant, or from a dimension end that is not on a corner\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlace the dimension on two corners, along an edge or along red, green or blue\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"organizing-and-viewing\"\u003EOrganizing and viewing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELayers of DWG and DXF site plans as tags\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe site plan comes in as one group: explode it and group the parts you want to tag\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESeveral section cuts at once, a section box, or a section&#039;s outline exported as a drawing\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/sections\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/sections\"\u003Esection plane\u003C/a\u003E cuts at a time; export the view as an image\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShowing soft edges dashed, or softening while you drag a slider\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/soften\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/soften\"\u003ESoften by angle\u003C/a\u003E in Entity info, and undo to try another angle\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAn online library of models to search, or components with options to pick (colour, style)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/starter\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/starter\"\u003Estarter library\u003C/a\u003E; import Collada, OBJ or glTF models; \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/stretch\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/stretch\"\u003Estretch\u003C/a\u003E a copy to size\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIndependent copies of a group\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMake it a component, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMake unique\u003C/span\u003E (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/troubleshooting#editing-one-group-changed-another\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/troubleshooting\" data-hash=\"editing-one-group-changed-another\"\u003ETroubleshooting\u003C/a\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"materials-and-colors\"\u003EMaterials and colors\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETextures, image materials and transparency\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/paint\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/paint\"\u003EPaint with solid colors\u003C/a\u003E; colors in imported Collada and glTF files are kept and exported again\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETextures in glTF and Collada\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThey are counted and left out on import\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"files\"\u003EFiles\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpening or saving SketchUp files\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExport Collada (\u003Ccode\u003E.dae\u003C/code\u003E) or OBJ from SketchUp, and import that\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDimensions and guides in DWG and DXF exports\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAdd them in the CAD program after exporting\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPoints, tables, multileaders, regions and 3D solids from DWG and DXF\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThey are counted and left out; text comes in as single-stroke lettering, not the drawing&#039;s font\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E3D solids, surfaces, regions, meshes, tables and multileaders from DWG and DXF\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThey are counted and left out too. Export the 3D parts from the CAD program as OBJ or Collada and import that\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIFC types, properties or materials\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe IFC export has \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/formats#ifc-ifc\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/formats\" data-hash=\"ifc-ifc\"\u003Estoreys\u003C/a\u003E and generic elements; add the rest in a BIM program\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESending a model straight to BimCraft\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExport \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIFC for BimCraft (.ifc)\u003C/span\u003E and open it in BimCraft\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELoose edges in glTF and STL\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUse OBJ or Collada for line work\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EglTF extensions, sparse data, separate \u003Ccode\u003E.bin\u003C/code\u003E files\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExport a single \u003Ccode\u003E.glb\u003C/code\u003E from the other program\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECollada skins, morphs, cameras, lights, look-at and skew transforms, external references\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELeft out on import and counted in the message\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESave As with a name, backup files\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERename the downloaded file. After a crash, ModelCraft offers your unsaved work back (&quot;Recover your work?&quot;)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECloud saving and sync between computers\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENot available yet. Save files to your computer, and copy them where you need them\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"performance\"\u003EPerformance\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUndo keeps a full copy of the model for each step, so very large imports make every edit slower. Keep imports lean, and save and reopen to clear the history.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"apps-and-platforms\"\u003EApps and platforms\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDesktop builds\u003C/strong\u003E of ModelCraft are not available yet. It runs in the browser on Mac, Windows and Linux.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETouch:\u003C/strong\u003E see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/orbit-pan-zoom\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/orbit-pan-zoom\"\u003EOrbit, pan and zoom\u003C/a\u003E for the finger gestures. Pressure and pen tilt are not used.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESheets:\u003C/strong\u003E ModelCraft \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/modelcraft/export-model#print-the-view-or-save-it-as-a-pdf\" data-topic=\"modelcraft/export-model\" data-hash=\"print-the-view-or-save-it-as-a-pdf\"\u003Eprints one view per page\u003C/a\u003E; there are no sheet layouts with several views or a title block. Export to CadCraft-friendly DWG/DXF for drawings to scale.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"ModelCraft is an early build. This page lists what it does not do yet, so you don't go looking for it, and the nearest way to get the job done today. Drawing tools Not yet Instead, today Freehand drawing Click the points with Curve Changing an arc's or circle's number of segments after drawing it Erase it and draw it again after typing 24s Choosing a typeface for 3D text, or editing its words after placing it Delete it and add the text again Changing an angle by typing into an angle dimension, or a cylinder's size from its circle's dimension Rotate the edges with Rotate; scale the cylinder with Scale Dimensions in exported model files Dimensions show in images, prints and PDFs of the view; model files carry geometry only Trim, Split and Outer shell Union, Subtract and Intersect closed groups, or intersect faces and erase what you do not need Choosing the rotation axis with the arrow keys Click the Rotate center on a face that faces the way the axis should point Locks on more than one inference at a time Lock one direction with an arrow key, then snap to a point Resizing along a slant, or from a dimension end that is not on a corner Place the dimension on two corners, along an edge or along red, green or blue Organizing and viewing Not yet Instead, today Layers of DWG and DXF site plans as tags The site plan comes in as one group: explode it and group the parts you want to tag Several section cuts at once, a section box, or a section's outline exported as a drawing One section plane cuts at a time; export the view as an image Showing soft edges dashed, or softening while you drag a slider Soften by angle in Entity info, and undo to try another angle An online library of models to search, or components with options to pick (colour, style) The starter library; import Collada, OBJ or glTF models; stretch a copy to size Independent copies of a group Make it a component, then Make unique (see Troubleshooting) Materials and colors Not yet Instead, today Textures, image materials and transparency Paint with solid colors; colors in imported Collada and glTF files are kept and exported again Textures in glTF and Collada They are counted and left out on import Files Not yet Instead, today Opening or saving SketchUp files Export Collada (.dae) or OBJ from SketchUp, and import that Dimensions and guides in DWG and DXF exports Add them in the CAD program after exporting Points, tables, multileaders, regions and 3D solids from DWG and DXF They are counted and left out; text comes in as single-stroke lettering, not the drawing's font 3D solids, surfaces, regions, meshes, tables and multileaders from DWG and DXF They are counted and left out too. Export the 3D parts from the CAD program as OBJ or Collada and import that IFC types, properties or materials The IFC export has storeys and generic elements; add the rest in a BIM program Sending a model straight to BimCraft Export IFC for BimCraft (.ifc) and open it in BimCraft Loose edges in glTF and STL Use OBJ or Collada for line work glTF extensions, sparse data, separate .bin files Export a single .glb from the other program Collada skins, morphs, cameras, lights, look-at and skew transforms, external references Left out on import and counted in the message Save As with a name, backup files Rename the downloaded file. After a crash, ModelCraft offers your unsaved work back (\"Recover your work?\") Cloud saving and sync between computers Not available yet. Save files to your computer, and copy them where you need them Performance Undo keeps a full copy of the model for each step, so very large imports make every edit slower. Keep imports lean, and save and reopen to clear the history. Apps and platforms Desktop builds of ModelCraft are not available yet. It runs in the browser on Mac, Windows and Linux. Touch: see Orbit, pan and zoom for the finger gestures. Pressure and pen tilt are not used. Sheets: ModelCraft prints one view per page; there are no sheet layouts with several views or a title block. Export to CadCraft-friendly DWG/DXF for drawings to scale."},{"slug":"help","scope":"basics","title":"Using this help","summary":"Open the help, search it, and see the steps for your computer.","section":"start","order":10,"keywords":["help","f1","search","tutorials","manual"],"headings":[{"id":"open-the-help","text":"Open the help","level":2},{"id":"find-a-topic","text":"Find a topic","level":2},{"id":"steps-for-your-computer","text":"Steps for your computer","level":2},{"id":"the-same-help-on-the-website","text":"The same help on the website","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EEvery app carries its own help. It is part of the app, so it opens with no connection and always describes the version you are running.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"open-the-help\"\u003EOpen the help\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHelp\u003C/span\u003E in the bar at the top of the app.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClose\u003C/span\u003E to put it away. Your drawing stays as it was.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:mac\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMac\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn most Mac keyboards the top row controls brightness and sound. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E together with the \u003Cstrong\u003Efn\u003C/strong\u003E key (or the globe key), or use the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHelp\u003C/span\u003E button. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/keys#function-keys-on-a-mac\" data-topic=\"basics/keys\" data-hash=\"function-keys-on-a-mac\"\u003EKeys on a Mac\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"find-a-topic\"\u003EFind a topic\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType in the search box at the top of the help. Results update as you type and include other programs&#039; command names, so typing what you would have typed elsewhere usually finds the right page.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETopics are grouped the same way in every app: getting started, walkthroughs, coming from other software, reference, and troubleshooting.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELinks inside a topic open the next topic in the same panel. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBack\u003C/span\u003E returns to where you were.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps-for-your-computer\"\u003ESteps for your computer\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESome steps differ between Mac, Windows and Linux, between browsers, and between being online and offline. The help works out which applies to you and shows those steps. The line at the top of a topic says what it chose, for example &quot;Showing steps for Mac, Chrome or Edge, online&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChange\u003C/span\u003E on that line to pick another computer or browser, for example to help a colleague.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow all\u003C/span\u003E to see every version of every step, each labeled.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EKeyboard shortcuts are written the way your keyboard labels them: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E reads as ⌘S on a Mac and Ctrl+S on Windows and Linux.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-same-help-on-the-website\"\u003EThe same help on the website\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe website shows the same topics at getcadcraft.com/tutorials, with every platform&#039;s steps. In the app, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen on the website\u003C/span\u003E at the bottom of a topic opens that page when you are online.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Every app carries its own help. It is part of the app, so it opens with no connection and always describes the version you are running. Open the help Press F1, or choose Help in the bar at the top of the app. Press Esc or choose Close to put it away. Your drawing stays as it was. On most Mac keyboards the top row controls brightness and sound. Press F1 together with the fn key (or the globe key), or use the Help button. See Keys on a Mac. Find a topic Type in the search box at the top of the help. Results update as you type and include other programs' command names, so typing what you would have typed elsewhere usually finds the right page. Topics are grouped the same way in every app: getting started, walkthroughs, coming from other software, reference, and troubleshooting. Links inside a topic open the next topic in the same panel. Back returns to where you were. Steps for your computer Some steps differ between Mac, Windows and Linux, between browsers, and between being online and offline. The help works out which applies to you and shows those steps. The line at the top of a topic says what it chose, for example \"Showing steps for Mac, Chrome or Edge, online\". Choose Change on that line to pick another computer or browser, for example to help a colleague. Choose Show all to see every version of every step, each labeled. Keyboard shortcuts are written the way your keyboard labels them: Ctrl+S reads as ⌘S on a Mac and Ctrl+S on Windows and Linux. The same help on the website The website shows the same topics at getcadcraft.com/tutorials, with every platform's steps. In the app, Open on the website at the bottom of a topic opens that page when you are online."},{"slug":"browsers","scope":"basics","title":"Browsers and computers","summary":"Which browsers the apps run in, what differs between them, and what your computer needs.","section":"start","order":20,"keywords":["chrome","edge","firefox","safari","brave","browser","mac","windows","linux","ipad","tablet","webgl","requirements","memory"],"headings":[{"id":"which-browser","text":"Which browser","level":2},{"id":"what-differs-between-browsers","text":"What differs between browsers","level":2},{"id":"mac-windows-and-linux","text":"Mac, Windows and Linux","level":2},{"id":"tablets-and-touch-screens","text":"Tablets and touch screens","level":2},{"id":"what-your-computer-needs","text":"What your computer needs","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe apps run inside a web browser. They do the work on your own computer: your files are opened there and never sent to us (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/your-files\" data-topic=\"basics/your-files\"\u003EWhere your files go\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"which-browser\"\u003EWhich browser\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUse an up-to-date version of one of these:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EBrowser\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EEngine\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENotes\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChrome, Edge, Brave, Opera, Vivaldi\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChromium\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe most complete: folder picking, saving for offline use, file handling.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFirefox\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGecko\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWorks. Opening a whole folder uses a simpler picker. Offline saving may not work in private windows.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESafari\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWebKit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWorks on Mac and iPad. Opening a whole folder uses a simpler picker. Safari may delete an offline copy you have not used for a few weeks.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAll three engines are fine for everyday work. Where an app does something differently in one of them, its help shows the steps for your browser.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-differs-between-browsers\"\u003EWhat differs between browsers\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOpening a folder.\u003C/strong\u003E Chromium browsers show a real folder picker and can read the folder directly. Firefox and Safari ask you to choose a folder and then read every file in it, which takes longer for big project folders; dragging the folder onto the app works in all three.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESaving.\u003C/strong\u003E The apps save by downloading a file. Your browser decides where downloads go and whether it asks first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:chromium\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EChrome or Edge\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn Chrome or Edge, turn on &quot;Ask where to save each file before downloading&quot; (in Settings, under Downloads) to choose a folder every time.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:firefox\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EFirefox\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn Firefox, open Settings, then General, then Downloads, and choose &quot;Always ask you where to save files&quot; to pick a folder every time.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:safari\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ESafari\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn Safari, open Settings, then General, and set &quot;File download location&quot; to &quot;Ask for each download&quot; to pick a folder every time.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mac-windows-and-linux\"\u003EMac, Windows and Linux\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe apps are the same on every computer. What changes is the keyboard: shortcuts are written for your computer automatically. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/keys\" data-topic=\"basics/keys\"\u003EKeys, mice and trackpads\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"tablets-and-touch-screens\"\u003ETablets and touch screens\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESome apps accept touch (one finger draws or selects, two fingers pinch to zoom and pan). Each app&#039;s help says whether it does. On an iPad, use Safari or Chrome. A keyboard helps with apps that use typed commands.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-your-computer-needs\"\u003EWhat your computer needs\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThese are planned minimums for the apps, not measured requirements:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E8 GB of memory (16 GB is recommended for large federated models).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA graphics chip with WebGL 2 for the 3D apps. If a 3D view stays blank, check that hardware acceleration is on in your browser&#039;s settings.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA screen at least 1280 pixels wide for the full toolbars; smaller screens wrap them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EVery large files (big plan sets, big models) use a lot of memory in a browser tab. Close other heavy tabs if an app slows down.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The apps run inside a web browser. They do the work on your own computer: your files are opened there and never sent to us (see Where your files go). Which browser Use an up-to-date version of one of these: Browser Engine Notes Chrome, Edge, Brave, Opera, Vivaldi Chromium The most complete: folder picking, saving for offline use, file handling. Firefox Gecko Works. Opening a whole folder uses a simpler picker. Offline saving may not work in private windows. Safari WebKit Works on Mac and iPad. Opening a whole folder uses a simpler picker. Safari may delete an offline copy you have not used for a few weeks. All three engines are fine for everyday work. Where an app does something differently in one of them, its help shows the steps for your browser. What differs between browsers Opening a folder. Chromium browsers show a real folder picker and can read the folder directly. Firefox and Safari ask you to choose a folder and then read every file in it, which takes longer for big project folders; dragging the folder onto the app works in all three. Saving. The apps save by downloading a file. Your browser decides where downloads go and whether it asks first. In Chrome or Edge, turn on \"Ask where to save each file before downloading\" (in Settings, under Downloads) to choose a folder every time. In Firefox, open Settings, then General, then Downloads, and choose \"Always ask you where to save files\" to pick a folder every time. In Safari, open Settings, then General, and set \"File download location\" to \"Ask for each download\" to pick a folder every time. Mac, Windows and Linux The apps are the same on every computer. What changes is the keyboard: shortcuts are written for your computer automatically. See Keys, mice and trackpads. Tablets and touch screens Some apps accept touch (one finger draws or selects, two fingers pinch to zoom and pan). Each app's help says whether it does. On an iPad, use Safari or Chrome. A keyboard helps with apps that use typed commands. What your computer needs These are planned minimums for the apps, not measured requirements: 8 GB of memory (16 GB is recommended for large federated models). A graphics chip with WebGL 2 for the 3D apps. If a 3D view stays blank, check that hardware acceleration is on in your browser's settings. A screen at least 1280 pixels wide for the full toolbars; smaller screens wrap them. Very large files (big plan sets, big models) use a lot of memory in a browser tab. Close other heavy tabs if an app slows down."},{"slug":"keys","scope":"basics","title":"Keys, mice and trackpads","summary":"How shortcuts are written, function keys on a Mac, right-clicking and panning without a mouse.","section":"start","order":30,"keywords":["keyboard","shortcut","ctrl","command","cmd","option","fn","function keys","trackpad","right click","middle button","magic mouse"],"headings":[{"id":"how-shortcuts-are-written","text":"How shortcuts are written","level":2},{"id":"function-keys-on-a-mac","text":"Function keys on a Mac","level":2},{"id":"shortcuts-the-browser-keeps","text":"Shortcuts the browser keeps","level":2},{"id":"mouse-buttons-on-a-trackpad","text":"Mouse buttons on a trackpad","level":2},{"id":"typing-in-a-text-box","text":"Typing in a text box","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"how-shortcuts-are-written\"\u003EHow shortcuts are written\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe help writes each shortcut the way your keyboard labels it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWritten\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EOn a Mac\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EOn Windows and Linux\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECommand (⌘) and S\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECtrl and S\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EControl (⌃), Shift (⇧) and F\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECtrl, Shift and F\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option ←\"\u003E⌥←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt+←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOption (⌥) and ←\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAlt and ←\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ethe Delete key\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBackspace\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Efn and Delete (forward delete)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDelete\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EReturn\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEnter\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA plus sign means hold the keys together. &quot;Then&quot; means press one after the other.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMost app shortcuts accept either Command or Control on a Mac, so \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E works both ways. A few, such as full screen (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), use the Control key on every computer; the help says so where that applies.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"function-keys-on-a-mac\"\u003EFunction keys on a Mac\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn most Mac keyboards the top row changes brightness, volume and media. To send F1 to F12:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ehold \u003Cstrong\u003Efn\u003C/strong\u003E (or the globe key) while you press the key, or\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eopen \u003Cstrong\u003ESystem Settings\u003C/strong\u003E, then \u003Cstrong\u003EKeyboard\u003C/strong\u003E, then \u003Cstrong\u003EKeyboard Shortcuts\u003C/strong\u003E, then \u003Cstrong\u003EFunction Keys\u003C/strong\u003E, and turn on &quot;Use F1, F2, etc. keys as standard function keys&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESome F-keys are also claimed by macOS itself (for example Mission Control). If a key does nothing in the app, try it with fn, or use the toolbar button instead.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"shortcuts-the-browser-keeps\"\u003EShortcuts the browser keeps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA web page cannot take some shortcuts from the browser, whatever it does. In every app:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command N\"\u003E⌘N\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+N\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command T\"\u003E⌘T\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+T\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command W\"\u003E⌘W\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+W\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E always open or close browser windows and tabs. The apps use buttons for New and Close instead.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Q\"\u003E⌘Q\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Q\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E on a Mac and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option F4\"\u003E⌥ F4\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt+F4\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E on Windows quit the browser. If you have unsaved work, the browser asks first (&quot;Leave site?&quot;); choose to stay, then save.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe browser&#039;s own full screen (\u003Cspan class=\"hp\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:mac\" title=\"Mac\"\u003EControl-Command-F, or the green button on the window\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hp-sep\"\u003E / \u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:windows,linux\" title=\"Windows or Linux\"\u003EF11\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/span\u003E) still works. The apps also have their own screen modes on \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, which can hide the app&#039;s bars too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mouse-buttons-on-a-trackpad\"\u003EMouse buttons on a trackpad\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe apps use the right button and the middle button. Without a three-button mouse:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:mac\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMac\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERight-click:\u003C/strong\u003E click with two fingers. Turn on &quot;Secondary click&quot; in \u003Cstrong\u003ESystem Settings\u003C/strong\u003E, then \u003Cstrong\u003ETrackpad\u003C/strong\u003E, if two-finger click does nothing. Holding \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while you click opens the browser&#039;s menu, but inside a drawing some apps read it as an ordinary click (with Control held), so prefer the two-finger click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMiddle-button drag (pan):\u003C/strong\u003E each app offers another way to pan, such as holding \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while you drag or dragging with the right button. The app&#039;s keyboard page says which.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWheel zoom:\u003C/strong\u003E two-finger scroll on the trackpad zooms in most apps. Pinch also zooms where the app supports it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:windows,linux\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EWindows or Linux\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERight-click:\u003C/strong\u003E click with two fingers, or click the lower-right corner of the touchpad. This depends on your touchpad settings.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMiddle-button drag (pan):\u003C/strong\u003E each app offers another way to pan, such as holding \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while you drag or dragging with the right button. The app&#039;s keyboard page says which.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWheel zoom:\u003C/strong\u003E two-finger scroll on the touchpad zooms in most apps.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"typing-in-a-text-box\"\u003ETyping in a text box\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhile you type in a box (a name, a note, a search), the app&#039;s one-letter shortcuts are off, so typing &quot;L&quot; types an L. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or click on the drawing to give the keys back to the app.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"How shortcuts are written The help writes each shortcut the way your keyboard labels it. Written On a Mac On Windows and Linux Ctrl+S Command (⌘) and S Ctrl and S Ctrl+Shift+F Control (⌃), Shift (⇧) and F Ctrl, Shift and F Alt+← Option (⌥) and ← Alt and ← Backspace the Delete key Backspace Delete fn and Delete (forward delete) Delete Enter Return Enter A plus sign means hold the keys together. \"Then\" means press one after the other. Most app shortcuts accept either Command or Control on a Mac, so Ctrl+Z works both ways. A few, such as full screen (Ctrl+Shift+F), use the Control key on every computer; the help says so where that applies. Function keys on a Mac On most Mac keyboards the top row changes brightness, volume and media. To send F1 to F12: hold fn (or the globe key) while you press the key, or open System Settings, then Keyboard, then Keyboard Shortcuts, then Function Keys, and turn on \"Use F1, F2, etc. keys as standard function keys\". Some F-keys are also claimed by macOS itself (for example Mission Control). If a key does nothing in the app, try it with fn, or use the toolbar button instead. Shortcuts the browser keeps A web page cannot take some shortcuts from the browser, whatever it does. In every app: Ctrl+N, Ctrl+T and Ctrl+W always open or close browser windows and tabs. The apps use buttons for New and Close instead. Ctrl+Q on a Mac and Alt+F4 on Windows quit the browser. If you have unsaved work, the browser asks first (\"Leave site?\"); choose to stay, then save. The browser's own full screen (Control-Command-F, or the green button on the window / F11) still works. The apps also have their own screen modes on Ctrl+Shift+F, which can hide the app's bars too. Mouse buttons on a trackpad The apps use the right button and the middle button. Without a three-button mouse: Right-click: click with two fingers. Turn on \"Secondary click\" in System Settings, then Trackpad, if two-finger click does nothing. Holding Ctrl while you click opens the browser's menu, but inside a drawing some apps read it as an ordinary click (with Control held), so prefer the two-finger click. Middle-button drag (pan): each app offers another way to pan, such as holding Space while you drag or dragging with the right button. The app's keyboard page says which. Wheel zoom: two-finger scroll on the trackpad zooms in most apps. Pinch also zooms where the app supports it. Right-click: click with two fingers, or click the lower-right corner of the touchpad. This depends on your touchpad settings. Middle-button drag (pan): each app offers another way to pan, such as holding Space while you drag or dragging with the right button. The app's keyboard page says which. Wheel zoom: two-finger scroll on the touchpad zooms in most apps. Typing in a text box While you type in a box (a name, a note, a search), the app's one-letter shortcuts are off, so typing \"L\" types an L. Press Esc or click on the drawing to give the keys back to the app."},{"slug":"find-tools","scope":"basics","title":"Find a tool or setting","summary":"Press Ctrl+K and type a few letters to run any tool, change a setting or open a help topic.","section":"start","order":32,"keywords":["find","search","command palette","quick open","run command","ctrl k","cmd k","tool","setting","shortcut"],"headings":[{"id":"find-and-run","text":"Find and run","level":2},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EEvery app has a search box for its tools, its settings and this help. You don&#039;t need to know where a tool sits in the toolbar.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"find-and-run\"\u003EFind and run\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command K\"\u003E⌘K\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+K\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFind\u003C/span\u003E in the bar at the top.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType a few letters of what you want: &quot;fillet&quot;, &quot;panel text&quot;, &quot;units&quot;. Every word you type has to match, so add a second word to narrow the list.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to run the first match, or move with the arrow keys first. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E closes the box.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA tool runs as if you had clicked it in the toolbar. A setting opens \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E at that setting. A help topic opens here, in the help panel.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhat you used last comes first the next time, so a tool you use often is one key press away.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETools that can&#039;t run right now, for example before a file is open, are listed but greyed out.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd-ons&#039; tools are in the list too, once the add-on is switched on.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Every app has a search box for its tools, its settings and this help. You don't need to know where a tool sits in the toolbar. Find and run Press Ctrl+K, or choose Find in the bar at the top. Type a few letters of what you want: \"fillet\", \"panel text\", \"units\". Every word you type has to match, so add a second word to narrow the list. Press Enter to run the first match, or move with the arrow keys first. Esc closes the box. A tool runs as if you had clicked it in the toolbar. A setting opens Settings at that setting. A help topic opens here, in the help panel. Things to know What you used last comes first the next time, so a tool you use often is one key press away. Tools that can't run right now, for example before a file is open, are listed but greyed out. Add-ons' tools are in the list too, once the add-on is switched on."},{"slug":"arrange-panels","scope":"basics","title":"Arrange the panels and use a second monitor","summary":"Dock the side panels left, right or at the bottom, float them over the drawing, or move them to their own window on another screen.","section":"start","order":35,"keywords":["panels","dock","float","move panel","drag","layout","second monitor","two screens","dual monitor","multiple monitors","window","pop-up","reset layout"],"headings":[{"id":"move-a-panel-in-the-window","text":"Move a panel in the window","level":2},{"id":"use-the-keyboard","text":"Use the keyboard","level":2},{"id":"put-the-panels-on-a-second-monitor","text":"Put the panels on a second monitor","level":2},{"id":"more-than-one-panels-window","text":"More than one panels window","level":2},{"id":"put-the-panels-back","text":"Put the panels back","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe panels beside the drawing can move. Put them where they suit you, or give them a window of their own on a second monitor so the drawing gets the whole of the first one.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis works in the apps whose panels have a grip: a slim bar with the panel&#039;s name and a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E button at the top of each panel. The other apps are getting it next.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"move-a-panel-in-the-window\"\u003EMove a panel in the window\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag the panel by its name at the top.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhile you drag, outlined areas appear on the left, the right and the bottom of the drawing. Drop the panel on one to dock it there. The area you are over darkens.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrop it anywhere else over the drawing to make it float.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBoth panels can share one side: they stack, and the line between them drags to share the space. Drag the inner edge of a docked side to make it wider or narrower; double-click the edge to put it back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA floating panel stays over the drawing. Move it by its name, and resize it by dragging its lower-right corner (or tab to the corner and use the arrow keys). Double-click its name to dock it again where it was.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:touch\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ETouch\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDrag with one finger on the panel&#039;s name. Drag the lower-right corner of a floating panel with one finger to resize it, and the edges between docked panels the same way. To float or dock with no dragging, use the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E menu.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"use-the-keyboard\"\u003EUse the keyboard\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery panel&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E button opens a menu: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDock on the left\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDock on the right\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDock at the bottom\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFloat over the drawing\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove to a new window\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPut it back\u003C/span\u003E. Tab to the button, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, move with the arrow keys and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E again. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E closes the menu.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"put-the-panels-on-a-second-monitor\"\u003EPut the panels on a second monitor\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E on a panel, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove all panels to a new window\u003C/span\u003E. You can also choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove panels to a new window\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA small window opens with the panels in it. Drag it to your other monitor and make it as big as you like.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EKeep working: the panels act on the drawing as before, and the drawing fills the main window.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EShortcuts keep working while the panels window is in front: keys you press there, other than typing in a box, go to the drawing. Questions and dialogs the app asks still open in the main window, so look there when one appears. The theme and the panel text size follow the main window.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:chromium\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EChrome or Edge\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChrome and Edge can place the window on the other screen for you. With a second screen connected, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPut it on my other screen\u003C/span\u003E in the panels window (or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPut the panels on my other screen\u003C/span\u003E in a panel&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E menu). The browser asks once whether this site may see your screens; after you allow it, the panels window opens on that screen every time.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:firefox,safari\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EFirefox or Safari\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDrag the panels window to the other monitor yourself. It opens in the same place and at the same size next time.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"more-than-one-panels-window\"\u003EMore than one panels window\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach panel can have a window of its own: up to four panels windows, for example one per monitor.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn a panel&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E menu, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove to a new window\u003C/span\u003E opens another window for that panel, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove to panels window 2\u003C/span\u003E (and so on) moves it into one that is open. The second window is called &quot;panels 2&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag a panel by its name from one window to another: let go over the main window to dock it there (the outlined areas show while you are over it) or to float it, and over another panels window to move it in. Let go anywhere else and it stays where it was.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA panels window that has no panels left closes by itself.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAfter a reload the app offers your panels windows again. Browsers open one window per click, so choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen the next one\u003C/span\u003E once for each window.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"put-the-panels-back\"\u003EPut the panels back\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPut the panels back\u003C/span\u003E in the panels window, or simply close it. The panels go back where they were.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClosing or reloading the main window closes the panels window too. When the app opens again it says your panels were in their own window; choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen it\u003C/span\u003E to bring the window back, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKeep them here\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset layout\u003C/span\u003E puts every panel back where it started.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf nothing opens when you move panels to a new window, the browser blocked the pop-up. The app says so and keeps the panels where they were. Allow pop-ups for getcadcraft.com, then try again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe layout is kept for each app in this browser, along with your other settings.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The panels beside the drawing can move. Put them where they suit you, or give them a window of their own on a second monitor so the drawing gets the whole of the first one. This works in the apps whose panels have a grip: a slim bar with the panel's name and a ⋯ button at the top of each panel. The other apps are getting it next. Move a panel in the window Drag the panel by its name at the top. While you drag, outlined areas appear on the left, the right and the bottom of the drawing. Drop the panel on one to dock it there. The area you are over darkens. Drop it anywhere else over the drawing to make it float. Both panels can share one side: they stack, and the line between them drags to share the space. Drag the inner edge of a docked side to make it wider or narrower; double-click the edge to put it back. A floating panel stays over the drawing. Move it by its name, and resize it by dragging its lower-right corner (or tab to the corner and use the arrow keys). Double-click its name to dock it again where it was. Drag with one finger on the panel's name. Drag the lower-right corner of a floating panel with one finger to resize it, and the edges between docked panels the same way. To float or dock with no dragging, use the ⋯ menu. Use the keyboard Every panel's ⋯ button opens a menu: Dock on the left, Dock on the right, Dock at the bottom, Float over the drawing, Move to a new window and Put it back. Tab to the button, press Enter, move with the arrow keys and press Enter again. Esc closes the menu. Put the panels on a second monitor Choose ⋯ on a panel, then Move all panels to a new window. You can also choose Move panels to a new window in Settings. A small window opens with the panels in it. Drag it to your other monitor and make it as big as you like. Keep working: the panels act on the drawing as before, and the drawing fills the main window. Shortcuts keep working while the panels window is in front: keys you press there, other than typing in a box, go to the drawing. Questions and dialogs the app asks still open in the main window, so look there when one appears. The theme and the panel text size follow the main window. Chrome and Edge can place the window on the other screen for you. With a second screen connected, choose Put it on my other screen in the panels window (or Put the panels on my other screen in a panel's ⋯ menu). The browser asks once whether this site may see your screens; after you allow it, the panels window opens on that screen every time. Drag the panels window to the other monitor yourself. It opens in the same place and at the same size next time. More than one panels window Each panel can have a window of its own: up to four panels windows, for example one per monitor. In a panel's ⋯ menu, Move to a new window opens another window for that panel, and Move to panels window 2 (and so on) moves it into one that is open. The second window is called \"panels 2\". Drag a panel by its name from one window to another: let go over the main window to dock it there (the outlined areas show while you are over it) or to float it, and over another panels window to move it in. Let go anywhere else and it stays where it was. A panels window that has no panels left closes by itself. After a reload the app offers your panels windows again. Browsers open one window per click, so choose Open the next one once for each window. Put the panels back Choose Put the panels back in the panels window, or simply close it. The panels go back where they were. Closing or reloading the main window closes the panels window too. When the app opens again it says your panels were in their own window; choose Open it to bring the window back, or Keep them here. Settings › General › Reset layout puts every panel back where it started. If nothing opens when you move panels to a new window, the browser blocked the pop-up. The app says so and keeps the panels where they were. Allow pop-ups for getcadcraft.com, then try again. The layout is kept for each app in this browser, along with your other settings."},{"slug":"offline","scope":"basics","title":"Working offline","summary":"Save an app for offline use, what works with no connection, and what needs one.","section":"start","order":40,"keywords":["offline","no internet","airplane","site visit","cache","service worker","install","download","connection"],"headings":[{"id":"save-an-app-for-offline-use","text":"Save an app for offline use","level":2},{"id":"what-works-offline","text":"What works offline","level":2},{"id":"what-needs-a-connection","text":"What needs a connection","level":2},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe apps do their work on your computer, so most of it works with no connection. The browser only needs to have the app&#039;s files saved first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"save-an-app-for-offline-use\"\u003ESave an app for offline use\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the app while you are online.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E in the bar at the top.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWait for &quot;Offline ready&quot;. The app&#039;s files, its file readers and this help are now saved in your browser. Apps that read DWG or PDF files save their readers too, so this can take a minute the first time.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDo this once per app, in each browser you use. To go back to the app later with no connection, open the same address you used before, or a bookmark of it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"net:offline\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EOffline\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou are offline now. If this app was saved for offline use, everything below works. If it was not, you can still use what has already loaded in this tab, but don&#039;t reload it until you are online again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-works-offline\"\u003EWhat works offline\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpening files from your computer, drawing, editing, measuring and checking.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESaving and exporting: every save is a download to your computer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThis help, including search.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-needs-a-connection\"\u003EWhat needs a connection\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpening the app for the first time, and saving it for offline use.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EGetting a newer version of the app. When you are online, the app picks up the newest version the next time it opens, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E refreshes the saved copy.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELinks to the website, such as \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen on the website\u003C/span\u003E and links to other pages.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECloud saving and sync between computers, which are not available yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA saved copy belongs to one browser on one computer. Clearing your browser&#039;s site data removes it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPrivate windows in some browsers, and some locked-down work computers, do not allow offline saving. The button tells you when it cannot save.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:safari\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ESafari\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESafari removes saved site data from sites you have not visited for several weeks. Open the app now and then while online, or save it again before a trip.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDesktop builds, which will not need a browser, are not available yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"The apps do their work on your computer, so most of it works with no connection. The browser only needs to have the app's files saved first. Save an app for offline use Open the app while you are online. Choose Save for offline in the bar at the top. Wait for \"Offline ready\". The app's files, its file readers and this help are now saved in your browser. Apps that read DWG or PDF files save their readers too, so this can take a minute the first time. Do this once per app, in each browser you use. To go back to the app later with no connection, open the same address you used before, or a bookmark of it. You are offline now. If this app was saved for offline use, everything below works. If it was not, you can still use what has already loaded in this tab, but don't reload it until you are online again. What works offline Opening files from your computer, drawing, editing, measuring and checking. Saving and exporting: every save is a download to your computer. This help, including search. What needs a connection Opening the app for the first time, and saving it for offline use. Getting a newer version of the app. When you are online, the app picks up the newest version the next time it opens, and Save for offline refreshes the saved copy. Links to the website, such as Open on the website and links to other pages. Cloud saving and sync between computers, which are not available yet. Things to know A saved copy belongs to one browser on one computer. Clearing your browser's site data removes it. Private windows in some browsers, and some locked-down work computers, do not allow offline saving. The button tells you when it cannot save. Safari removes saved site data from sites you have not visited for several weeks. Open the app now and then while online, or save it again before a trip. Desktop builds, which will not need a browser, are not available yet."},{"slug":"your-files","scope":"basics","title":"Where your files go","summary":"Files stay on your computer, how saving works, and what the browser remembers.","section":"start","order":50,"keywords":["privacy","upload","cloud","save","download","storage","local","security","confidential"],"headings":[{"id":"your-files-stay-on-your-computer","text":"Your files stay on your computer","level":2},{"id":"how-saving-works","text":"How saving works","level":2},{"id":"what-the-browser-remembers","text":"What the browser remembers","level":2},{"id":"cloud-saving","text":"Cloud saving","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"your-files-stay-on-your-computer\"\u003EYour files stay on your computer\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen you open a drawing, model or PDF, the app reads it inside your browser. \u003Cstrong\u003EThe file is not uploaded.\u003C/strong\u003E We never see it, and it works the same with no connection.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-saving-works\"\u003EHow saving works\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA browser page cannot overwrite files on your computer by itself, so the apps save by \u003Cstrong\u003Edownloading\u003C/strong\u003E a new file:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe download goes where your browser puts downloads, usually your Downloads folder, unless you have set the browser to ask each time (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/browsers#what-differs-between-browsers\" data-topic=\"basics/browsers\" data-hash=\"what-differs-between-browsers\"\u003EBrowsers and computers\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EApps that change a file save a copy with a new name, such as &quot;(edited)&quot; or &quot;(marked up)&quot;, so your original stays untouched.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf you close the tab with unsaved work, the browser asks first (&quot;Leave site?&quot;).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-the-browser-remembers\"\u003EWhat the browser remembers\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESome apps remember small settings in your browser, such as your author name or tool styles. They stay in that browser on that computer, and clearing site data removes them. Each app&#039;s help says what it keeps.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"cloud-saving\"\u003ECloud saving\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECloud saving and sync between computers are part of the commercial license. They are not available yet. Until then, keep your files where you keep your other project files and back them up the usual way.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Your files stay on your computer When you open a drawing, model or PDF, the app reads it inside your browser. The file is not uploaded. We never see it, and it works the same with no connection. How saving works A browser page cannot overwrite files on your computer by itself, so the apps save by downloading a new file: The download goes where your browser puts downloads, usually your Downloads folder, unless you have set the browser to ask each time (see Browsers and computers). Apps that change a file save a copy with a new name, such as \"(edited)\" or \"(marked up)\", so your original stays untouched. If you close the tab with unsaved work, the browser asks first (\"Leave site?\"). What the browser remembers Some apps remember small settings in your browser, such as your author name or tool styles. They stay in that browser on that computer, and clearing site data removes them. Each app's help says what it keeps. Cloud saving Cloud saving and sync between computers are part of the commercial license. They are not available yet. Until then, keep your files where you keep your other project files and back them up the usual way."},{"slug":"recover","scope":"basics","title":"Recover unsaved work","summary":"How the apps keep a recovery copy while you work, and how to get your work back after a crash or a closed tab.","section":"trouble","order":55,"keywords":["autosave","recover","recovery","crash","lost work","closed tab","unsaved","restore","backup"],"headings":[{"id":"when-the-copy-is-kept","text":"When the copy is kept","level":2},{"id":"get-your-work-back","text":"Get your work back","level":2},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EWhile you work, each app keeps a recovery copy of what you have not saved yet. It is not a save: your file on your computer only changes when you save it. The copy is there so a crash, a closed tab or a computer that turns off does not cost you your work.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-the-copy-is-kept\"\u003EWhen the copy is kept\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA few seconds after you stop editing, and at least every 30 seconds while you keep going.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhen you switch to another tab or window.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe copy stays in this browser, on this computer. Nothing is uploaded.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"get-your-work-back\"\u003EGet your work back\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the same app again, in the same browser.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf it closed before your work was saved, it asks \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERecover your work?\u003C/span\u003E and lists what it kept, with the time of the last copy.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERecover\u003C/span\u003E to open it, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDiscard\u003C/span\u003E to throw it away.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESave it to a file as soon as it opens.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENot now\u003C/span\u003E keeps the copies for 30 days, and the app asks again the next time it opens. A reload counts too: reload a tab with unsaved work and the app offers it back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhen you save, or close a file on purpose, its recovery copy is removed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAnother tab of the same app that is still open never offers your copies. It only offers work from a tab that has gone.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPrivate windows in some browsers, and a browser with no space left, cannot keep a copy. The app tells you when that happens, so save to a file more often.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClearing your browser&#039;s site data removes the copies.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENot every app keeps a recovery copy yet. Each app&#039;s own help says whether it does.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"While you work, each app keeps a recovery copy of what you have not saved yet. It is not a save: your file on your computer only changes when you save it. The copy is there so a crash, a closed tab or a computer that turns off does not cost you your work. When the copy is kept A few seconds after you stop editing, and at least every 30 seconds while you keep going. When you switch to another tab or window. The copy stays in this browser, on this computer. Nothing is uploaded. Get your work back Open the same app again, in the same browser. If it closed before your work was saved, it asks Recover your work? and lists what it kept, with the time of the last copy. Choose Recover to open it, or Discard to throw it away. Save it to a file as soon as it opens. Not now keeps the copies for 30 days, and the app asks again the next time it opens. A reload counts too: reload a tab with unsaved work and the app offers it back. Things to know When you save, or close a file on purpose, its recovery copy is removed. Another tab of the same app that is still open never offers your copies. It only offers work from a tab that has gone. Private windows in some browsers, and a browser with no space left, cannot keep a copy. The app tells you when that happens, so save to a file more often. Clearing your browser's site data removes the copies. Not every app keeps a recovery copy yet. Each app's own help says whether it does."}]};
